        <p>,+++.,1.4,4-t-c-‘..+4-4-:-...-:-.,)•:-:.•,:-&gt; •••:•i••:.-:••:••:-.:-.7.--: .:•.:,-:•-•., 4.4. , - 4..2</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S W • RLD.</p>
        <p>44, 4.41. •</p>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>these are quite plainly trimmed. White felt hats are usually trimmed with black velvet, and sometimes a small cluster of fruit is added.</p>
        <p>The One-piece Dress. The one-piece dress in dark colours is to have a vogue this winter, for it is far too useful to be dispensed with. More especially will it be worn under autumn and winter fur coats. Many of the summer styles of this popular gown have been adapted for this season's dresses. The greater number, unfortunately for comfort and convenience, button dawn the back. The rest gown is another garment that is worn very much just now, and very charming it is, although quite simple in design. All sorts of materials are used, but more especially velvet and velveteens made in Yorkshire. For cool autumn evenings this latter fabric is most com- fortable, and it is shown in the most beautiful colours. The little coatee is having a vogue too just now, for it is so useful in smartening up a dress for dinner or tea. Old pieces of lace are used for this confection, and it is often made tabard fashion. Chantilly is a favourite lace for these useful accessories, but any handsome material, such as brocade, gauze, embroidered chiffon, sequined net, &amp;c., may be employed.</p>
        <p>Furs Should be Bought Now. Now is the time to buy furs, for winter prices will soon be asked. Alterations of all sorts should be seen to before the winter work begins. Stoles of whole skins laid on velvet or some other foundation will be as much worn this year as last. Natural musquash will be very modish, and seal coney coats with the new Padre collars are shown in the fur departments of the stores and big shops. Reversible and adaptable coats and capes, some of which may be used in as many as three different ways, are novelties for this winter.</p>
        <p>Frocks for the School Girl. At the end of the holidays, dresses for school girls assume an importance otherwise denied them by designers and dressmakers. Good taste demands that growing girls should be dressed as simply as possible, and utility is the first con- sideration in choosing materials. Thus blue serge has deservedly become almost a uniform</p>
        <p>ketchup, pepper and salt to taste. Cut the smuepaetrfliunotuos sflaitc,espee( nl otth et 000 n otnhsi n) tan, d criumt t hoefrint hi ne slices, melt some of the fat in an iron saucepan, and fry the meat and onion together in this fat until they are a pale brown colour, but do not cook them sufficiently for eating. When this is accomplished pour away as much of the fat as possible, and cover the meat with one pint of stock; add pepper and salt and a small piece of carrot and turnip. Let all simmer for one hour and a half, skimming the fat as it rises to the surface while the meat is cooking. Cut the carrot and turnip into small and neat shapes, and throw each into separate saucepan of boil- ing salt and water, and cook without any cover on the pan until they are fender. When these 'are all cooked have ready a deep dish thoroughly heated. Lay in the pieces of meat; cover and keep them hot while you break the flour with the ketcup, and, if necessary, a little cold water; add it to the stock, and stir until boiling; then pour it over the meat, and garnish the dish with the pieces of carrot and turnip. This dish is much better when prepared the day before it is served, as the fat can so much more easily be removed wheii cold. If any fat be served with the haricot it is apt to be too rich and indi- gestible. To Remove Mildew on Leather. Mildew on leather, and many other stains, may be removed with a little pure vaseline. Rub this into the leather till quite absorbed, and then carefully polish with a clean chamois leather. To Remove Stains from Wood.</p>
        <p>Wood is.more or less porous, and hence the various stains sink in In cleaning wooden- ware, such as wooden spoons, dough boards and butter paddles, soil and stain is best removed by the use of cold water and not hot, because the latter causes the wood to tighten, and the stain is more likely to remain. Some cleanser other than soap is more efficient than soap, because the soda in the soap yellows the wood. Washing soda should be used only in extreme cases, where a sweetening of the wood seems necessary, or where the dirt needs bleaching out.</p>
        <p>eip 04;* 42, in the case of the school girl, and nothing is more appropriate than white or cream serge for afternoon and smart wear, and both can be worn all the year round—a distinct advantage. The all-in-one frock is the most popular for this autumn. It may have a flounced skirt which is becoming to the tall girl. A very pretty model in white serge had three flounces put on in quite a novel way—a broad single pleat alternated with gathers, the width of the latter, and the effect was excellent. The bodice was pleated to match the flounces, and prettily trimmed with a heavy cream lace. Another model in fine navy serge, had a pleated tunic, and the bodice was relie*ed with gaily coloured Russian embroid- ery. For party and afternoon wear, also for dancing classes, no better material can be chosen than a washing silk, Some of these fabrics have tiny patterns of flowers in delicate colours, and when shades of pink or red pregall coral buttons are effectively used. Transparencies are not suitable for girls in their early teens, but muslins of all kinds may take the place of silk, if preferred, more especially where economy need not be studied. •</p>
        <p>Some Fashionable Stones. Semi-precious stones are very much donned just now, and some of them are quite beautiful. Among these may be mentioned the peridots, olivines, and tourmalines. Still cheaper stones are garnets, topazes—which are green and light blue, as well as yellow—and zercons. Opals and amethysts have increased in value very much lately. New stones are discovered (at more or less frequent intervals), of which our grandmothers were quite ignorant, such as kun- zite, benito and calef. The latter stone resem- bles the sapphire in colour, whilst kunzite is a brilliant yellowish brown, something like a cairngorm.</p>
        <p>British Table Waters. There seems to be no phase of life that is not touched by the war. Even the table waters are affected. Very large quantities come from Ger- many, Austria and France, and as these cannot now be obtained, Buxtott; Malvern and ,Bath waters are finding an increased sale. This is as it should be, for British spas are quite as effi- cacious as those of the Continent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>By MARJORLE.</head>
        <p>By MARJORLE.</p>
        <p>The Court in London. This is the first year since the late 'Porties that the Court has not paid its autumn visit to Balmoral. Never since the year 1820, when the trial of Queen Caroline took place, have there been so many members of our Royal Family in London during August and September. Queen Alexandra refuses to leave town, as she, like Queen Mary, is busily employed for the soldiers and sailors and their families. The King's keepers are killing the stags on the Royal estates in Scotland, and the venison is sent to the hos- pitals for the wounded. The Princess Royal has ordered a number of deer in the Mar Forest to be killed, and this venison is also sent to the wounded.</p>
        <p>Protest Against War. American women living in New York made a solemn protest a few days ago against war. Garbed in black, they marched in procession to the beating of muffled drums down Fifth Avenue. Women belonging to the five nations now at war joined in the silent demonstration, and refugees from Belgium and France formed a section by themselves. A squadron of motor cars conveyed those who were too old to march, an. mothers with babies in their arms.</p>
        <p>Autumn Modes. The principal Paris houses of fashion have branches at Biarritz, and these are showing the new models prepared for this season. The alteration in the line came quite at the end of the summer, and beyond this there will be very little change in styles during the coming winter. Designers agree that, the present is not the time to lannch extravagances, consequently new modes are simple and graceful. The sailor and boat shape hats are the most popular, and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>HOME NOTES.</head>
        <p>HOME NOTES.</p>
        <p>Recipes and Useful Suggestions. PRESERVING FRUIT. At the present time there is a large supply of English fruit, of both eating and cooking varie- ties, full use of which should be made. Much of this may be wasted unless proper steps are taken to preserve it against decay. Large .growers should arrange for the storage of their produce in properly constructed fruit sheds or cold storage, or for drying plums, &amp;c., in evapor- ating machines, hop oasts, and by other means. The following methods are suggested for the use of small-holders in a leaflet that may be got free on application to the Board of Agriculture, 4, Whitehall-place, London, S.W. : Apples, especially those of the later varieties, should be carefully stored in cool dry sheds, with a free circulation of air. Bruised and de- cayed fruit should be removed at once. Even with sugar at the present high price a certain quantity of fruit should, of course, be made into jams and jellies. In the case of those who have to purchase their supplies the low price of fruit will compensate for the high price of sugar. If the price of sugar should rise, or if difficulty is experienced in securing sufficient quantities, liquid (syrup) glucose may be par- tially or even entirely substituted for cane sugar. Glucose is made in this country in large quantities from maize and other starchy materials. In making jam first-rate results may be got by replacing from a quarter to a third of the sugar by syrup glucose, and very fair preserves may even be made without any sugar by using n to parts of syrup glucose to 1 part of fruit, ac- cording to its sweetness. If jam made in this way is intended for sale it should be labelled as containing glucose.</p>
        <p>Bottling Fruit. A part of the fruit supply should be bottled. There are several old-fashioned methods which might be adopted where no proper sterilising apparatus can be obtained, be these are all attended with considerable risk of the fruit' being rendered tasteless and unsightly or of decay from exposure to the air. A proper steri- lising apparatus can be obtained for about 10s., while air-tight quart bottles cost about 3s. 6d. to 4s. a dozen. No sugar is required for this pro- cess. Simple directions for use are given with the outfit by makers of sterilising apparatus, and a full description is given in Leaflet No. 250 (Fruit Bottling for Small-holders), which can be obtained on application to the Board. The pro- cess consists in filling the bottles with fruit. adding water to within i-inch of the rim, and putting them in the steriliser, the temperature of which should be raised slowly to about 165 degrees Fahr. to 170 degrees Fahr. for about ten minutes. The bottles are then securely fastened down, being screwed or capped according to the system adopted, and allowed to cool. Apples and pears must be peeled and quartered—a silver or plated knife being used—and dropped into water directly afterwards, or the flesh will be discoloured. Large plums may be cut in half and stoned. The stalks should be removed in every case.</p>
        <p>Haricot Mutton. 'rake two pounds of mutton, one large carrot, two onions, one turnip, one pint of stock, one dessertspoonful of flour, two tablespoonfuls of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>HERE AND THERE.</head>
        <p>HERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>A Needed Lesson. Kind Lady: "Look here! You said that if I'd give you your dinner you'd mow the lawn for me." Lazy Tramp: "I'd like to do it, ma'am, but I got ter teach yer a lesson. Never trust th' word of a total stranger." The Dearest Fellow. Mark Twain, as an example of unconscious humour, used to quote a Hartford woman who said one day in,the late spring: "My husband is the dearest fellow. " I said to him this morning, 'are you very hard up just now?' "'I certainly am hard up,' he replied soberly. 'This high cost of living is terrible. I don't know what I'm going to do.' "'Then, Jim,' said I, 'l'll give up all thought of going to the country for July and August this year.' "But the dear fellow's face changed, and he said: "'lndeed, then, you won't darling. I thought you wanted to buy a hat with an aigret or some such foolishness. No, no, my darling. Jim can always find the money to let his dear little wife go to the country.' "</p>
        <p>horses to make them go quicker and quicker, and ht last they galloped through the gate into the city. Immediately the iron gates com- menced to close. In the meantime the three men had gained a lot of ground, but when they were nearing the gates the Sikh took the lead. He reached the gates first. They were, nearly closed. What could he do? He could not push them open .himself, so he put one of his arms between the gates as,they were rolling together. His arm was crushed, in an instant, but he knew that so long as it was between the gates they could not be closed. As the enemy on the other side were hacking his arm away he motioned to our soldiers to hurry up. They were doing this, for they were rushing like mad- men. Just as the Sikh's arm was being cut off he pushed his other arm through. Our soldiers were drawing nearer and nearer, and they reached the great swinging gates as the gallant Sikh was losing his second arm. He smiled as the bury British soldiers began to push the gates open. At first the men could not Move them, but gradually they began to give way, and at last our soldiers entered the city and hoisted the Union Jack. When the soldiers were pushing at the gates the Sikh, who had lost both his arms, looked on. He was proud that his action was the cause of the gates being opened. As the troops were rushing 'into the city a young officer complimented him. "You have done well," be said, "you ran a good race." That young officer was Frederick Roberts, now Lord Roberts, V.C. This shows how brave the Indian troops are. They will give the Germans a hard fight. s</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>HIGH BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>A HIGH BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>The great Victoria Falls Bridge. over the Zambesi, in Africa, is one of the highest bridges Irt the world. It is 2,875 feet above the sea leyel, and is 650 feet in length.</p>
        <p>DIFFERENT ALPHABETS. Nearly .200 different alphabets are known, of. which 50 are in use. The alphabets of the folloring nations have the number of letters men f oned below: English 96, French 25, Italian 22, Spanish 27, German 26, Slavonic 42, Russian 35, Latin 22, Greek 24, Hebrew 94, Arabic 28, Persian 32, Turkish 28, Sanscrit 44. Chinese radical characters 214, Chinese alphabet (said to have been invented by Bishop Eligius Casi, of Canton, in 1880 33 letters. The city of Canton, by the way, is of great antiquity. It was founded about 200 n.c., and was the only city with which Europeans were allowed to trade till the treaty et A:ugust 290, 184,1, _ Ilr</p>
        <p>Milo</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH. Useful Information in Popular</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH. Useful Information in Popular</p>
        <p>Language.</p>
        <p>Failing Hair. Betweea the ages of thirty and forty many women begin to find they lose their hair a good deal when brushing or combing it, and if they could but have a good look at their own scalp they would probably find—especially if looked at through a microscope—that the scalp. was red and spongy looking. If this is so, then a tonic is necessary, and although I cannot say the one I mention is infallible, it certainly does good, and should be appli4 night and morning:— Quarter pint of a strong decoction of Peruvian bark, half /glassful brandy (wineglass), half tablespoonful glycerine. Good brushing of the hair night and morning to encourage the growth of new roots and strengthen the hair generally, is imperative. There are very many other remedies and recipes, says a writer in "Farm and Home," which I could mention, but I think the few given will be found of help in these little worries and at any time I am always glad to reply to letters. Within a few weeks I hope to write a little article on how to prevent and remedy wrinkles. One of the richest inheritances woman has received from Nature is the glory of beautiful hair. Beautiful hair may be an inheritance, but it can also be the result of careful and system- atic care, as constant " grooming " has an ex- cellent effect and helps to promote growth. There are women with lovely long hair who say they have never used a brush and who "never wash their heads." I have never met them, but one frequently hears about them. The only reason their hair is thick is because Dame Nature has been kind, but they seldom retain really good, thick hair after middle age, although, of course, there are a few exceptions. The hair should be brushed carefully every night. Some beauty specialists say ten minutes, but few women, after a tiring day, will care to spend ten minutes in brushing, so I suggest merely a "good brush"; then take out the tangles, and plait loosely for the night. If you always get a cold after shampooing the head, add a little—but only a little—methylated spirit to the rinsing water, and you will find the hair dry more quickly, and, in consequence, there is less likelihood of catching cold.</p>
        <p>Dangerous Food. Any food is liable to become decomposed, or contaminated, by the microbes of putrefaction, particularly meat, poultry, fish, and especially foods composed of mixtures, such as brawn, sausages, and pork pies. They may have been kept too long, or else have been placed close to something that is already decaying. Sometimes the evidence of the poison being present in the food is so very slight that people partake of it before they are aware of their danger. It is just as well to be on the safe side, and never buy any prepared food to be eaten unless it is known to have been freshly made. Canned foods are sometimes, blamed for cases of poisoning. They must never be eaten if thke is the least sign of fermentation, or if the flit bulges, and a rush of air when the tin is pierced should put every- one on guard. Stale fish are very dangerous to health, and stale shellfish often cause unpleasant results, if they do not produce a more serious form of poisoning.</p>
        <p>PUSS IN PARIS. (By JESSIE Sinn.) In "gay Paree," for fashion famed, We thought to have some fun, So strolled about in Paris hats,</p>
        <p>To let all know we'd come. We washed our faces, brushed our hair,</p>
        <p>And then, to match our coats, Our tails we carefully tied round 'Our dainty little throats. But, sad to say, our little plans— And tempers—were upset, For down came rain with might and main, And we got very wet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>UNITS OF THE ARMY.</head>
        <p>UNITS OF THE ARMY.</p>
        <p>Explanation of Military Terms.</p>
        <p>An Infantry Brigade in our Army consists of four battalions each 1,000 strong. In most for- eign armies it consists of two regiments each of six battalions. This infantry brigade has its medical and supply service, besides machine guns; but it has no field artillery. A Cavalry Brigade in our Army consists of three regiments each of three field squadrons of approximately 150 men. Abroad it consists usually of two regiments each of five or six squadrons. With each cavalry brigade there are machine guns and a signal troop. An Artillery Brigade consists of three bat- teries, howitzers or field guns, each battery of six guns, with about 200 men. In France each battery has four guns. An Infantry Division is the smallest tactical unit which possesses all arms. It is normally of 12 battalions and has with it from 36 to 72 guns, besides field companies of engineers, medical, supply, signal and transport services. The cavalry with the division is usually two squadrons, but in some cases more. The strength of a division is approximately 20,000 men. The Cavalry Division consists of two to four brigades of cavalry and one to four batteries of horse artillery, besides mounted engineers and auxiliary services. The normal foreign cavalry division has 4,500 men, and a combatant strength of 3,500 sabres, 12 guns, and eight machine guns. An Army Corps consists of • two or three divi- sions, usually two -active and one of reserve. The German Army Corps of two divisions has 41.000 men and a combatant strength of 26,900 rips, 48 machine guns, 1,200 sabres, and 144 guns. The German Army Corps of three divi- sions is approximately 60,000 strong. The French and Russian Army Corps are practically the same. .</p>
        <p>The Army consists of two or more Army Corps. Usually three to four Army Corps form an Army._</p>
        <p>The Group of Armies consists of two or moi</p>
        <p>Armies.</p>
      </div>
