        <p>41 CHILDREN'S CORNER. Conducted by UNCLE NZD. 5,').2.X).2.(V•r005(950'ut,0,4250-"1/4)1W'6'04`),;(2</p>
        <p>MY DEAR CHILDREN,— I want you to make every use of this Corner. If in your daily walks and in your reading you come across anything you do not Properly understand, write it down and send it to me. I will be pleased to answer any questions that are sent, week by week. Do not forget that I want to help you all I can. BY sending in questions you will be helping Your fellow-members of this Corner. Perhaps they do not understand the things you v:ant to know. The competition closes to-day and the winner will be announced next Saturday. Although I have received a lot of entries I would like a lot more for the next competition. Are you trying t 9, get 'any of your fliends to send in their names for my big book? Your loving UNCLE; NED.</p>
        <p>A BRAVE SIKH. Now that we are dailY expeciting to bear the beatlasttadwiers have given news that our native Indi of inter. the Germans a thorough is • est to retell a tale of the Indiat7Niit hero of this true story was a sllill,:rsiiisCe:riv:litcjihtielnes, kind of Indian soldier—through ;01' a city was taken from the enemy. For a number of days the British the help of a large number of faithful ilclis had been trying to capture a big citY but t enemy beat off all the attacks. The "ails round the city were thick and very Strong and the gates were very big ones, made af lieu. Our soldiers had tried to shoot down the gates, out could not succeed. It seemed that the only way to take the city was by stopping all food from being taken in. side so that the people would have nothing to eat and would then surrender. This was what our soldiers decided to do, but one day as they were watching the city they saw that the great gates were being slowly opened. They also saw that a waggon with a lot of food on it was dashing towards the city. The food was for the enemy, but as the waggon was being rushed towards the gates the British soldiers also began to run towards them. At the head of our troops, as they made this great dash, were three men—two white men and our hero the Sikh. They were having a great race for the gates the firs honour of reaching the big iron t. of the faad waggon las)</p>
        <p>hed</p>
        <p>1 great our hero race for th, gates the first. Of "NW."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 26, 1914. FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 26, 1914. FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>EMERGENCY CROPPING. Hints on inc easing the yood Supply.</p>
        <p>. - The output of wheat can be increased by- grow- ing wheat after wheat, after roots, or by the breaking up of old grass land. In the first case, in which the consent of the landlord may be required, and will no doubt be readily given, it will -be necessary to manure either with 12 tons of farmyard dung on the stubble, or with 4cwt. to &amp;cwt. of basic slag per acre, -harrowed in at the time of sowing. It is not an uncommon practice to grow wheat after roots, when the land is usually rich in the residue of the man- ures given to the mangels or turnips; but if popular opinion has any influence, the chief addition will result from the ploughing down of old turf. . .</p>
        <p>This is a *proceeding in which the average farmer has little experience, as, for the last 30 years, the tendency has /been in the opposite direction. It is known, however, that the suc- cess of the plan is by no means a certainty. Land that has been under grass for a number of years is often badly infested with wireworm, which makes it difficult to establish a full plant of any cereal. On this account a crop of pota- toes is sometimes taken first, but this plan would not answer the immediate object of the present scheme. It is suggested that the risk might be lessened if a heavy dressing of lime were applied before the old turf is ploughed down. Lime is a useful insecticide, but it would also neutralise the acid that is apt to accumulate in land long under grass, and would thus conduce in a double sense to a satisfactory result. As a rule no manure will be required after grass unless the turf has shown signs of poverty, although in the spring a dressing of lcwt. of nitrate of soda to the acre may be given.—" The Times."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>SUCCESSIVE WHITE CROPS.</head>
        <p>SUCCESSIVE WHITE CROPS.</p>
        <p>Though two successive white crops may be both wise and profitable where high farming is practised, the plan is not advisable on poor or run-down land. Much depends en the means available for restoring fertility in the latter case. The trouble is usually caused by too continuous cropping, with insufficient manuring, or the too frequent use of artificials alone. I remember a case of a farm which had been badly done for a long time, heavily cropped and lightly stocked with sheep or cattle. It was sold and fell into the hands of a butcher from a large city twenty miles away. He could get unlimited supplies of town manure, and with the aid of a traction -engine he plastered this farm with muck, and then-stocked it with sheep and feeding cattle to its utmost limit. That farm was wen doubling its crops of grain, besides producing fine fields of potatoes and a large quA.ntity of beef and mutton.—" Agricultural Economist and Horti- cultural Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>"BUSINESS AS USUAL."</head>
        <p>"BUSINESS AS USUAL."</p>
        <p>It will be a relief to many to hear that tlhe British Dairy Farmers' Association are arrang- ing to hold their show as usual, in spite of the war. It is not only an interesting exhibition, which is becoming more and more popular as a London entertainment, writes a correspondent In "Farm and Home," but it .has proved itself to be a highly educative medium in all that relates to the dairy and to dairy cattle. 'The great movement for the cultivation of improved dairy cattle owes an important proportion of its success to the association, and there is doubtless a future before it for still greater achievements. No one can expect that the attendance of visitors will be quite as -large as usual, but even Lon. 'doners might do worse than spend a few hours amongst the cattle and homely scenes of the London Dairy Show as a temporary relief from war's alarms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>MILK PROSECUTIO_NS.</head>
        <p>MILK PROSECUTIO_NS.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Sadler moved the folloiVing resolution at the last meeting of the British Farmers' Association:—</p>
        <p>"In view of the large number of convictions of farmers and distributors for selling milk de- ficient in butter fat or non-fatty solids, although it has been proved to the satisfaction of the magistrates that no abstraction or addition has taken place, and that therefore the milk in question was the genuine milk of the cows, the time has arrived when combined action should be taken to test the legality of these decisions, especially in view of the decision in the Scotch Court of Appeal in the case of Scott v. Jack, 1912."</p>
        <p>This was seconded by Mr. John Kendrick and carried.</p>
        <p>Mr. Sadler then gave notice that he will move at the general meeting to be held during the dairy show week that concerted action be taken to carry out the terms of the resolution.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>FOR SOWING. OR PLANTING IN OCTOBER AND EARLY NOVEMBER.</p>
        <p>_ T„he following vegetables might be sown or planted in October or the beginning of Novem- ber: 1; broad beans; 2, early peas; 3, all varie- ties of cabbage and kale raised from seed sown in July or August. If cabbage and kale plants are required for distribution on a large scale in October, seed should be sown4„in August at the rate of about 251 b. per acre. Seed beds of 1 acre should furnish about 500,000 plants. These plants will yield crops in the following spring and early summer, but too late to allow of potatoes being successfully planted after them. They could, however, be succeeded by onions, broccoli, Brussels sprouts, kale or car- rots. Growers should remember that it is inad- visable that one crop of the cabbage family should be succeeded by another of the same kind. Beans and peas should be followed by cabbage. and cabbage by onion, carrots, or some kind of crop other than the cabbage tribe.</p>
        <p>DERELICT LAND. In many districts fertile grass land will not be available for cultivation by spade labour, and if additional land is to be brought under tillage poor soil or waste land must be broken up. There are many acres of land of this class available, especially in the neighbourhood uf large towns, brit care is needed in treating it if it is ever to bear a satisfactory crop. The follow- ing treatment is recommended :---</p>
        <p>In the first place all ;:arc 'growth of weeds should be cut down with a scythe. If the weeds are of a soft nature they might be put in heaps with any available grass for making into a coin - post, or ,if annuals and free from ripe seeds, they may be dug in at once. If of a fibrous or woody nature they should be burnt. The land might then be skimmed and bastard 'trenched, but the greatest care must be taken (1) not to bring the fubsoil to the top, and (2) to clear out the roots of such weeds as docks, couch grass, creeping buttercup, convolvulus, nettles, &amp;c. Time spent in the careful eradication of such weeds will be well repaid. Land of this character when freed from weeds and properly trenched will bear a useful crop! the following year if properly manured. As stable or farmyard manure will probably not be available in such places it will be necessary to supply the plant food from other sources. The chief ingredients required to secure satisfactory : growth ate (1) nitrogen, (2) potash, ' (3) Phos- ph ates. Nitrogen can be supplied by digging in all soft vegetable matter such as grass, leaves of , trees, and decaying vegetable matter of any sort. The droppings of any kind of animal may be used. Poultry and pig manure should be well mixed with earth on removal froth the pens or sties before applying it to the land. Potash may be supplied by collecting and burning all kinds of woody material, such as hedge clippings, prunings from trees, Scc. The ash should be carefully saved in bags and kept dry. It is especially useful on land that is to be cropped with potatoes. Seaweed is a valu- able potash manure and should be collected at all rocky sea coasts. Phosphates will not be easy to supply from natural sources, unless large quantities of fish waste are livailable, but phosphatic fertilisers such as basic slag, superphosphate and bone- meal should be easily procurable through the usual trade sources. Lime will be required in many cases and should always be applied to rich pastures after breaking up. It should be worked into the sur- face layer when the land is dug. As to insect pests and weeds, great care should be taken to get rid of both before the loud is ::own or planted with any crop. It would he better to keep such land vacant till the spring, when potatoes might be planted. Free copies of leaflet dealing with these ques- tions may he obtained on application to the Secretary. Board of Agriculture and Fisheries, 4, Whitehall-place, London, S.W. Letters of application so. addressed need not be btenved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>4. 4.1.444.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.44+4.4.4.+4.÷ + 4. * POULTRY PEN $ 4. q. 44 Hints and Interesting Facts and ,I 4 + Chat for Amateurs. wl. 41 •14 5.6144,141.14.101,4444,1444,14,144+4014,1444014 4.</head>
        <p>4. 4.1.444.4.4.4.4.4.4.4.44+4.4.4.+4.÷ + 4. * POULTRY PEN $ 4. q. 44 Hints and Interesting Facts and ,I 4 + Chat for Amateurs. wl. 41 •14 5.6144,141.14.101,4444,1444,14,144+4014,1444014 4.</p>
        <p>The attention of cottagers and allotment holders is directed to the importance of pre- serving, and if possible increasing, their stock of I poultry, and of paying special attention to the management of their birds in order to stimulate increased production. There is at the present time special need to increase home production, and an opportunity is afforded to all classes of the cOmmunity to contribute to such an increase. Farmers and others who operate on a large scale would sell most of their produce. Smaller occupiers should first supply their own house- holds, disposing of whatever is in excess of the requirements of their own farailies. Every addi- tional egg laid and every additional chicken hatched helps the food Supply, and the number of people who might keep poultry is so great that if all of them made but a slight effort a large additipn to the country's resources would I be secured. _ _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>SUGGEST lONS.</head>
        <p>SUGGEST lONS.</p>
        <p>To present breeders of poultry;:—(1) Cockerels may be sold as soon as they are large enough to be disposed of profitably. Old hens and surplus cocks should also be sold. In this way room will be left for the more productive stock, and at the same time the food bill will be reduced. (2) All healthy pullets sliould be retained for egg pro- duction, for breeding stock, or for sale to others. (3) Care should be taken not to overstock the ground which is available for poultry. It is often the case that a dozen or a score of hens may be profitably maintained on a small area, say, 120 square feet, whereas double the number would involve loss. Management is important, but good management will not counterbalance the effect of overcrowding. (4) One or two batches of chickens should be hatched as fertile eggs are obtained and hens become broody, or where an -incubator is available. Chickens hatched in autumn and early winter will require care and good management if they are to make satisfactory progress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>FOUR TIMES PRESENT YIELD.</head>
        <p>FOUR TIMES PRESENT YIELD.</p>
        <p>To those who do not at present breed poultry: (1) The country is capable of producing four times its present yield of eggs and poultry. Many people have found poultry keeping profit- able, and many people who have not hitherto kept poultry can now increase the, food supply by producing eggs and chickens for use in the household. (2) Part of the allotment or garden ground available can be used for fowls without involving any very considerable expenditure. The time and effort will be well rewarded. (3) If knowledge of poultry-keeping is limited, oper- ations should at first be restricted, especially with regard to the number of birds kept. As experience increases the stock of poultry may he enlarged. (4) Fowls will utilise profitably household scraps and surplus garden stuff. At a time like the present an effort should be made to utilise all kinds of scraps and "waste" so that nothing may be lost that can possibly serve as food for poultry.</p>
        <p>Present and prospective poultry-keepers whose available ground is very limited should endea- vour to secure more land, or, in country dis- tricts, obtain permission from farmers and others to use their pasture and park lands temporarily. Advice as to how to proceed may be obtained in leaflets supplied free by the Board of Agri- culture, Whitehall Place, London, S.W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>NO GERMAN GOODS.</head>
        <p>NO GERMAN GOODS.</p>
        <p>Warrington Guardians' Patriotic Decision.</p>
        <p>LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD AND ALIENS</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians was held on Wednesday, when Mr. A. J. Jolley presided.</p>
        <p>NO FOREIGNERS NEED APPLY.</p>
        <p>It was reported that at a special meeting of the Board to consider the contracts, it was de- cided that in all cases the Guardians adopt the principles of the fair wages clause. It was also decided that no goods originating from Germany and Austria be accepted and that goods be sup- plied monthly. The Rev. A. M.-MITCHELL : "That no foreigner need apply," was that unanimous? The CHAIRMAN: Not quite; but there was a majority of the Board in favour. The Rev. A. M. 3lrrcusu.: It Was not unani- mous?</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : No; but still it was the deci- sion of the Board.</p>
        <p>STILL NO APPLICANTS.</p>
        <p>Resident Medical (iffic-ei:sh-i-p- dOis—A.begging. It was reported that at a meeting of the House Visiting Committee it was decided that the Clerk should confer With Dr. Hodgkinson as to the advisability of inserting another adver- tisement for an assistant resident medical officer. The minutes were confirmed. ' _ _</p>
        <p>Reporting on the same subject Dr. Hodgkin- s= recommended "that the salary be fixed at £2OO per annum. When the supply met the demand the salary could be reduced. He also suggested "that the phrase 'medical women eligible' be inserted in the advertisement. This would in no way commit the Board to the ap- pointment of a lady doctor.' Mr. BROOKS: I think we might adopt that and advertise accordingly. The Rev. A. M. Mitcnett : I think the sugges- tion or proposal to revert to a low salary is rather going too far. It would be better to leave that part out. Mr. BROOKS: It does:not affect us. Mr. bIATHER : It is only a matter of opinion. The Rev. A. M. MiTcarn.: I don't think there is any need for that suggestion. It wati decided to insert the advertisement again.</p>
        <p>NO GERMAN SAUSAGE. Several tenders had been sent in to the Board, and the Rev. A. M. MITCHELL asked: Are there any for German sausage? Mr. MCKINLEY : No! They would be bad before they got from Berlin. (Laughter.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>MAINTAINING ALIENS.</head>
        <p>MAINTAINING ALIENS.</p>
        <p>... - -- A letter was read from the Local Government Board authorising the refunding to Poor Law authorities of whole, or part. of the cost in- curred by them owing to warlike operations. in the reception and support of aliens. They asked for full particulars from the Board. The Clerk was authorised to supply the neces- sary details.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Not Guaranteed.</head>
        <p>Not Guaranteed.</p>
        <p>" The Daily News": "If every man and woman in the country were mated, the number of men who would still remain bachelors would more than equal the entire population."— " Daily News." " Punch ": "The Press Bureau cannot guar- antee the truth of this."</p>
      </div>
