      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>CYCLISTS' NOTEBOOK.</head>
        <p>THE CYCLISTS' NOTEBOOK.</p>
        <p>Good-bye Till Next Seaso n.</p>
        <p>CLEANING CONES AND BALLS.</p>
        <p>Bearings are now so well made by all the noted firms that unless a. cyclist is an all- weather- rider the balls and the cones will re- quire little or no attention. The following advice is for the " mud-plugger," who is gener- ally the most healthy of cyclists. One of the simplest and best devices for keeping liquid mud out of bearings is to put a piece of chenille dipped in oil around each one and fix it there with a twist of the wire upon which it is made. It will last a long time. "Scottish Cyclist" says its experience is that after a long and soak- ing ride the bearings run as free and clear as ever.—Bowden's "Points for Cyclists."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>PLACES WORTH SEEING.</head>
        <p>PLACES WORTH SEEING.</p>
        <p>Hoghton Tower.—Situated on the summit of an isolated and conical hill midway between pxeston and Blackburn, stands an interesting mansion, the ancient home of the De Hoghtons. The position of the mansion on a rocky emi- nence, with its massive towers and masonry, rendered it no mean stronghold in the troublons times of Charles I. For many years it stoutly held out against the Parliamentary forces, and was only reduced ,at last by treachery, when part of its gate tower was blown up, killing about 200 men, and affording an easy entrance to the enemy. It owes its chief interest to the visit of James I. in 1617, when, after one of the magnificent banquets given there in his honour, he knighted the loin of beef. The story is graphically told by Harrison Ainsworth, who relates that after' this knight-making monarch had called to the carver for a second helping of beef, he turned to his host, Sir Richard Hogh- ton,_ and said:</p>
        <p>"It is delicious! Finer beef nae man ever put teeth into. What joint do ye ca' it, Sir Richard?"</p>
        <p>"This, sire, is a loin of beef." "A loin !" exclaimed James, taking the carv- ing knife from the server, who stood by. "By my faith, that is not a title honourable enough for joint sae worthy! It wants a dignity, and it shall hae it. Henceforth," he added, touch- ing the meat with the flat of the long blade, as if placing the sword on the back of a knight expectant, "henceforth it shall be 'Sir-Loin,' and see ye Ca' it sae."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>SEASON 1914.</head>
        <p>THE SEASON 1914.</p>
        <p>This is the first season that I have been tempted to write a review of the seven months generally included on the club cards. The year that opened so pleasantly for us, with fine Saturdays every week, has closed in a style we little looked for, amidst a confusion that makes is wonder if there will be any cycling season next year. And if we in our tight little island feel the shock so greatly, for a score of Wesley Guilders alone have gone at the call, what about the places where all this ruck is going on? To a cyclist-photographer it is all the more heart- breaking, for he has records of so many of the places wantonly or accidentally devastated. "My heart bleeds for Louvain !" Yes, you old hypocrite! and if we don't make your nose bleed for it, it won't te our fault._ _</p>
        <p>Apart from the war, what an ideal summer it has been! The weather has been just right for everybody and at every time. The hay was got in in splendid weather, the land has at no time suffered from either drought or drowning, the corn of all species has had a splendid fortnight for harvesting, and now the potatoes look as if they are grandly ripening. Yes, those of us Who cannot directly help in maintaining our coun- try's defence can earn our Saturday's health- giving half-holiday by doing our best the other six days, and it is to be hoped that few bicycles will be put away after the club rides. "Keep fit" is the Briton's motto in this crisis, for the war will not be won till the peace is ready for signing. And nothing keeps you so fit as con- stant exercise and fresh air and scenes. And although one feels reluctant to mention the name of our Maker when so much blasphemy is flying about, one has only to get out into the glorious English country to become devoutly thankful for the Straits of Dover and a good fleet with dry powder. There now! Was there ever such a "good-bye" yenned by a cycling notist to his readers? Well, better luck to us all next year!</p>
        <p>ole darken all over at a fairly early stage. gndolle picture will be quickly buried in the 611'1' ,00d thing in passing may be mentioned; whatever can come of holding the deve'ilito negative up to the dim light, but „. to fogged with such treatment. The onlY judge when development is complete watching the emulsion by reflected light yfted • is, when it is flat in the liquid or ill* out. As soon as the last trace of vilitte..:l, for sion is darkening, that is the time to a half-minute and then fix. A writer ill tography" says: "The real need in photography is not for fast speeds but t'iwi - ones. To show how great this is I InaYY tbe out that during a three weeks' holidaY Spot south of Europe, where the light was brnit and with a camera fitted with a lens t "oces- isitost.rapid kind, I never once found 011,, the an to use a more rapid exposure tha;pater thirty-second of a second, while the P'-of s number were made with a sixteenth , second." SILVER-Sl°</p>
