      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>AMATEUR PHOTOGRAPHY.</head>
        <p>AMATEUR PHOTOGRAPHY.</p>
        <p>Pretty Negatives.</p>
        <p>The commonest, fault in modern photography is under-exposure. It has come with Me —Sand camera and the lightning shuteer. The maga- zines, too, are proud to show the reproduction of photographs obtained in some tiny fraction of a second, so that the amateur who has the wrong lens, the wrong plate, and the wrong light for such marvels thinks, he can take his pictures in the same fraction of time as some lucky snap by a man who has spent half a life- time at his profession. One cannot doubt that two out of three nega- tives or prints show under rather than over, or even correct, exposure. And the worst feature of under-exposure is that it cannot be teme- died. What the light has not had time to record cannot be put on with chemicals, whilst the action of the right liquid on the negative will often correct the over-exposure that has re- corded everything there is to record. The pret- tiest negatives are those that have not had enough exposure. They are bright and clear, and the beginner cannot help saying in the dim light of the red lamp, "Now here is an ideal negative." The shadows—the most transparent parts—are so white in the development, and so clean in the fixer, and the image remains so dis- tinct right up to the end, that one cannot help being satisfied with one's work. In reality, the correct exposure will cause the negative to</p>
        <p>Sold everywhere in bo)</p>
        <p>pills) tre 219 (168 Pills)._</p>
      </div>
