      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>TWENTY-FIVE YEARS in the FORCE Two Warrington Police Officers Retire.</head>
        <p>TWENTY-FIVE YEARS in the FORCE Two Warrington Police Officers Retire.</p>
        <p>A few days ago two Members of the War- rington Borough Police Force retired on pen- sion, each having 25 years' service to his credit. One of them, Acting-Detective-Sergeant Silas Clough, has long been one of the best known figures in the local constabulary. A native of the village of Croft, he joined the Borough Force in August, 1889, and soon proved himself an active and zealous officer, the result being that for the greater part of his service he was employed on plain clothes duty. During the whole of his residence in Warrington he has maintained a close and intimate connection</p>
        <p>with his native village, where he is well known and highly respected by all classes. In par- ticular he has taken a keen interest in Croft Parish Church, which he attended on occasions of special services whenever possible, and for which he rendered good service in raising.funds for necessary church improvement schemes from time to time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Policeman and Sidesman.</head>
        <p>Policeman and Sidesman.</p>
        <p>Some little time ago, in view of his approach- ing retirement, he accepted the office of sides- man in the church, and will probably ere long again take up residence in or near his native village. His comrades in the Warrington Force, as well as his many friends in Croft and district, wish him every happiness in his well- earned retirement.</p>
        <p>The other officer who has taken his pension is Constable Alfred Wood, of Sankey Bridges, in which district he did duty for a number of years. A native of the Peterborough district, he joined the Warrington Police in September, 1889. He was a frequent competitor at the local Police Sports and the winner of many prizes in the various events. Ex-Constable Wood maintains his connection with the service of the Corporation, having taken up employ- ment in another department, and that he may he long spared to enjoy his well-merited pen_ sion is the wish of the comrades he has left, and all others who have made his acquaintance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>WHITE 'PLAGUE.</head>
        <p>THE WHITE 'PLAGUE.</p>
        <p>A Disease 'That is Worse Than a Great War.</p>
        <p>ADULT SCHOOL LECTURE. A</p>
        <p>At the Sankey Bridges (Men's) Adult School on Sunday, Mr. dailies Snailhain, of the Cor- poration Health Department, gave a most instructive lecture on Some Factors in Con- nection with Pulmonary Tuberculosis." Mr. Snailham said it was now 32 years since the great German scientist, Professor Koch, of Berlin, discovered that the cause of tubercul- osis was due to a micro organism, the tubercle bacillus. After years of experimental research it was universally admitted that consumption was entirely caused by this dread little para- site. The annual harvest in Great Britain of this disease was over 50,000 human beings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>Loss t the Community.</head>
        <p>The Loss t the Community.</p>
        <p>In Warrington last year 116 persons died of Ipulmonary tuberculosis and 32 of non-pulmon- ary tuberculosis. The disease generally ! claimed its victims when they had reached an age at which they should be of the greatest value to the community. In the deaths in Warrington last year there were 20 b.A%%een the ages of 15 and 25 years, 47 between the ages of 25 and 45, 28 between 48 and 65 years of age. This showed that the most fatal time was be- tween the ages of 15 and 45 years, which ages were also found the most fatal throughout Great Britain. Statisticians hk.d estimated that an ,adult man was worth £2OO. If this figure was accepted for both males and females, Warrington lost by death from oonsumption last year between the ages of 15 and 45 years £13,400, between the ages of 45 and 65 .25,600, or a total of 0619,000. This was only a portion of the loss. During the early part of its course pulmonary tuberculosis did not interfere much with the working capacity, but for months or even a year before death the victim might have ceased work, and thus become a burden on the family, instead of earning wages. The patient also required a great amount of attention dur- ing the later stages of the disease, and in many cases a member of the family had to relinquish work to carry out the necessary nursing duties, thus further reducing the income of the house- hold at a time when it is most needed to pur- chase extra nourishment. It did seem remarkable, in view of these facts of lamentable and preventable mortality and the financial loss and sorrow, that it had taken all these years since the discovery of the virus before public opinion was ripe enough for the Government and local authorities to make a great endeavour to prevent the further ravages of this terrible scourge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>What 'Local Authorities Are Doing.</head>
        <p>What 'Local Authorities Are Doing.</p>
        <p>It w-a-s—a- hopeful sign ib-a-t -during the past I few years legislation had been passed through Parliament enabling local authorities ,and In- surance Committees to deal with the prevention and cure of consumption. Although local auth- orities were given power to erect hospitals for the treatment of this disease as early as 1875, with the exception of a few of the larger cities and towns, the treatment had been left to the Poor Law authorities and voluntary hospitals, who had done good tvork. In 1912 the sana- torium benefit under the Insurance Act came into force and was Undoubtedly doing a magni- ficent work in cure and prevention. The re- cent report of the Warrington Insurance Com- mittee stated that during the past two years 80 cases had received treatment at Sankey Sanatorium, 61 cases•had received home treat- ment, and of these 52 had also received extra nourishment in the form of milk and eggs. In 1912 the Local Government Board issued , a circular to local authorities stating that the Government would pay to local authorities two- thirds of the cost of treatment of tuberculosis in sanatoria or dispensaries. This was a great inducement to make the necessary provision for the treatment of the, disease.</p>
        <p>In 1913 two important Orders were issued by the Local Government Board (1) The compulsory notification of tubercul- osis, its object being the early notification of the disease to the Medical Officer of Health in order that the homes of the patierits may be inspected and the isolittion and treatment of the patient attended to.</p>
        <p>(2) The Tuberculosis (Animals) Order, which aims at securing the destruction of every cow found to be giving tuberculous milk, as well as all bovine animals suffering from tuberculosis with emaciation.</p>
        <p>These regulations showed that the responsi- bilities of local authorities had increased con- siderably during recent years.</p>
        <p>Exposing Food.</p>
        <p>Mr. Snailham drew attention to a matter of first importance mentioned in the Warrington Medical Officer's report of 1913,‘ that of the practice of exposing meat and food intended for human consumption in open shops and pubhe pltaces. In dry weather, when the street dust was blown about, the food-stuff must become coated with dust and matter which might not be free from tubercular and other germs. All food-stuff capable of infection in this way should be kept under cover, and street trading in like commodities should be discour- aged. Mr. Snailham also spoke upon the problem of slums, overcrowding and housing in connec- tion with consumption. He further commented upon the great good being done by the teaching of hygiene in the schools, and gave instances where such teaching had borne fruit. He said the awakening intelligence in the younger gen- eration was a hopeful sign. At the close many questions were asked and an interesting discussion followed upon many points raised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
