      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 26, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 26, 1914.</p>
        <p>PEARSON &amp; KNOWLES.</p>
        <p>Forty-First Annual Report.</p>
        <p>FIVE PER CENT. FOR PAST YEAR.</p>
        <p>The report of the directors of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Limited, to the forty-first annual meeting of shareholders, to be held at the company's office in Warrington next Tuesday, states that profits for the year ended June 30th, 1914, amount to £98,812 16s. id.; the</p>
        <p>balance brought forward frotu last year, £19,548 Cs. 2d., together, £118,361 2s. 3d.</p>
        <p>An interim dividend at the rate of 6 per cent. per annum on the first and second preference shares and 5 per cent, per annum on the ordin- ary shares was paid in March last for the half. year ending December 31st, 1913, amounting to .425,377 18s. 4d. The directors now recommend that a dividend be paid for the past half-year at the rate of 6 per cent. per annum on Ur first and second preference shares, and 5 per cent. per annum on the ordinary shares, making with the interim dividend 5 per cent, for the year, and a total distribution of ...t50,699 13s. 9d.</p>
        <p>------ The directors further recommend that £20,000 be added to the reserve fund, raising it to £402,500; that £lO,OOO be placed to a special reserve fund; that the sum of £13,450 15s. Bd. which represents the expenditure on capital account for the year, and £2,368 us. sd.—the cost of issuing the new bonds—be written off, leaving a balance of £21,842 Is. sd. to be carried forward.</p>
        <p>In view of the difficulties affecting business in all its branches and of the uncertainty attach- ing to the immediate future, the directors con- sider that provision should be Made out of the profits of the year to meet any unforeseen con- tingencies that may arise, and they propose that the special reserve of £lO,OOO be set aside for that purpose.</p>
        <p>WAR GIVES PARTINGTON ITS CHANCE. During the past year the volume of the com- pany's trade in all its departments has steadily declined, and the prices in the iron, steel and wire trades have been low and unremunerative. The position and prospects of this section of the company's business have, however, undergone a remarkable change since the war broke out, and the sudden cessation of German competition has already proved beneficial in many directions. Some loss and great inconvenience was at first experienced by the total failure, without any notice, of all foreign supplies of raw material which were largely in use in the works, but fortunately this company had at its disposal the ample resources of the allied works of the Partington Steel and Iron Co., Ltd., now in full operatign, otherwise at least one-half of its iron- works and wire mills would have been closed down for an indefinite period. The general dislocation of trade and tlipe scarcity of labour due to the war, are at the present moment causes of much anxiety and troible, and it is imi)ossible to forecast the immediate future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>£5,000 FOR SOLDIER-EMPLOYES.</head>
        <p>£5,000 FOR SOLDIER-EMPLOYES.</p>
        <p>A resolution will be submitted to the meeting Placing at the disposal of the directors the sum of -f-5,000 to used at their discretion for the pur- pose of assisting the wives and families of those Of this company's workmen who have joined Kitchener's Army, and the Territorials and Reservists. In compliance with the Articles of Associa- tion, Mr. John Bleckly, jun., retires from the Board, and is eligible for re-election.</p>
        <p>The auditors, Messrs. Blouse and Sons, also retire, and are eligible for re-election.</p>
        <p>IRON AND STEEL TRADES AND THE WAR. The monthly report just issued of the British Steel Smelters' Association states that the posi- tion of the iron and steel trades, after a month of war, is of a satisfactory character. Firma executing Government orders are busy, and there are improvements in other directions, due in part to the entire suspension of imports from Germany and Belgium. Some firms are working for stock in anticipation of a demand for steel goods which they would be required to meet. Iron and steel prices remain firm, and there is in some quarters a tendency to adopt an atti- tude of retaliation towards consumers of manu- factured steel who previous to the war were obtaining their supplies from the Continent The report concludes "We venture to ques- tion the wisdom of such an attitude, and to suggest that a better course will be for the manufacturers to do all possible to foster the home market, and to take the exceptional op- portunity of demonstrating that the require- ments of home consumers can be adequately met without the necessity of bringing in outside competitors."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>EDUCATION COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Considerable business was got through in little time at the monthly meeting of the War- rington Education Committee, which was held in the Town Hall on Monday evening, and was attended by the following: Councillor Roberts (chairman), Mr. A. J. Jolley (vice-chairman), the Mayor (Alderman Peacock), Mrs. Jackson Holmes, Canon Stevenson, the Rev. Father Wil- son, Dr. Joseph, Aldermen James Evans, Ben- nett and Smethurst, Councillors Henshall, Osborne, Broadhurst, F. Lindley, Wilkinson, J. Pemberton, T. Locker, Dickenson, Archer; and Messrs. Jackson Holmes, J. Longland, R. T. Fairclough, Kingdon, Upson, C. E. Parker, Jar- man, Plinston, and G. Walker; together with the .secretary and director (Mr. J. Moore Murray).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>FINANCIAL.</head>
        <p>FINANCIAL.</p>
        <p>....... The Financial Committee recommended Vile payment of accounts totalling £6,439 13s. 5d., and reported • that instalments of fee grants totalling ..e491 2s. 6d. had been received from the Board of Education.</p>
        <p>It was reported to the committee that Mr. Joseph Benion and Mr. William Pinson, clerks in the office, had joined Lord Kitchener's Army and the Territorial Force respectively, and it was resolved that the sum of 10s. per week be paid to their nominees during the period of their absence on military service. It waS noted that the Town Council had in- creased the rent of the office from 9-50 to I:100 per annum. The report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>SUCCESSFUL STUDENTS. Gratifying Examinatan Results.</head>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL STUDENTS. Gratifying Examinatan Results.</p>
        <p>Canon STEvrs- so-a, moving the t;CI-o-ITiien of the School Management Sub-committee minutes, said the results of the science and art examina- tions had not yet been tabulated, but would be put before the committee in due course, but he would like to take that opportunity of saying that they showed a distinct improvement upon last year, and were very satisfactory. The Rev. Father WILSON seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. UPSON referred to i-C-P-iWa-g-r.aph in the report relating to the science and art examina- tions, of the Board of Education and the science examinations recently conducted by the Union of Lancashire Institutes, and said that, whilst in the nature of things it was impossible to communicate in the printed reports all the re- sults of those examinations, he thought the members of the full committee ought to know that the list of successes was of a surprising natu% in so far as the positions obtained by Warrington students in those examinations went. They reflected the very highest credit on the schools concerned, and also upon the students themselves. In one of the subjects in- volved a Warrington student had gained the ' second place in all England. The minutes cer- tainl3- ought not to pass without the attention of the committee being called to those very cellent results achieved by students and teachers of the local schools. The minutes were confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>APPOINTMENTS, SALARIES, AND SCHOLARSHIPS.</head>
        <p>APPOINTMENTS, SALARIES, AND SCHOLARSHIPS.</p>
        <p>_ The following recommendations of the Cbm- mittee were also confirmed:—</p>
        <p>Mr. John Foulkes was appointed a certificated assistant at Hamilton-street School.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Cora E. Humphreys, supply teacher at St. James' Infants', and Mrs. Mary Appleton, St. Mary's Boys' School. Miss Charlotte E. Penney, certificated assist- ant mistress, infants' department, Bolton Coun- cil School, at a commencing salary at the rate of .280 per annum, and Miss Dorothy May Per- cival, pupil teachership in the girl? department of the Bolton Council School, for two years, at a salary of £l5 for the first year and £2O for the second year. The committee recommended with regard to the staffing of the mixed department of the Trinity School, that in view of the present number of children on the books the managers be informed that the committee Are not prepared to sanction any change in the present staffing arrangements. Increases of salary were recommended to Mr. Frank S. Phillips, Arpley-street Mixed Depart- ment, £5; and Mr. Maurice J. Glover, Parochial Boys' Department, .25. It was decided to renew for a further year the special scholarship (of .27 10s.) to the School of Art awarded to Miss Mildred J. Shakeshaft. Conditionally upon a( satisfactory report being received at the end of the first year, the com- mittee recommended that Reginald C. Essen- high be granted £4O per annurd for two years to enable him to take up a Board of Education free studentship at the Royal College of Art, London.</p>
        <p>The committee approved the recommendations of a sub-committee that Mr. George Newburn be appointed superintendent of the Beamont Branch Technical School for the session 1914-15; that Mr. Wm. C. Entwhistle be appointed teacher of the men's class at the Evelyn-street Branch Technical School; and that Mr. Richard Dahl be appointed teacher of German at the Commercial Institute, at a salary of £3O for the session.</p>
        <p>It was recommended that the salary of Mr.</p>
        <p>Norman E. France, assistant master at the Secondary School and Technical Institute, be increased £lO per annum. The Rev. Canon Stevenson and *Messrs. A. Johnson and J. Upson be appointed a sub-com- mittee to consider the question of the care of the, Secondary School athletic field.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>FREE MEALS FOR POOR CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>FREE MEALS FOR POOR CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The School Attendance Committee reported that during July and August 100 children were exempted from attendance at school in order to go to work, and that ten prosecutions were undertaken, eight parents being fined and one attendance order made, whilst one defendant was dismissed with a caution. The Director re- ported that during the school holidays a fund had been raised by the Mayor, and that 5,392 meals had been provided to poor children; fur- ther, arrangements had been made for the put- ting into operation of the Provision of Meals Act (1906) from and including August 17th. It was pointed out that the amended Provision of Meals Act now legalises the supplying of meals during holidays and on other days when the schools are not open, and abolishes the necessity of obtaining the sanction of the Board of Educa- tion to expenditure.</p>
        <p>In moving the adoption of the report, Mr. PLINSTON said that the committee deeply appre- ciated the services of the official staff in connec- tion with the arrangements made for school meals during the holidays. The provision now made for the supplying of meals during school holidays would appeal to everybody. The report was then adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>SITES AND BUILDINGS.</head>
        <p>SITES AND BUILDINGS.</p>
        <p>' The Sites and Building; —6o-rn-niltiee recom- mended that the tender of Messrs. S. and F. Acton, amounting to ..t.79 Ios., for supplying and fixing two "Robin Hood" boilers at the Bea- moat Council_ School, be accepted. It was de- cided to advertise for tenders for the erection of the proposed Parr-street School, the tenders to be delivered at the Education Office by noon on October 9th. A letter was read from the Town Clerk with reference to a joint erection of boundary walls on the site of the Parr-street Council School, in which it was stated that the Electricity Com- mittee had decided not to entertain the idea, but that the Sanitary Works Committee had passed the following resolution :•That the Edu- cation Committee be allowed to remove, and to re-erect, on the boundary line, at their own ex- pense, the wall belonging to this compittee, and that this committee agree to pay one-half the cost of completing the wall to the same height and design, along the remainder of this com- mittee's land. It was resolved that the provi- sion of a boundary fence on the site contiguous to'the Electricity Works be not proceeded with for the present. It Was resolved by the committee that the tender of Mr. T. Speak, amounting to £27 10s., be accepted for re-wiring the electric lights in the girls' department and basement at the Eve- lyn-street Council School. Mr. FAIRCLOUGH moved and Alderman SMETHURST seconded the adoption of the• minutes.</p>
        <p>Councillor ARCHER referred to the proposal that the tender of Mr. T. Speak for re-wiring the electric lights in the girls' department and basement at the Evelyn-street Council School be accepted, and saidothat it seemed rather strange that the work should have to be done again, seeing that the 'schools were so modern. More- over, he would like to know who Mr. Speak was. Were there no contractors in Warrington who could do that class of work? He thought there ought to be some explanation. The minutes were adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>WAR, THE ENEMY OF MANKIND.</head>
        <p>WAR, THE ENEMY OF MANKIND.</p>
        <p>Presiding over the usual pleasant Sunday afternoon service at Wycliffe Church qn Sunday afternoon, Mr. H. Osborne, in the course of his opening remarks,. spoke briefly of the horrors of war, grouping his subject under three heads: What war does; what it is doing; and what it will do for the future! Mr. Arthur Griffiths gave the address, taking as his subject the pro- phecies concerning the coming wars of nations and the prophecy ü to the future, "when men shall learn war no more." War, declared the speaker, was contrary to the mind of God, to the love of Christ, and to the brotherhood Of man; it was the enemy of mankind. Mr. liCEachern read the lesson, and the Secre- tary (Mr. B. Farrar) offered a prayer of inter- cession for our country in the hour of her need, for her safety and prosperity, and for comfort in the homes desolated by the war. A vote of sympathy was passed to Mr. Hackett and family in the loss they have sustained by the death of their son.</p>
        <p>The Secretary read a message from one of the brethren—Private Yeates, of Orford—now serv- ing at the front.</p>
        <p>Touched. the Bare Spot. The little Quaker sat behind two ladies of the Four Hundred at the opera. "I am cold," complained one, so that he could hear. He leaned forward and touched her gently on the shoulder. "I think," said he, "that what thee needs is another necklace."</p>
      </div>
