      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>LONDON TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>LONDON TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>Changed Aspects the War has</p>
        <p>Brought About.</p>
        <p>SUCCOURING REFUGEES.</p>
        <p>BY A SPECIAL CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>Sundays are becoming more normal than they were at the opening of August. I said more normal, not like as they were wont to be; for everywhere the gentlemen in khaki are in evi- dence, in the street, on the 'bus or tram, in the train, whether on or underground, they are to be seen; and only to be seen; it is seldom that they are heard. They are heard, perhaps, occasion- ally, marching to "It's a long way to Tipper- ary," or shouting "Are we downhearted?" to which last the answer is a chorus in NO! But there is not that atmosphere of doubt, forebod- ing, bewilderment, which was felt the first week of the war. Sundays now are as quiet almost as on ordinary days, except that there are so many strangers.</p>
        <p>French and Belgian Arrivals. A novel aspect of London to-day is found at the gates leading to Charing Cross Station. There is a crowd every day now from five to eight in the afternoon. The sight looked for by many spectsprs is the quota of Belgian and French refugees arriving from Folkestone. When one observes and hears the crowd it is found quite a mixed one. French, in various tones and degrees of elegance, is heard; and though some strangers look wistful, there is a remarkable degree of calm maintained. I am proud to be able to add that the crowd is not wholly idle; the serious view of affairs taken by so many, the human sympathy called forth so widely, are quite apparent; and touching tales of readiness to help the strangers at these very gates are quite common. Sure it is that chil- dren's children will have tales to tell of these times at Charing Cross.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>Sharing the Burden.</head>
        <p>Sharing the Burden.</p>
        <p>A fried of mine, who is much concerned for France in particular, while naturally very kind- hearted, was telling me this afternoon how his wife and a neighbour had started a place of succour at Bushey, Herts. Touched by the tales of forlorn folk, they got a neighbour to let them have a large empty house of his free of rent. My friend was able to say it should be free from rates; the Water Company declared, therefore, it would not be charged for water; and the Gas Company not only gave gas freely, but fitted the house up completely. The house was washed down and cleaned, the neighbours con- tributed beds and bedsteads and bed clothing, and now as many as 30 to 35 refugees are found installed in comparative comfort already. More, very readily a subscription list of over ~tl.O a week has been secured, and these ladies and their husbands are working with smiling faces to comfort their guests. These guests, in turn, are helping themselves, for it was found there was talent and skill among them. Several had tied from home with but a scant supply of clothing; and now the Bushey refuge is busy with dressmaking and millinery, and so forth. To that, social and domestic entertaining of the foreign guests have been added, with much ad- vantage to all. Thus London and neighbours live this day.</p>
        <p>A Blessed Country. That reminds me of Friday night last, when a friend was at Charing Cross Station at 8.20 p.m., but on finding the place full of French- speaking Belgians and others, as he spoke French very well, he joined the lady workers in succouring the hundreds who had been dumped down—in Lo.ndon for the first time! and desti- tute! The old woman carrying a bundle tied in part with string and in part with red flannel, while in her other band she held a little cage, within which was a linnet, was a sad figure. On inquiring whether she had a place to go to, she confessed that she had not, but soon she was placed in charge of a trustworthy person, and carried to a comfortable home. This is a blessed country—so they say, so many of these fugitives from war's untender Imercies. And, again, don't our own women come out strong in these untoward circumstances? Ministering angels are they.</p>
        <p>• All Willing. The special constable is still a novelty. Before me lies the "Instructions for Guidance of Special Constables," signed by Colonel E. W. D. Ward, lately the efficient Secretary of the War Office, and a fine organiser; and they are counter- signed and approved by Sir Edward Henry, the Commissioner of Police of the Metropolis—for this circular has been issued in London and for London. A friend of mine, of the quill and pen driving persuasion, has joined the ranks of the special constables, and every morning I get a ' particular account of his vigils in Battersea, whither he was sent to watch the gasometers about Nine Elms. The hours of night and morn- ing spent sniffing odours of gas and tar are not lively. Perhaps it is fortunate that they are not, as he might be more elevated than he wished to be. But this is a specimen of what is being done, of the spirit animating all and sun- dry. There is a dogged spirit.of carrying this war through; and every man is prepared to do something, whatever it may prove to be, so that it serves our common Alma Mater—Old Eng- land.</p>
        <p>Two Grim Generals. "As you were !" " Business as usual!" That, too, is an indication of the spirit which is abroad. The war on the Continent is the biggest that ever broke out, but we are going through, and it is agreed that two of the most eminent generals fighting on our side in this campaign are General Hunger and General Money. The former is commanding grimly, in Germany, and thousands, we are told, are already clamouring for food in Berlin. The German Government is shutting ears to their. cries as much as they cans in order to divert supplies to the soldiers. General Money will fight on our side •at home and on the Continent, now as during the Napo- leonic Wars-1793-1815--and hence the motto, "Business as usual." If we are to aid Belgium, carry the war through from struggle to clear victory, we must have money, and we must go on making it. And believe me, reader, there is a good time coming for both agricultural and industrial Britain. Orders, orders, orders will pour in shortly to this country—the only Euro- pean country in a position to execute them promptly. Be ready for them; cultivate a cheer- ful mind; keep your powder dry; and business as usual.</p>
        <p>German Shipping. I, too, have observed that humorous spectacle in Cockspur-street—the recently developed West End appendix of Leadenhall-street, where all the shipping companies have passenger booking offices. Thither too came, a few years ago, the Hamburg-Amerika Company, and the Nord Deutcher Lloyd Line—the two great German rivals of the Cunard and the White Star Lines— and other companies, such as the Allen and the Royal Mail Companies. The Hamburg-Amerika Company, with ,Herr. Ballin as leading spirit. the rival of Sir Owen Philipps, of the Royal Mail combination of companies, erected a new building, with fine granite fi-ont, splendid plate- glass windows of which the Suffragettes were only too fond of cracking), and a fine clock sus- pended from above the footway. War came from the clouds, arid suddenly the fine plate- glass windows were covered carefully with boards, and all business suspended, doors bolted, and barred. Now, on the abandoned premises. in a long line at the bottom of the boarded windows, there appear the War Office posters, urging all to arms, K. of K. wants 100,000 men U! I wonder that the senior partner in the firrr 'eh und Gott—can sleep at nights.</p>
        <p>Pubs. and Clubs. London public-houses have to close at eleven at night! Is that a hardship? Is that a real cur- tailing of necessary accommodation? Let me remind the reader that what is stopped is the tap, but not the buttery; in other words, food may be had, no restaurarts are affected by this magistrates' order, issued at the request of the Commissioner of Police. Again, it is directed as a safeglignl for those foolish men among the enlisted who will get -drunk, of which a consid- erable number have been seen in London. It should be added, in fairness, that the great majority of the enlisted are above the average 'in this matter of drink, as in other respects, and their conduct is unexceptionable. Now that public-houses have to close earlier, it is antici- pated that an Order in Council will affect the sales of liquor in clubs also.</p>
        <p>The inventor has never been born who could patent a scheme for making both ends meet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>HOW BRITAIN KEEPS HER WORD.</head>
        <p>HOW BRITAIN KEEPS HER WORD.</p>
        <p>Premier and Chancellor Shatter German Slander.</p>
        <p>Mr. Asquith, speaking in Edinburgh, referred to the cynical references made by Germany to our concern for the maintenance of treaties. Since I last spoke, he said, some faint attempts have been made in Germany to dispute the accuracy and the sincerity of this statement of our attitude and aim. It has been suggested, for instance, that our professed seal for treaty rights and for the interests of small States is a newborn and simulated passion. What, we are asked, has Great Britain cared in the past for treaties or for the smaller nationalities except when she had some ulterior and selfish purpose of her own to serve? I am quite ready to meet that challenge, and to meet it in the only way in which it could be met, by reference to his- tory; and out of many illustrations which I might take I will content myself with two, widely removed in point of time, but both, as it happens, very apposite to the present case. Pitt's Example. For nearly 200 years the Great Powers of Europe had guaranteed to Holland the exclu- sive navigation of the River Scheldt. The French revolutionary Government invade&amp;what is now Belgium, and as a first act of hostility to Holland declared the navigation of the Scheldt to be open. Our interest in that matter then, as now, was relatively small and insignificant. But what was Mr. Pitt's reply? I quote you the exact words he used in the House of Com- mons; they are so applicable to the circum- stances of the present moment. This is in 1793: "England will never consent that another country should arrogate the power of annulling at her pleasure the political system of Europe established by solemn treaties and guaranteed by-the consent of the Powers." (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>Gladstone's Example. I come down to 1870, when this very treaty to which we are parties no less than Germany, and which guarantees the integrity and independ- ence of Belgium, was threatened. Mr. Glad- stone was then Prime Minister of this country —(cheers)—and he was, if possible, a stronger and more ardent advocate of peace even than Mr. Pitt himself. Mr. Gladstone, pacific as he was, felt so strongly the sanctity of our obliga- tions that—though here again we had no direct interest of any kind at stake—he made agree- ments with France and Prussia to co-operate with either of the belligerents if the other vio- lated Belgian territory. I should like to read a passage from a speech 11). years later, delivered in 1880 by Mr. Gladstone himself in this city of Edinburgh, in which he viewed that transaction and explained his reasons for it. ' After narrating the facts which I have sum- marised, he said this: "If we had gone to war" —which he was prepared to do—" we should have gone to war for freedom. We should have gone to war for public right, we should have gone to war to save human happiness from being invaded by a tyrannous and lawless Power. (Cheers.) That," Mr. Gladstone said, "is .what I call a good cause, gentlemen, though I detest war, and there are no epithets too strong if you will supply me ivith them that I will not en- deavour to heap upon its head."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>BLACKGUARD NATION.</head>
        <p>A BLACKGUARD NATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lloyd George, speaking in lOnClOn. said The man who declines to discharge his debt because his creditor is too poor to enforce it is a blackguard. (Cheers.) We entered into this treaty, a solemn treaty, a full treaty, to defend Belgium and her in- tegrity. Our signatures are attached to the document. Our signatures do not stand alone. This was not the only country to defend the integrity of Belgium, Russia, France, Austria, and Prussia—(hisses)—they are all there. Why did they not perform the obligation? It is sug- gested that this treaty is purely an erause on our part. It is our low craft and cunning, just to cloak our jealousy of a sußerior civilisation, which we are attempting to destroy. Our answer is the action we took in 1870. Mr. Glad- stone was then Prime Minister. Lord Gran- ville, I think, was Foreign Secretary. I have never heard it alleged to their charge that they were ever jingoes. That treaty bond was this: We called upon the belligerent Powers to re- spect that Treaty. We called upon France, we called upon Germany. At that time, bear in mind, the greatest danger to Belgium came from France and not from Germany. We inter- vened to protect Belgium against France exactly as we are doing now to protect her against Ger- many. We are proceeding exactly in the same way. We invited both the belligerent Powers to state that they had no intention of violating Belgian territory. What was the answer given by Bismarck? He said it was superfluous to ask Prussia such a question in view of the treaties in force. France gave a similar answer.</p>
        <p>We received the thanks of the Belgian people in a communication to Queen Victoria, which stated that next to the unalterable attachment of the Belgian people to their independence, the strongest sentiment which fills their hearts is that of an imperishable gratitude to the people of Great Britain. (Loud cheers.) That was in 1870. Three or four days after that document of thanks the French Army was wedged up against the Belgian frontier, every means of escape shut up by a ring of flame from Prussian cannon. There was one way of escape —by violating the neutrality of Belgium. The French on that occasion preferred ruin and humiliation to the breaking of their bond. The French Emperor, French marshals, 100,000 gal- lant Frenchmen in arms preferred to be carried captive to the strange land of their enemy rather than dishonour the name of their coun- try. It was the last French Army defeat. Had they violated Belgian neutrality the whole his- tory of that war would have been changed. And yet it was the interest of France to break the treaty. She did not do it. It is the inter- est of Prussia to break the treaty and she has done it. ("Shame.") She avowed it with cynical contempt for every principle of justice. She says treaties only bind you when it is to , your interest to keep them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>WARS CLAIMS.</head>
        <p>WARS CLAIMS.</p>
        <p>School Architect Takes Up Arms. PROFESSIONAL LOYALTY.</p>
        <p>The Sites and Buildings Committee of the Education Committee reported at the meeting of the latter authority on Monday night that they had resolved that in consequence of Mr. F. J. Barnish ,having been called up for military service, Messrs. William and Segar Owen be appointed to submit plans and carry out the necessary alterations at the Silver-street School, in place vf and on behalf of Mr. P. J. Barnish. . _</p>
        <p>Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOUGH said that Messrs. Owen had undertaken to do the work for lir. Barnish, so that Mrs. Barnish and her child might have appreciated very much the spirit the benefit of their services free. (Hear, hear 4, offer, and he TwlllieieChomprmomittpeeted Messrs .Owens' thought it was only proper that it should he brought ,to the notice of the Committee. It was only proper that it should be mentioned that Messrs. Hamblin and Wright made a similar offer. (Hear, hear.) He was sure the Committee would agree that it spoke very well for the architects in the town that they should he willing to give their services entirely free for the benefit of a professional rival who had gone to serve his country.</p>
        <p>WOMEN REPLACE MEN. .- The School Management Committee reported that Mr. Francis J. had been ca Coakley, assistant master in the Boys' pePartment of St. Mary's School, tgorariliatecil Force, up for service with the Terri- e lled aonfd• abitsenwcae during serilodie be of his military service, and that his nominee be paid the difference between the amount of his rsived the that pe wages and his Army pay. It was further re- Ported that six other assistant masters were absent from school duty on military service. and in their cases consideration of the pay- ments to be made was deferred to a later meet- ing. It was resolved that permission be given for the appointment of five supply teachers in place of the five assistant masters from the Bearnont School, who have gone on active service, and that the following be offered the posts :—Mrs. Elizabeth Norman, and the Misses Esther J. Kiisilaw, Alice D. Woods, Gwendoline 0. Hol- man, and Margaret M. Barker. The School Attendance Committee reported that Mr. John Knowles, one of the school at- tendance officers, had re-joined the Territorial Force, and it was recommended that he be granted leave of absence during the period of his military service, and that his nominee.be paid the difference between the amount of his wages and his Army pay.</p>
      </div>
