      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <p>"</p>
        <p>BIRTHS. MARRIAGES, DEATHS. Notices of births, marriages, and deaths, in memoriam notices, and acknowledgments of sympathy are inserted in "The Examiner" at the following rates :—Not exceeding 20 words, is. 6d. each; every additional seven words, 6d.</p>
        <p>Deaths.</p>
        <p>EURlOWS..—Septembe-r-21-st:.. at 10, Matthews-</p>
        <p>street, John Burrows, aged 54 years. BENNETT.—At Grange Mount, Padgate, on Tues- day, September 22nd, John Bennett (late of Bewsey Hall), in his 54th year. PEARCE.—On the 21st inst., at Glasgow, Thomas William, eldest son of Mr. and Mrs. Pearce, of 133, Bewsey-road, Warrington, aged 24. (Formerly organist at Bewsey-road Church.)</p>
        <p>MOUNFIELD &amp; SONS carry out Funerals EP • in town and country. Every equipment.— Office : 15, Church-street. Tel. 99.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>- - SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 26, 1914.</p>
        <p>-TUE NEW PATRIOTISM.</p>
        <p>There is some truth in the theory put forward by the advocates of•militarism that war is a cleanser .and,a regener-</p>
        <p>ator of peoples; but the truth rests upon other foundations than those such advocates would ,have us believe. In his remarkable speech last Saturday Mr. LLOYD GEORGE pointed the Empire to the way along which this terrible ordeal through which we are passing may lead to noble and beneficent ends. That speech has been hailed by politi- cal friend and political opponent alike as the utterance of a great man, a man who sees things in their true perspec- tive—one of the very few men that belong to a century who can grasp essentials and prophesy with faith and certainty. "The greatest speech of his public career," is the verdict of "The Times," which, although it "never questioned his great gifts or the purity of his patriotism," has often "had occa- sion to criticise his oratorical methods" and been "at variance with his political views." But DOW LLOYD GEORGE, the " pro-Boer " of olden times, the dema- gogue, the robber of hen roosts, and the possessor of all the rest of the epithets that a somewhat degenerate age tolerated, stands forth as the one man who can interpret the soul of his fellow-countrymen and put that inter- pretation in language that burns its way into our very .nature, and com- mands our attention and our homage, whatever our previous ideals may have been. It was not his terrible logic that anni- hilated the diplomatic case of our enemy that called for our special notice; the PRIME MINISTER had frequently performed that service. It was not the justice of our stand that he • preached.that appealed so much to our minds; SIR EDWARD GREY had covered that -ground completely. It was not his sledge-hammer blows at the enemy that pleased us ; Mr. CHURCHILL had already delivered sufficient oratorical thrusts even for this great occasion. It was his picture of the final outcome of the struggle in a social sense that made , the CHANCELLOR'S deliverance the most valuable piece of eloquence that has been uttered for a century. We shall return to that point presently. Al- though, as we have said, the war itself had been dealt with by other speakers —including all the prominent members of the Opposition, who have, most patriotically, ranged themselves on the side of the Government, so that we have only one party now, the party of KING and country—Mr. LLOYD GEORGE handled the various points in the dispute as only he can. Germany , told us our treaty with 13elgium was but "a scrap of paper." Bank notes, • said Mr. LLOYD GEORGE, are but paper made of rags. "What are they worth?" he asked • answering himself, "The whole credit of the British Empire." What,, he asked, would become of German trade if their commercial currency became as debased as this specimen of their political currency? He described the GERMAN EMPEROR as the road hog of Europe, who would ride down anyone in his path—a bully who -would be torn from his seat. He made short work of the German claim . to execute civilians for shooting Ger- man soldiers by asking what right had German soldiers in Belgium. One of the most dramatic parts of the speech was his presentation of the original quarrel between Austria and Servia. Th-e Russian Slav, said Mr. LLOYD GEORGE, on being told to stand by with 'folded arms whil his little brother was strangled to death, said: "You . lay hands on that little fellow and I will tear your ramshackle Empire limb from limb "—and he is doing it. "We are going through terror to -triumph," declared the CHANCELLOR, and it will be a two-sided triumph. We shall win in the field, but we shall also gain at home—which brings us to the really great part of the great speech. There is, said Mr. LLOYD GEORGE, in one of the noblest perorations ever uttered, another bless,, infinitely greater and more enduring, is emerging already out of this gret -a new patriotism, richer, nobler -Nil than the old. I see a new mgst all classes high and low, -nselves of set. fishness—a new of a country does no, the honour merely upon the maintenance of itshe field, but in protecting stricken tregs as well. It is a new from dis- which is bringing a new outlook over au c lasses. The great flood df luxury and of sloth which had submerged the land is receding, and _ new Britain is appearing. We can Sea, for the first time, the fundamental things that matter in life, and that had been obscured 'from our vision by the tropical growth of We have been prosperity ........ • living in a sheltered valley for generations. ;We have laeen too comfortable, too indul- !ffc gent, many, perhaps, too selfish, and the stern hand of fate has scourged us to an elevation where we can see the great ever- P, lasting things that matter for a nation- - the,great peaks of honour we had forgotten-- Duty, Patriotism, and—clad in glittering white—the great pinnacle of Sacrifice, point- !4. ing like a rugged finger to Heaven. We shall descend into the valleys again, but as long as the men and women of this genera- tion last they wilt carry in their heaits the</p>
        <p>image of these great mountain peaks, whose foundations are not shaken though Europe rock and sway in the convulsions.of a great war.</p>
        <p>We are going to see our duty with a clearer vision in future. A great threat —a great nightmare that has, as Mr. AUSTEN CHAMBERLAIN says, been op- pressing us for thirty years, is to be cleared out of the way, and in the clearing we shall come to recognise the fundamentals of humanity and for- get our selfishness and reject our flabby self-indulgencies! May we not hope that the first fruit of the speech and the most hopeful sign of the good time coming is to be found in the fact that on the day after it was delivered, Mr. LLOYD GEORGE'S speech was commended to the attention of the people from more than one Church of England pulpit—including that of the Warrington Parish Church.</p>
        <p>which A contest— , more exalt, recognition arm shedding the, recognitioh tha\ depend. glory in s its homes , - patriotism</p>
      </div>
