      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>WAR WASTE AND SPENDING.</head>
        <p>WAR WASTE AND SPENDING.</p>
        <p>Prospect of Unproductive Outlay of £ 1,000,000,000. ,</p>
        <p>* NECESSARIES v. LUXURIES.</p>
        <p>HOW TO SPEND AND HOW TO SAVE</p>
        <p>PROFITABLY.</p>
        <p>BY C. E. PARKER. We are engaged in what will be the most expensive war the world has known. We are spending g 1,000,0001 a day. In a yperaorb'ablstyime, the drain upon the wealth of the country will have been over ..t350,000,000. If it goes on for three years it will be about £1,000,000,000. From what source can this waste be made good? But perhaps first we should ask from what source does this money come, and we can best realise the position if we think of it in the terms of actual wealth and not in what is used to measure wealth—money. This wealth is being consumed in the form of shot and shell, guns And armaments, clothing, boots, saddles, accoutrement, &amp;T. It is being used to feed the two hundred and fifty thousand men at the front, and all who are helping to convey the stuff by the long line of communica- tion, beginning at the factory and ending with the trenches. These men are for the time being non-producers. They are consuming wealth, labour's product, and not in any way replacing it. From what source can this replacement be made? .How can this was?", be made good?</p>
        <p>Cut Off Luxuries. The best way, and the way in which all can help, is undoubtedly to cut off luxuries—all those things which do not go to maintain the efficiency of the worker. The large proportion of our people who are at present producing luxuries would then be free, not alone to pro- duce the necessary things required for the war, but also those things required to keep the workers in food and comfort, and to maintain their capacity in production.</p>
        <p>Saving by cutting off luxuries is not of itself sufficient to replace the enormous daily waste, but it would go a long way, and I dwell on it because it is one in which all, according to their capacity, can take a share. By ceasing to spend our money on luxuries and thus saving we help to make good the destruction of wealth inevitable in connection with the war. We help to replenish the capital which has been withdrawn from the capital of the country, mainly in business enterprises by war funds and taxes, and thus is kept flowing the fertilising stream of capital which is in some danger of being parched and dried up by the war drain. But there are some who say that money should be spent in these things as usual, and for the following reason: If no one bought motor cars, or attended the theatres, or bought new bonnets, the makers of cars and bonnets, and those who earn a livelihood in the theatre, would be thrown out of work. Many will re- cognise in this argument an old friend that has played a prominent part in the Fiscal contro- versy, the Land Taxes, Death Duties, &amp;T. The Tariff Reformer says: "These foreign imports ought to be made in this country because, to use a common expression, these imports take the bread out of the mouth of the honest British working man." The landowner says: "You have increased the taxes upon my land, I will have to dismiss a butler and a gamekeeper, and stop my subscription to the local Cricket Club, and men will be thrown out of employment." An All-important Truth. • The argument is the same in all three cases. If it is sound then in this present war we ought to spend all we can, and those who are able more than usual, irrespective of what we get for it. That is, we should seek to main- tain all employment in its present channels; or, as ,some put it, we should keep money cir- culating, as if the circulating a money of itself increased wealth. To my mind the argument is unsound. We must get hold-of the elemen- • tary, but all important, truth that material well-being comes from the products of work and not from work itself, that work is a means to an end and not an end itself. Work which produces no material result is unnecessary waste. Whatever can be said on the matter in normal times, we cannot at this hour afford any national waste, since the outcome of this gigan- tic struggle is dependent upon which of the contending parties can the longest stand the strain of the waste of wealth consequent upon war.</p>
        <p>While it is sound sense (viewing the ques- tion nationally) to save all we can and to pro- duce all we can, so that the drain on produced wealth may be lessened and the necessarily consumed wealth replaced, we should not lose sight of the suffering and inconvenience in indi- vidual cases. The idea contained in the phrase "The greatest good for the greatest number" ought not to blind its of the individual loss of the smaller number. '</p>
        <p>Avoid Overtime. Hence every effort should be made to help those who are temporarily thrown out of em- ployment as the first result of the policy of saving, but we must not saerifice the greater for the less. Every effort should be made so to distribute the work upon necessary things so that all may have an opportunity of earning the wherewithal to live a healthy life. It is not an easy thing to adapt to new trades em- ployes who have been engaged in something entirely different, but this is what is being done to a very large extent, and Iwherever it is pos- sible room should be made for those tempor- arily out of work, and overtime in busy trades should be avoided wherever possible. When this is done and there is still any considerable number of persons unemployed work should be found by the State or municipality, but such work must be of a productive character, bear- ing in mind that it is the results of work that add to our wealth and well-being, and not work itself. This is a far sounder economic policy , than spending money on things we do not need merely to keep some in employment making unnecessary things.</p>
        <p>A Revelation. FRYING in ATORA Block Beef Suet is a revelation. Sweet and wholesome, no unpleasant smell when heated, and no after-taste. Your grocer sells it—Ask for ATORA IN nom Re- fuse substituted brands.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>BROUGHT TIMOUGH THE ENEMY'S</head>
        <p>BROUGHT TIMOUGH THE ENEMY'S</p>
        <p>LINES.</p>
        <p>‘Vounded Soldier's Letter to Ills</p>
        <p>Mother.</p>
        <p>Bombardier G. Brough, of the Royal Field Artillery, in the course of a brief letter to his mother, who resides at 55, Wakefield-street, Bank Quay, Warrington, says: "I am in the 23rd Battery, attached to the 40th Brigade. As I write these few lines I am told that the Ger- mans are being driven back and a lady is going to try and get us our letters through. I hope this will reach you, because I know you have not received a letter from me yet. . . Cheer up, mother, for lam going on grand. You know I got wounded in the left knee, but I am , pleased to say it is doing grand. It is three weeks since it was done; in fact, it was done in the first battle we had. Please remember that ' I don't want you to upset yourself. . . We are having the best of the Germans. I shall have lots of news to tell you when I come on furlough. Tell to cheer up; I shan't be long before I see her now." The foregoing letter was brought from the temporary field hospital at Mons by a "Daily Sketch" courier, who received it from the writer. "The letter was taken right through the German lines at considerable risk," so a covering note to the recipient explains, the note adding: "We have had to submit it to the censor, as all letters from abroad must pass through his hands. Ile has now re- leased it, and we have received from him a letter of thanks for our trouble in the matter. If you write at once we shall endeavour to have the answer taken back by the same courier." Needless to add the mother and other members of the family were only too pleased to avail themselves of the kind offer Made by the enter- prising picture paper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>FOUGHT IN THE BATTLE OF THE</head>
        <p>FOUGHT IN THE BATTLE OF THE</p>
        <p>AISNE.</p>
        <p>Wounded Warringtonian's Chem Message to Ills Wife.</p>
        <p>Warrington has been represented at most of the great battles during this war, and that now progressiTg—the Battle of the Aisne—is not an exception. Local "Tommies," how- ever, ,take it all as a matter of course, and write home quite cheerily about the hardships they have to endure. Mr. George Thomas Yeates, of New Cot- tages,, Orford, is a private in the "Devons," and left Warrington seven weeks ago to join his regiment, which, after staying for a fort- night at Exeter, crossed over ,to France. His wife then received the following post card, written in French: "Madame,—Yesterday I had the pleasure of meeting your husband. He is in good health, and at present is going well on the journey. He asks me to send kindest regards to yourself and child." The card was sent from Havre by an obliging French gentle- man, who ivould doubtless be only too glad of the opportunity of doing an English soldier a good turn. During the following weeks this soldier's 'anxious wife received from time to time censored messages from her husband, assuring her of his health and safety, but on Thursday he wrote from the Alexandra Hos- pital, Coshain, near Portsmouth, as follows : "Just a line to let you know I arrived in Eng- land last night, and I am feeling quite happy and as well as can be expected. I suppose you are surprised to hear lam here. My wounds are not serious, although very painful, but I supppse they will be much better after the bullets have been removed from my thighs, and as soon as I can walk I shall be home with you for at least 28 days." Yesterday Mr. B. Farrar, secretary of the Wycliffe P.S.A., received a letter from this gal- lant private, saying that he arrived in England , on Tuesday and that he is wounded in both thighs and the right hip, but only his left thigh is really seriously hurt. "I was hit by shrapnel," he wrote, "but the piece will be removed from my leg by now. All I can say about the front is from my own experience. I was at the Battle of Leihnutow, but came out of that safe, after marching 54 miles and sleep- ing in trenches at night, with counter-attacks by the enemy." This, the letter goes on to say, continued until the great pitched Battle of the Aisne was commenced. "That was on Satur- day," says the writer, "and we fought day and night without food, except a biscuit now and again. Nobody can realise what a dreadful thing it is. During the four days I was fight- ing we were lying in mud and water, and it was raining all the time." The writer con- cludes by paying a tribute to the influence of the P.S.A. Brotherhood, and says that, whilst he was fighting, he thought how he would have liked to have been enjoying a service with his fellow=members in Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>TIIE STRICKEN CHESIIIRES.</head>
        <p>TIIE STRICKEN CHESIIIRES.</p>
        <p>In the early casualty lists 'published the names of the greater portion of the officers of the Cheshires appeared, and it was felt that the regiment must have been in a particular warm corner at Mons. Quartermaster-Sergeant R. Lyon, of the Ist Battalion Cheshire Regiment, who has returned to Chester convalescent, describes how they marched to Mons and entrenched. There were three battalions—the Cheshires (leading), the Norfolks, and the Dorsets. The Germans, he says, let us get into position and then they opened a terrific artillery fire on us frBm all quarters. They had a big superiority in guns—they had the advantage that way all along. Our men took what bit of cover we could get, and there wasn't much, I can tell you. We threw ourselves down and had to make the best of it. We were hoping our guns would be able to silence those of the enemy, but they were too numerous. The artillery duel was terrific and shells were flying thick. We held on about three hours and a half, and then the order came to retire. It was during the retirement that the losses occurred.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>WARIRIN6TON HIGHLANDER'S STORY. Officer Traced by a Reference to "Our Young Sub."</head>
        <p>WARIRIN6TON HIGHLANDER'S STORY. Officer Traced by a Reference to "Our Young Sub."</p>
        <p>Last week we published a thrilling descrip- tion of the fighting at Mons, as seen by Private Smiley, a Gordon Highlander, of 183, Knutsford- road, Warrington. A letter by Private Smiley also appeared in "The Times." This week that journal says : There has been an interesting sequel to the vivid narrative of a bit of the fighting at Mons which was published in "The Times" on Sep- tember 15th, under the heading "In the Trenches," from a Gordon Highlander named Smiley, and in which he referred to "our young sub." - Yesterday we received a letter dated "Hotel St. Cloud, St. Lawrence-on-Sea, Ramsgate," in the course of which the writer says:— __..</p>
        <p>I, and many others, read with intense in- terest the letter in your good column last week from G. H. Smiley, Cosham Hospital, near Portsmouth (of the Gordon Highland- ers). lat once wrote to Mr. Smiley, anxious to know, if the "sub." he wrote about waq nephew, whose loss (reported as missing) his parents are grieving over. I have just had a reply, of which I at once</p>
        <p>send you a copy. . . . The reply referred to, which is dated from Warrington, contains .the following passages : I am here for one month's furlough, and have had your letter from Cosham. You will be pleased to know that the "sub." who led us was 2nd Lieutenant Robertson. I am not aware of his initials, but I am also unaware of any other "sub." in the, regiment bearing the same lame. If he belonged to the D Company, then it was your nephew who led us. He was all right up' to the night 26th-27t1i August, outside Cambrai, and, I believe, was captured, unwounded, on that night, although I cannot vouch for the unwounded portion of the information. He was my platoon, 14th, commander, and I cherish happy memories of him. I sincerely hope he is your nephew, and I am more than glad to convey good informa-</p>
        <p>tion to you. Second Lieutenant A. W. M. Robertson, Gor- don Highlanders, was included in the official list of " Officers Missing."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 26, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 26, 1914.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>A programme which is proving exceedingly popular is presented at the Hippodrome this week, and, despite the present grave crisis, the audiences are large. Jennie Johns proves to be a comedienne of exceptional merit, and does not fail to please with her breezy style and such catchy songs as "On the pier," &amp;c. Other topical numbers earn for her a most hearty re- ception. Fun of the fast and furious order is provided by The Krakes in " The Electric Car Conductor." They get in some capital knock- about business and a host of good jokes. Their scenic effects are also well carried out. Dan Whitley is the essence of eccentricity and causes roars of laughter with his book on "Etiquette," and his popular song "It's for your sake that I'm singing here to-night," which is " accom- panied" in appropriate fashion. The well- known Medelssohn Trio, who, in view of the fact that even the names of German composers are anathema nowadays, are henceforth to be known as the " Halson " trio, prove to be expert musicians, and give a pleasing drawing-room entertainment, which includes songs and flute, piano, violin and piccolo solos. Despite their name, "They're English, quite English!, you know." Charles Emerald and Maude Dupre delight the audience with their sparkling come- ' dietta, "A Hot Scotch," and the Five York- shire Lasses are successful as singers and dancers. The miniature comedian, A. E. Burtie, who appeared as " It " in the "Darling of the Gods" one of the big Beerbohm Tree productions, is a great success as a comedian, and creates much mirth with his impersonation of the elderly "old maid," anxious to get "off the shelf." The war pictures are received with much enthusiasm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>"All Star:" Programme Next Week.</head>
        <p>"All Star:" Programme Next Week.</p>
        <p>An "all star" company has been booked for next week, and includes Solo, pantomimist, in "A Lesson in Temperance"; Edna Latonne, male impersonator; Henry and Liezel, acrobats and dancers; Lilian Carter, soprano vocalist; Edna Zora and Co., in a song scena; Fred Durant and Co., in a wonderful burlesque, "Everything"; and John and Lilian Grand, vocalists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>DOMESTIC DRAMA AT THE ROYAL COURT.</head>
        <p>DOMESTIC DRAMA AT THE ROYAL COURT.</p>
        <p>Those playgoers to whom good, robust, domes- tic drama makes an appeal should not miss "For Auld Lang Syne," which is being played at the Royal Court Theatre this week by a capable company toured by Mr. Bulmer. Although, perhaps, there is little that is strikingly orig- inal about the plot, the drama is well-con- structed and effectively written, a strong love theme running through it and the interest being well-maintained from the first act to the last. Epitomised, the story deals with the parting of Hester Clifton from her husband through the machinations of the villain, Phillip March- mont, and their subsequent re-union' after passing through many trials and troubles. Harold Clifton,: by marrying his heart's desire, instead of the woman selected for him by his family, brings upon himself his father's clis- pleasure, and is ordered to quit the ancestral home. Lies concerning him are told to his wife and eventually the pair, whose wedded : life began so auspiciously, are separated, only Ito meet again in the death chamber of the wife's brother, when all doubts are dispelled I and all misunderstandings cleared U. An outstanding character in this effective little drama of the fireside and homestead is that of Len Rushton, and it is cleverly taken by Mr. Clavering Craig, who avails himself to the full of the opportunities afforded him. Miss Mary Daiby, who impersonates the good-hearted Audrey Vernon, also acts exceedingly well, whilst the part of Sir Frederick Clifton, the old baronet, is delineated by Mr. Syd W. Davies, who gives a polished character study, rising to great heights in the stormy scene which ter. minates in Harold Clifton, the only son, being ordered from the manor house, with his wife, Hesta, a character most. capably sustained by Miss Elsie Savile. Mr. Alex. Gillette, as the underhanded Phillip Marchmont, earns the con- demnation of the "gods," who express the same after the time-honoured manner. The minor parts are all capably taken and the entr'acte music by the orchestra, under Mr. James Roy, is particularly pleasing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>For Next Week.</head>
        <p>For Next Week.</p>
        <p>Andrew Melville's company will appear next week in the renowned drama "Driven from Home," which has now been played before 6,000,000 British people. It is a powerful drama and should attract large houses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>STAR KINEMA.</head>
        <p>STAR KINEMA.</p>
        <p>This week's first programme included "Mon- key Intelligence," which proved to be an instructive as well as an amusing film. "The Two Ordeals" pictured the further adventures of "Kathlyn Hare, queen of Allaha." The law that no unmarried woman. can reign in this Actitious realm without first facing the wild beasts is enforced by a jealous prince, but Kathlyn is adroitly rescued by an American hunter. The Latest Fashions" was followed by "The Beggar Princess," which told how the little daughter of a prince who married a lowly maiden was thrown upon the world by the death of her mother, but was restored to her right position through the efforts of a blind , musician. The "Gaumont Graphic" described the war and sport, as also did the "Pathe Gazette."</p>
        <p>The second programme included "Germans entering Brussels," and "The Bosun's mate," one of the inimitable W. W. Jacobs' series. For next week " The Night Riders of Peter- sham" will be the chief film for Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday, and "The Midnight Wedding" for the remainder of the week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>WAR PICTURES AT ANDREWS'.</head>
        <p>WAR PICTURES AT ANDREWS'.</p>
        <p>The capital bill of fare at this popular pic- ture house this week includes a most interesting film, entitled "Trained Pets," which shows a number of clever performing animals in a variety of tricks. "The Viscountess Veschi's Jewels" pictures the theft of the heroine's valuable trinkets at a ball, and shows how sus- picion is thrown upon a poor guest, who, how- ever, is saved by his sweetheart—the host's daughter—the real thief being unmasked in dramatic, fdshion. "A Spiteful Neighbour" is the title of a light comedy film, which tells how one of the undesirable genus indicated con- spires to ruin Simple Simon's little romance of the heart by means of anonymous letters, despite which Simon manages to elope with the lady of his choice. "Called to the Front" is a stirring production, in which that big film hero, "Warrington of the Civil Service," ap- pears. Warrington twice falls into the hands of the Germans and is finally rescued from their clutches by his fiancee. From a pictorial picture of the war, patrons turn to a real one, entitled "Important Happenings," and showing scenes in connection with the great struggle of nations now being waged on the Continent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>PICTURE HOUSE.</head>
        <p>PICTURE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>`.` Heart of Midlothian," adapted from Sir Walter Scott's•,excellent story, is one of the Premier films to be shown at the above popular house next week. "The Impersonator" is the title of another picture which should prove attractive.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>PICTUREDROME.</head>
        <p>PICTUREDROME.</p>
        <p>An attractive programme is - announced for next week. It will include the " star " films "The Stolen Code" and "An Englishman's Home."</p>
        <p>MR. F. E. SMITH LEAVING FOR THE FRONT.</p>
        <p>• Mr. Ch'urchin, speaking at Liverpool on Mon- day night, announced that Mr. F. E. Smith, K.C., M.P., whose friendship, he said, was one of the most cherished possessions of his life, was about to leave for the front. Mr. Smith, speaking later at the same meet- inc, said the organisation of the Press Bureau, which he undertook when the war broke out, was complete, and he was now in a position to take his place in the forces of the country. That would probably be his last pubLe once before the end of the war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>LETTERS INVITED. ,</head>
        <p>LETTERS INVITED. ,</p>
        <p>We shall be glad to receive letters for publi- cation, in whole or in part, from those who receive communications from friends or rela-</p>
        <p>tives at the war. These letters often give, in a few lines, a more vivid description of the fighting than can be otherwise obtained; and their publication also informs a large circle of people of their absent friends' whereabouts and condition. All letters entrusted to us will be cared for and returned in good order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>"MOWED US DOWN."</head>
        <p>"MOWED US DOWN."</p>
        <p>German Soldier's Letter- to His Wife.</p>
        <p>1,200 REDUCED TO 194.</p>
        <p>So many letters and statements of our own wounded soldiers have been published in our newspapers that the following epistle from a German soldier of the 74th Infantry Regiment (Xth Corps) to his wife may also be of interest: My Dear Wife,— I have just been living through days that defy imagination. I should never have thought that men could stand it. Not a second has passed but my life has been in danger, and yet not a hair of ray head has been hurt. It was horrible, it Was ghastly. But I have been saved for you and for our happiness and I take heart again, although I am still terribly un- nerved. God grant that I may see you again soon and that this horror may soon be over. None of us can do any more, human strength is at an end.</p>
        <p>• I will try to tell you about it : On the sth September the enemy were re- ported to be taking up a position near St. Prix (N.E. of Paris). The Xth Corps,i which had made an astonishingly rapid advance, of course attacked on the Sunday. Steep slopes led up to heights which were held in considerable force. With our weak detachments of the 74th and 91st Regiment's we reached the crest and came under a terrible artillery fire that mowed us down. However, we entered St. Prix. Hardly had we done so than we were met with shell fire and a violent</p>
        <p>lii's-filzde from the^ene-M;;; infantry. Our Colonel was badly wounded—he is the third we have had. Fourteen men were killed round . . . We got away in a lull wittout being hit.</p>
        <p>* * * *</p>
        <p>The 7th, Bth and 9th of September we were constantly under shell and shrapnel fire and suffered terrible losses. I was in a house which, was hit several times. The fear of a death of agony which is in every man's heart, and nat- urally so, is a terrible feeling. ' How often I thought of you, my darling, and what I suffered in that terrifying battle which extended P'ong a front of many miles near llontittira. you cannot-possibly imagine. Our heavy artillery was being used for the siege of Maulieuge; we wanted it badly, as the enemy had theirs in force and kept up a furious bom- bardment. For four days I was under artillery fire. It is like Hell, but a thousand times worse. , On the night of the 9th the order was given to retreat, as it would have been madness to at- tempt to hold our position with our few men, and we should have risked a terrible defeat ! the next day. The First and Third Armies had not been able to attack with us, as we had ad- ! vanced too rapidly. 1 Our "moral" was absolutely broken. In spite of unheard-of sacrifices we had achieved nothing. I cannot understand how our Army, after fighting three great battles and being terribly weakened, was sent against a position which the enemy had prepared for three weeks; but naturally I know nothing of the intentions of our Chiefs. . . . They say nothing has been lost. In a word, we retired towards Cormontreuil and Rheims by forced marches by day and night. We hear that three armies are going to get into line, entrench, rest, and then start afresh our victorious march on Paris. It wo.s not a defeat, but only a strate- gic retreat. I have confidence in our Chiefs that everything will be successful. Our first battalion, which has fought with unparalleled bravery is reduced from 1,200 to 194 men. These numbers speak for themselves. . . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>COMFORTS FOR OUR SOLDIERS.. •WHO WILL OBLIGE "TOMMY" WITH A</head>
        <p>COMFORTS FOR OUR SOLDIERS.. •WHO WILL OBLIGE "TOMMY" WITH A</p>
        <p>SMOKE?</p>
        <p>His Worship the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) has opened a fund for supplying the rgen of the 2nd South Lancashire Regiment, serving at the front, with pipes, tobacco and cigarettes. He accordingly invites subscrip- tions, which should be sent to the Borough Treasurer, marked "Tobacco Fund." It is proposed to despatch the first consignment on Monday October sth, and it should ,be a large one. In this connection it is well to remember that quite a lot of "fags" can be bought for a shilling or two, and that every single little paper cylinder-full of tobacco will earn the heartfelt thanks of the gallant lad upon whom it may be bestowed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>HAVE YOU A SPARE BED?</head>
        <p>HAVE YOU A SPARE BED?</p>
        <p>•••• • • •••••• _ Gallant little Belgium has played her part in the great war in a manner which adds undy- ing lustre to her story. She has been called upon to withstand "war's rudest shocks," and many of her poorer people have been left in a desperate plight. Hundreds of them, renilered homeless and penniless, have been brought across to England, and the Local Government Board have asked the local authorities of the country to make some provision for their hous- ing and maintenance. The Warrington Council have decided to ask the townspeople to do their share, and as a preliminary have accepted the offer of Mr. Robert Pierpoint of the use of St. Austin's, Bold-street. They now invite gifts or loans of beds, bed linen, furniture, clothin-g and offers of food, as well as subscriptions. Com- munications should be addressed to the Town Clerk. Enough said!</p>
        <p>COMFORTS FOR THE "SOUTH LANCS." Mrs. Ireland-Blackburne earnestly appeals for articles of clothing, cigarettes, chocolates, &amp;c., for the men of the South Lancashire Regiment (Prince of Wales' Volunteers) now at the front.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>WORKING PARTIES BUSY.</head>
        <p>WORKING PARTIES BUSY.</p>
        <p>The Town Hall was a scene of great.acttVity</p>
        <p>on Thursday afternoon, when the ladies were busy receiving and apportioning the garments sent -in. Four hundred and thirty articles were received, including handsome gifts from the Warrington Women's Liberal Association (second donation), the Ladies' Working Parfy at Sankey, and the Working Party at Whitley, per Miss Fairhurst. Four, bundles were even- tually made up, one to Fazakerley Hospital, one to Burscough, one to St. Andrew's. Hall, Liver- pool, for use in the hospitals at the front, and one for the Belgian refugees.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>LIBERAL WOMEN AT WORK FOR "MR. ATKINS."</head>
        <p>LIBERAL WOMEN AT WORK FOR "MR. ATKINS."</p>
        <p>The Warrington Women's Liberal Association has been very active for weeks past and the party of over 100 ladies who constitute the sewing class, which is held every Monday and Thursday, are most assiduous in their work, making various useful articles for our brave soldiers. Up to the present. over 700 such articles have been dispatched, and the antici- pated joy a the recipients is the only reward these generous women get or seek. The goods are sent to the Base Hospitals, the Belgian Relief Society, and for the immediate use of recruits who are stationed at Liverpool and 'elsewhere. Everyone can help in this great work. We heard of the case of the lady who made a quantity of toffee, sold it to friends and sent the proceeds to the Relief Fund; also of a little girl of ten summers attending Arpley- street School who handed in a pair of socks, which she had made herself, at the Memorial Hall. Into the foot of one she had placed cigarettes, and in the other chocolates, with a note to the effect that the cigarettes and choco- lates were for the "Tommy" who received the socks.</p>
        <p>TERRITORIAL EQUIPMENT FUND. The total amount of the fund for Territorial Equipment Special Account is £567 16s. 9d. Included in this has been received :—Lady Greenall (second donation), £5O; Mrs. J. Cros- field, &lt;25; Mrs. Voisey, £2 25.; Mrs. Hawthorn, 4:2 25.; collections—Park Picture Palace, A:2 6s. 6d.; Star Kinema House, 16s. 11d.; "8.P.," 10s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>"CALLING BACK liEfi SONS."</head>
        <p>"CALLING BACK liEfi SONS."</p>
        <p>Patriotism that may be Relied Upon</p>
        <p>when War Drums Beat.</p>
        <p>PREVAIIANG CONDITIONS IN CANADA'S GREAT NORTH WEST.</p>
        <p>BY H. WHITHAM.</p>
        <p>We have received the following interesting letter from Mr. H. Whitham, of San Fran- cisco, who, as our readers know, is an old Warringtonian, and one of the most valued correspondents of "The Examiner."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</head>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—l am at the present time in Edmonton, one of the pioneer cities of the Province of Alberta, Canada. Something like two thousand miles lie between this place and my home, and I hope, ere another week is over, I shall be aboard the train that will convey me to Cal- gary, there to change for the train to Van- couver, which will wind its devious way up and down, through the Canadian Rockies. My wife is with me, and, together, we shall once more strain our eyes and crane our necks to find:and to gaze upon, the snow-clad peaks of these world-famous Canadian Rocky Mountains. In the years that are passed I have often noted the statements of travellers, wherein the globe-trotting Englishman was charged with being a surly, dumb dog, allowing often caste or the absence of an introduction to stand in the way of a genial effort to "kill time" while travelling within the narrow confines of a rail- road car. I have long been of the opinion that the charge was unfair, and now, after my most recent experience, I am quite sure the English- man's critic may well turn his critical powers towards his own inward self and say "You're another." While on the trains, from the time I left San Francisco to the day I placed my foot on Edmonton soil, I found it a most difficult task to elicit either answer to question or friendly comment on the grand and majestic scenery through which we were passing. Since being in Edmonton I have kept in mind the letters that have recently appeared in "The Warrington Examiner." Doing this, and re- membering my own experience in San Francisco some twenty-seven years ago, I feel fully able to pose as judge, and so to give an opinion, or verdict.</p>
        <p>Gigantic Infants. Edmonton is a city of some 70,000 inhabitants, and is the capital of the Province of Alberta, which covers a total area of nearly 256,000 square miles. Both province and city are still in their infancy, comparatively speaking, and much construction work was going on in the northern and central portions of the province, prior to the commencement of the war. In these times of excitement, I mingle with the heterogeneous crowds that gather around the bulletin boardsl of the newspapers, and I hear much of tile! language of these labourers from the construe- , tion camps. English is the language of the business man and the man of affairs. The United States, Great Britain and France supply the leading spirits always needed in successful pioneering. The Ruthenian holds the fort—he speaks in many tongues, and he comes from many and various lands. Take him and his woman-kind, and you have a 'race of downright hard and rough workers. They provide the navvies of the numerous divisions of the con- struction camps, and woe to the town-bred Englishman that enters into competition with them. These men are not especially or know- ingly clannish. They are not conscious of in- juring their fellow-workers; it is simply a con- dition that drives the English labourer to tread the ties, and keep on treading until he writes home in despair.</p>
        <p>Twenty-five years ago I was down, and very nearly out. An acquaintance I had made took me along to a labour bureau. I was given a job and a pass to go to a place in the mountains where a trestle bridge•had been burnt out. I was a common labourer, and joined the crowd that boarded the cars as such. The question was very soon put to me, " Where is your hammer, saw and square?" Too late, the scales fell from my eyes. Had I taken these tools with me I would have been considered a skilled workman, and instead of being placed with a crowd of Chinese to carry lumber across a val- ley where the bridge had been burned out, I might have become a "bridge-builder." I stayed at the job two days, and when I asked for my time I was told I was in debt to the cook and the captain bold of the construction gang. Essentials to Success. The chief features making for success ,in Canada are, first, health and strengtb, com- bined with a willingness to work; next, if a skilled workman, his trade should be closely allied to construction work in any of its various branches. A family should not be looked upon as an encumbrance, and temperance is always and everywhere strictly insisted upon, wherever efficiency leads to success. At the present time business and labour of all kinds is in a very ' bad state. The streets are crowded with Ruthenians—men hailing from Russia, Austria and Servia. The people known as Serbs are numerous and very hardy. The Servian sober and steady in every respect, and, when accompanied by his women-folk, makes a good citizen. While I have been here several regiments have left Edmonton for the seat of war. If we try to divide these men up we find that they consist of possibly two classes—the foreigners who are answering their country's call, and the Canadians and British who are answering the call of the Empire. Throughout Canada there is at the present time the greatest desire to stand by the British Empire. The people fully appreciate the situation. They fully under- stand the inner and underhanded history and purpose of the persistence in building Dread- noughts. With those at home, their strong desire is to see the suicidal and dishonest pro- gramme of Dreadnought building brought to an end, and they know that nothing but a fight to the finish will accomplish this object. Love for the Old Country.</p>
        <p>- - - Two days ago my wife and I stood in the midst of a dense and immense crowd in the . railway station of dmonton, as the soldiers marched from the armouries to the station. Scores of women were in the lines; these were mothers and wires, sisters and sweethearts. They were all smiling bravely; all had that drawn look of a conscious effort to be cheerful, which is more pitiful to the beholder than tears. A big, bronzed private stood chatting with his wife, who had in her arms a baby only a few months old. "I shan't be wanting these," sud- denly said the soldier, as he handed to his wife half a dozen medals won for service during the South African War. The wife took them, pro- ,' raised to keep them till he returned, and tried to smile, but the-unbidden tears rolled-down her cheeks. In this way love for the "Old Country" niakes itself manifest.</p>
        <p>All the way East, from Vancouver to Edmon- ton, stirring demonstrations of patriotism are reported. At Ottawa colours were presented to the Princess "Pat" Regiment. At this place the Legion of Frontiersmen came to join the Princess "Pat" Regiment. The Militia De- partment have decided to take few horsemen. This decision has caused great disappointment to these rough-riders, so it has now been given out that Cavalrymen can go as Infantry. These men claim they can muster a thousand horses without trouble. The Commanding Officer, Colonel Osborne, has just received orders to remain in Edmonton, and attend to the recruit- ing end of this terrible and tragic business. The poet is also well to the front, and from his active pen are coming blood-stirring lines. It is scarcely possible to take up a Canadian paper without coming across lines which breathe of the most earnest patriotism. I give the fol- lowing as a sample:—</p>
        <p>Britain's Call. Across the broad Atlantic seas, Above the surges roar, There comes a message on the breeze To Canada's fair shore. It's Britain calling back her sons Unto their native strand, And there with her behind the guns Defend the Motherland. The clogs of war are loose again, The dove of peace has flown; And o'er the world with martial strain Destruction claims her own. And Britain, calling to the men In her dominions wide, To take to arms once more and then Stand nobly by her side. For justice, truth and liberty, Britannia ever stands.</p>
        <p>Continued in next column,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>WARRINGTON WAR INCIIY</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON WAR INCIIY</p>
