        <p>National Relief Fund. The Hill Cliffe Baptist Church has sent direct to London £7 Os. 4d.</p>
        <p>• •On the 'Transfer List: George Thomas, the Warrington Rugby for- ward, has been placed on the transfer list. Sale of Work Postponed. The sale of work in connection with St. Mary's Church, Latchford, fixed for October Ist, 2nd and 3rd, has been indefinitely postponed. More Firemen Wanted. The Corporation are inviting applications from men desirous of becoming firemen. The necessary forms of application can be obtained from the Superintendent at the Fire Station.</p>
        <p>Early Closing of Public-houses. A petition urging the authorities to close public-houses in Warrington earlier during the war is being extensively signed. The Intoxi- cating Liquor (Temporary Restriction) Act is now in force in many places in the country.</p>
        <p>Lymm Baptist Pastor's Health. We are pleased to learn that the, Rev. E. E. ' Lovell, pastor of the Lymm Baptist Church, who has been ill, is on the high road to recov- ery, and hopes to be able to conduct the forth- coming harvest festival services in his church. Golborne-street Baptist Church. Harvest Thanksgiving services will be held at the Golborne-street Baptist Church to-mor- row (Sunday), when the Rev. A. E. Robinson; of Manchester, will be the special preacher. Anthems will be rendered at all the services, and a musical treat is promised. Local Cleric's Preferment.</p>
        <p>_ The Rev. H. H. Corsbie, after Acting as curate at St. James' Church for almost eight years, has accepted the curacy-in-charge of Thornton-le-Moors, near Chester. Mr. Corsbie has proved a very energetic and sympathetic worker in St. James' parish, rendering services which have been much appreciated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Workers' Educational Association.</head>
        <p>Workers' Educational Association.</p>
        <p>The second year's session of the local tutorial class in connection with the above association commences next Friday evening at 7 o'clock, in the Technical Schdol. The subject to be taken this session is "Economics," and the tutor will be Mr. A. N. Shimmin, of Manchester. It is hoped that, notwithstanding the present crisis, all the students will attend. Accidents.</p>
        <p>During the week the following have been ad- mitted into the Infirmary :—Alfred Barker, 30, lard-street, with a fractured leg; John Geraghty, labourer, employed at Bewsey Old Hall, suffering from a bullet wound in the shoulder; William Dutton, 24, Walton-road, Stockton Heath, with a broken leg; and Robert Trimble, 18, Monk-street, suffering from caustic in the eye.</p>
        <p>Loss to Local Musical Circles. Local musicians will learn with regret of the death of Mr. John Burrows, of 10, Matthews- street, Warrington, which sad event took place on Monday. The deceased was well-known in musical circles, and was a member of Mr. T. J. Down's Orchestra. In former years no ama- teur band was considered complete without him. The funeral took place at the Warring- ton Cemetery on Thursday afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Suicide from Boxyley Bridge.</head>
        <p>Suicide from Boxyley Bridge.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse held an inquest at War- rington yesterday on the body of Thos. Herbert Williams (21), formerly lodging at 48, School Brow, who drowned himself in the Mersey on Tuesday night.—The young man's mother, liv- ing at 1, Lomax-yard, admitted in answer to the Coroner, that when her son had been locked up" by the police he had threataued to commit suicide.—A verdict of "Suicide whilst of unsound mind" was returned. Field Glasses for Territorials.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Fairclough has been asked by Colonel Fairclough, in command of the Warrington Territorials, to make an appeal to those in this neighbourhood who possess field glasses or good opera glasses to lend them to the Battalion for the use of the non-commissioned officers under him. Good opera glasses will be acceptable if there is room to fit them with a strap. Mrs. Fairclough will be glad to receive and forward glasses to the Battalion, by whom they will be appreciated and to whom they may be of incalculable value</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Commercial Travellers.</head>
        <p>Commercial Travellers.</p>
        <p>Mr. P. O'Brien presided over a good attend- ance of members of the Warrington and Dis- trict Branch of the United Kingdom Commer- cial Travellers' Association at a meeting held in the Lion Hotel, Warrington on Saturday evening. He was supported by the vice-chair- man and honorary treasurer (Mr. W. P. Ash- ton), the honorary secretary (Mr. T. Percival), and the honorary assistant secretary (Mr. W. L. Walker). An interesting report of the recent meeting of the North-Western Federation of the association, recently held at Lancaster, was ,given by Mr. O'Brien, who was accorded a hearty vote of thanks. For the Mission Field.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Thomas Vickers has been appointed a missionary under the Universities' Mission to Central Africa, and expects to leave Warring- ton in the new year, to commence work in Zanzibar. As that district is in German East Africa, however, the arrangements may have to be altered.—Mr. Vickers was .educated at Hat- field Hall, Durhain; where he graduated Bachelor of Arts' in 1909. In the same year he was appointed a deacon, and in 1910 a priest, by the Bishop of ,Liverpool. He then com- menced his curacy at the Warrington Parish Church, since when he has become greatly es- teemed by all with whom he has been brought in contact.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>BLGHT SOAP FOR BABY'S SKIN IS CUTIGURA SOAP.</head>
        <p>THE BLGHT SOAP FOR BABY'S SKIN IS CUTIGURA SOAP.</p>
        <p>In the care of baby's skin and hair, Cuticura ' .Soap is the mother's favourite. Not only is it unrivalled in purity and refreshing fragrance, but its gentle emollient properties render it of great value in promoting skin and hair health generally. For the treatment of eczemas, rashes and other itching, burning infantile eruptions, warm baths with Cuticura. Soap, followed by gentle applications of Cuticura Ointment are usually effective when other methods fail. Cuti- cura Soap wears to a wafer, often outlasting several cakes of ordinary soap and making its ,use most economical. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists throughout the world. Skin book will be sent post free on request. Address post card to F. Newbery and Sons, 27, Charterhouse- square, London, E.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>WARRINGTON RELIEF WORK.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RELIEF WORK.</p>
        <p>Yesterday " The Examiner" representative was informed that during the week the Soldiers' and Sailors' Wives' and Families' Association, whose local office is at the Parr Hall, had re- lieved 100 Warrington families. During the seven weeks this relief has been in operation assistance has been afforded in over 1,100 local cases, the total sum paid out to them amount- ing to about ..t9OO. -</p>
        <p>Owing to the new scale of allowances issued by the Government, the scale of the Association has been increased proportionately, but there are no less cases needing relief.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>MAYORESS'S APPEAL TO WOMEN.</head>
        <p>MAYORESS'S APPEAL TO WOMEN.</p>
        <p>There are many women in Warrington- -anxious to help their country in the crisis caused by the European War. In order to collect information as to what kind of work is wanted, and what work indi- viduals can offer, an Information Bureau is open at the Parr Hall. It is requested that the various charity and other organisations in the town will send in in- formation as to what help they require, whether in kind or in personal service. Any women who can offer voluntary work, as nurses, needleworkers, interpreters, secretaries, social workers, &amp;c., are requested to give in their names at the Parr Hall any morning , next week between 11 and 1 o'clock. It is probable that every kind of service can be utilised in some way. May I ask for the hospitality of your c4lumns for this letter?—Yours, &amp;c., M. PEACOCK., Mayoress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
