      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 26, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 26, 1914.</p>
        <p>WORLD'S GREATEST WAR. What This Country Has at</p>
        <p>Stake.</p>
        <p>"GOING TO BE NO PATCHED UP PEACE."</p>
        <p>An audience of large dimensions, albeit not quite so numerous as might have been expected, seeing the paramount interest of the subject dealt with, assembled in the Parr Hall, War- rington, on Tuesday evening to listen to a lec- ture by Mr. W. Herbert Garrison on "The greatest war in human history." The charges for admission to the reserved seats, together with a collection made bA the doors, were in aid of the National Relief Fund.</p>
        <p>The Mayor (Alderman Peacock) was in the chair, and briefly introduced the lecturer to the audience. A RECORD IN HUMAN HISTORY.</p>
        <p>In the course of his remarks, whic-11-;e;e illus- trated by many excellent photographic lantern slides and helpful diagrams, Mnl Garrison said five empires and four nations were at war, and 25,000,000 armed men were involved. It was no exaggeration to say that there had been nothing like it in human history. We were in it, and our cause was 'a righteous one and must prevail. (Applause.) Here was a war of right against might, a war for liberty, for freedom, for the upholding of international law, for the safety and security of a Continent, for the rights and integrity of little nations. Not only had Germany broken her faith and flung to the four winds her treaty with Belgium, but she had behaved altogether in a manner which placed her outside the pale of civilisation. (Hear, heat.) Since it was in- tended all along that our time should come, we might thank our stars that our statesmen had the grit and determination to do what was right, regardless of the consequences, the sacri- fices and the cost. "</p>
        <p>How War Has Brought Unity at Home.</p>
        <p>How wonderful and glorious it was to see the unity of all the factions in England in connec- tion with the war. The blood of the British people was up, and it was going to be a fight to a finish, and moreover, it was a fight that could only have one result unless civilisation was to go down. So far as the British Empire was concerned, let them remember Lord Rosebery's solemn words, "If we go down now, we go down for ever." There was no man in his senses who could fail to realise that all along Germany had been aiming at Britain, rather than at France or at Belgium. More than anybody else, it was Britain that she wanted to crush.. She had now shown her hand and had said, in so many words, "England must be crushed, and the British Empire cease to exist. London must be razed to the ground and Oxford, too! The tombs of Shakespeare and Milton and Faraday must be desecrated !" And they liter- ally intended to do it—if we let them! The Germans had actually gone to the length of pro- phesying that they were going to be in Paris by August 16th—of course, they were not—and in London a few days afterwards. "The express train is not up to time," commented the lec- turer to the accompaniment of a chorus of cheers, "and methinks she is going to be very late."</p>
        <p>King Edward.</p>
        <p>The first slide projected on to the screen Proved to be a portrait of the late King Edward. "Let us," said the lecturer, pointing to it, "give a thought to the man who was respon- sible for the entente cordiale, which led to the triple entente, which, in turn, has developed into the Triple Alliance." Next came a por- trait of Sir Edward Grey, whom the lecturer described as a fine type of the British gentle- man, as well as a splendid statesman-.-"a man as straight as a die, without a trace of wavering or humbug about him; a magnificent statesman, and one of the finest Foreign Secretaries we had ever had." (Applause.) Sir Edward was the man whom the Germans were feeling very bitter against at the present moment, because he dared to stand by his pledges and the pledges of the whole nation. He was the one man they would "like to kill; they said so in so many words. "Personally," said the lecturer, again striking a popular note, "I prefer one Sir Ed- ward Grey to a hundred Kaisers."</p>
        <p>Our Gift of Heligoland a Terrible Thing.</p>
        <p>Apropos of the war on the sea, it was a terrible thing to reflect on the fact that Heligoland— which Germany was making use of as her naval base—once belonged to England, and that a statesman of ours in 1890 ceded it to the Ger- mans. It was enough to make that man turn in his grave to see the situation in which we were now placed owing to parting with the little island. But he (the lecturer) would ven- ture to prophesy that ere long Heligoland would again belong to England, and the Kiel Canal would either be handed back to Denmark, or, what was more probable, would become an in- ternational waterway. (Applause.) Going on to speak of the German army, the lecturer described it as the most wonderful in the world in many respects. It was magnificently equipped to the last button and the men were not cowards. The pluck of the little Belgian nation in standing up to the Germans in the way she had was simply superb; something that the whole civilised world could admire. (cheers.) As an indication of what Bplgium had suffered, the lecturer exhibited photographic slides show- ing the ruin wrought in historic Louvain, pic- turesque Dinant, and elsewhere, explaining that similar treatment would be meted out to our towns if ever the Germans were permitted to land here. The danger that they might come over here was by no means past; indeed, we were told that the Germans were now busy preparing a hundred Zeppelins, some of which were to be employed in wrecking. London and Oxford. The battle of Mons, the 'lecturer continued, was equal in size to ten Waterloos, and the battle of the Aisne was even greater still. Might the time soon come when the modern Napoleon would hear the summons to surren- der!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Our Gallant Allies.</head>
        <p>Our Gallant Allies.</p>
        <p>• From our own forces the lecturer turned to those of our Allies. The army of France, he said, had undergone a marvellous change since 1870-71, and was fired by a wonderful spirit of unity, solidarity and patriotism; it was actuated by a splendid spirit and led by magnificent officers, with General Joffre, a magnificent strategist, at their head. The Russian army of to-day was made up of true patriots and daring soldiers—brave, simple-hearted, earnest, honest men. (Applause.) There were a section of the people in this country who regretted that we should have Russia as an ally, forgetting that the Russia of to-day was .no longer the Russia which we had learned to despise, but was a country that had undergone wonderful changes. The Austrian army the lecturer dismissed in a few words. It had, lie said, been crumpled al) like tissue paper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Empire.</head>
        <p>The Empire.</p>
        <p>The lecturer then went on to talk of the way in which our colonies have rallied to the help of the Mother Country. British boys in the four corners of the earth had leapt forward with offers of assistance. India had sent 70,000 men, New Zealand 8,000, Australia 20,000, and Canada 40,000, with a promise that 200,000 will come from the great North American province, should their services be required. Not only had Britain overseas given of her men, but she was also prepared to pour out her money like water and' to send value in kind as fast as the ships could bring it. The loyalty of the Empire, the consolidation of the Empire, that had been brought about by the act of Kaiser Wilhelm was worth living to witness, for it was a great epoch In our history. The loyaltrof India, too, was a magnificent feature of these stirring times.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>VOTE OF THANKS'.</head>
        <p>VOTE OF THANKS'.</p>
        <p>The Mayor, in proposing a vote of thanks to the lecturer, said that he felt sure that if there were any young men present who were eligible to serve their country at the front they would regard it as a privilege to do so. Alderman Bennett, in seconding, said that it was a great country for which we were fighting to-day, and it was a great cause in which we were engaged. We had to see the matter through. The fate of freedom on the Continent of Europe depended upon the issue of this great struggle. The safety of our own country de- pended-upon it, and, above art the liberty and welfare of the 400,000,000 people overseas who were proud to belong to the British Empire, to call themselves subjects of King George, and who were rallying from every quarter of the globe to our support. (Applause.) The audience having signified their approval in the usual manner, Mr. Garrison briefly re- turned his thanks.</p>
        <p>The lantern was manipulated by Mr. W. Parkinson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>CALL TO ARMS.</head>
        <p>THE CALL TO ARMS.</p>
        <p>Splendid Response of the Nation</p>
        <p>, Maintained. WHEN AN HOUR IS WORTH AN AGE.</p>
        <p>The Prime Minister, in his speech at Edin- burgh, concluded with a telling peroration. Great and incalculable, he said, is the debt which we have owed during these weeks, and which in increasing measure we shall continue to owe, to our Navy. The Navy needs no help, and as the months roll on—thanks to a far- sighted policy in the past—its proportionate strength will grow. If we turn to our Army we can say with equal justice and pride that, during these weeks, it has revived the most glorious records of its past. Sir John French and his gallant officers and men live in our hearts as they will live in the memories of those who come afterwards. But splendid achievements such as these— equally splendid in retirement and in advance— cannot be won without a heavy expenditure of life and limb, of equipment and supplies. Even now, at this very early stage, I suppose there is hardly a person here who is not suffering from anxiety and suspense. Some of us are plunged in sorrow for the loss of those we love, cut off, some of them, in the springtime of their young lives. We will not mourn for them overmuch. "One crowded hour of glorious life Is worth an age without a name."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>TERRITORIAL RESERVE BATTALION. Public Meeting Next Tuesday.</head>
        <p>TERRITORIAL RESERVE BATTALION. Public Meeting Next Tuesday.</p>
        <p>"PALS" COMPANIES FOR WARRINGTON. . Despite the fact that the War Office deemed it unnecessary to form a Warrington "Pals" Bat- talion, opportunities are now being offered for inaugurating " Pals " companies in the reserve Battalion of the 4th South Lancashire Regi- ment, now being formed. The battalion is primarily for home defence, but it is desirable that the great majority of the 700 men required shall volunteer for foreign service, so that they can, if necessary, be drafted into the ranks of the 4th Battalion. It is -earnestly hoped that the necessary number of men will make their applications as early as possible. The companies of " Pals " scheme will enable those who were desirous of joining the Warrington " Pals " Bat- talion to throw in their lot with the new bat- talion, as the officer commanding will do his utmost to secure friends drilling, Sic., together, in their respective companies, or half com- panies, or sections, as the ease may be. A public meeting will be held in Parr Hall on Tuesday evening, over which the Mayor will preside, to further the formation of the Bat- talion. Leading local men will speak.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>CHANGE OF COMMAND.</head>
        <p>CHANGE OF COMMAND.</p>
        <p>Capt. Max Woods took over the command of the local Territorial depot on Thursday morn- ing, from Lieut. Robertson, who, together with Sergeant-Instructor Russell, is proceeding to the war depot of the b6ltalion on Monday.</p>
        <p>EX-N.C.O.'s NEEDED.</p>
        <p>Owing to the raising of the height and chest measurement standards, recruiting is taking place on a reduced scale. During the week about 100 men have been passed at the Cairo- street Schools for military service, although a greater number have presented themselves. The recruiting officers state that ex-N.C.O.'s are urgently needed. Those who are over 45 years of age or unfit for service abroad may enlist or home defence only, and these should report themselves at the recruiting offices.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE AND</head>
        <p>CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE AND</p>
        <p>RECRUITING.</p>
        <p>The Church Lads' Brigade scheme of raising and training a 'special battalion of members and ex-members for the New Army is meeting with gratifying success. The War Office accepted the offer, and, known as the C.L.B. Battalion of the King's Royal Rifles, the bat- talion will form part of that distinguished regiment. The details are now completed, and enlistment is taking place throughout the country, with the result that over 1,600 appli- cations to join the battalion have been received, and, since it can only be 1,000 strong, the possi- bility of a second battalion is being considered. Field Marshal Lord Grenfell, the Governor and Commandant of the C.L.8., asks that any eligible ex-members should send in their names without delay to the Secretary, Church Lads' Brigade Headquarters, Aldwych House, Cath- erine-street, Strand, W.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>APPEAL FOR FIELD GLASSES. Valuable Aid for Those on Active</head>
        <p>APPEAL FOR FIELD GLASSES. Valuable Aid for Those on Active</p>
        <p>Service.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—The result of pay appeal to sportsmen ' who are unable to take the field to give the use of their race glasses, field glasses, or stalking glasses to our non-commissioned officers under orders for the front, has been most gratifying. In the first three days after the issue of the appeal over 2,000 glasses were received. These glasses are being distributed as rapidly as pos- sible among the non-commissioned officers des- tined for active service. I should like to take the opportunity of conveying their sincere grati- tude to the owners who have given them the use of their glasses. Most of the glasses received have been of the best modern patterns, and it is easy to realise how valuable they will prove in the field. Those who do not possess field glasses, and who desire to assist, should send chNues to the Secretary, National Service League, 72, Victoria-street, London, S.W. All glasses should also be sent to this address. It will be my pleasure to send a personal letter of thanks to those who in this way con- tribute to the safety and welfare of our splen- did soldiers., _ .</p>
        <p>Every effort will be made to restore the glasses at the conclusion of the war. In all cases an index number is stamped upon the glasses, and a record of their disposal registered at the offices of the National Service League.—Yours very truly, ROBERTS, F.-M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>WARM-HEARTED WARRINGTON. Hospitality for Swansea Recruits.</head>
        <p>WARM-HEARTED WARRINGTON. Hospitality for Swansea Recruits.</p>
        <p>Under the above heading the "South Wales Daily Post" says : Writing from the Barracks, Warrington, Fred. J. Stephens, a Swansea boy who has joined the South Lancashire Regiment, together with a number of Swansea lads, asks us to publish the following facts:— Dear Sir,—l beg of you to grant us a little space in the " Post " for this letter. We (the Swansea recruits) wish to thank the inhabitants of Warrington for their gracious kindness in providing accommodation when we were in such difficulties. We cannot find words to express our thanks. Being in need of somewhere to sleep, owing to the overcrowding of the barracks and other causes which I need not mention, the Warrington people invited us to their comfort- able homes and made us guests for as long as we stayed here, the question of payment in many instances being regarded almost as an insult.</p>
        <p>The Swansea boys will never forget Warring- ton, and if one of them ever hears ill spoken of Lancashire people there will be trouble. We are quite happy here, but have not had our regi- mental numbers, so there is nothing doing in the way of drills, Sze. The reason for writing this letter is to let Swansea people know • What Warrington People Are Doing for the boys of Swansea. We are about 100 in number, and our boys are keeping up the repu- tation of Swansea for singing. Once more thank- ing you for being ever ready to receive com- munications fur the "Post" regarding Swaxisea and its boys.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>PROFESSIONAL CRICKETER RESIGNS. Mr. J. Hannam Joins the R.A.M.C.</head>
        <p>PROFESSIONAL CRICKETER RESIGNS. Mr. J. Hannam Joins the R.A.M.C.</p>
        <p>In e-ery quarter Kitchener's appeal is meet- ing with a ready response, and on Thursday another "star" of the local sporting world in the person of Mr. Jack Hannam, our popular professional cricketer, displayed his patriotism in practical form by joining the Royal Army Medical Corps. ' He left for Aldershot yester-</p>
        <p>(Continued in next columnj</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>NOVEMBER ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOVEMBER ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>SHALL THERE BE PEACE IN</p>
        <p>NOVEMBER?</p>
        <p>"A Member" and Cliques.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—" A Member," whoever this may be, is the usual type of charlatan who is ever prone to make accusations against public men, whilst lacking the courage to disclose his own identity. Is it not time this kind of thing was put down , by compelling those who put other persons in the pillory to at least let their victims know , by whom they are being tipbraided? As "A Member" he must know that corre- spondence is passing between the parties con- cerned by which arrangements may be arrived at to avoid a contest. This being so, what pur- pose does he hope to effect by the assertions . made? The accusations are,more fitted to his own actions. He is just the jelly fish type of individual who usually works in cliques—the, cat's-paw to pull the chestnuts out of the fire for those who dare not do their work openly. What cliquism can there be about a policy openly defined at a delegate meeting with a public Press present? Do the other people carry on their business as openly? I think not. Men of all shades of political opinion attend as delegates, and, for reasons best known to them- selves, "select men of Socialist views as their representatives. Why? Simply because no one else has yet been found to accept the responsi- bility. Obviously "A Member" does not understand the position of a Labour candidate or he would be loth to enter an objection. For years Labour men have been wanted, but owing to victimisa- tion and secret terrorism men have shrunk from the onerous duties. What have I and my comrades to gain by giving these services? Can "A Member" tell us? .From the standpoint of personal advantage, nothing. You cannot pawn your soul in the Labour movement. There are no Shylocks with whom to make the pledge. Is there anyone prepared to say Labour representa- tion is less necessary now than at any other time? Every trade unionist is agreed that our claim is overdue and even our opponents agree that we are at least entitled to some representa- tion. Then why not give it? If the other parties do not desire contests let them make an offer of representation and so avoid it.</p>
        <p>Our " Friend" gives his ease away when he says he does not agree "that some want turning out of the Council." Seeing that some must come out if others go in, he obviously disagrees with Labour representation. Instead of taking his stand against it he seeks by methods of subterfuge to effect his purpose. Yes, "A Mem- ber" is correct. There will always be cliques and Judas has also a long line of descent. Long before the war broke out it was known that we intended to run three candidates, for the November elections. We therefore claim to be entitled to consideration in any scheme to avoid contest. If by any mutual .arrangement we can agree to any course of action, so much the better. We are awaiting developments, and will act accordingly. With reference to the quotation " The Mili- tary Ideal," as this appeared in the July issue of the "Labour Gazette," it has no bearing on the war and recent developments. The tactics which make such an intrusion in connection with municipal elections are beneath contempt, and go to demonstrate the length to which some persons will go to vent their spleen. In conclusion, I ask "A Member" to come out of ambush and let us see who we are fighting; we may then know who are pulling the wires. As a trade unionist he belongs to the fraternity of Labour; be should be a comrade not a spy. He has fired into his own camp and hides under the white flag of a non-de-plume. This is the barbarism of literary conflict, the Prussianism of perverters, behind which the eavesdroppers and poke-noses ply their avocation. CHAS. DUKES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>RED CROSS WORK.</head>
        <p>RED CROSS WORK.</p>
        <p>Lectures for Nurses.</p>
        <p>The local Red Cross nurses are to undergo a course of lectures by Nurse Emmerton, from the Union Hospital, who has had great experience in the work. Miss Broadbent asked for her ser- vices at the Board of Guardians meeting on Wednesday, and pointed out that the Master said it would not inconvenience them at the Workhouse very much if she were allowed the necessary time off. The Mayor had promised the use of the Parr Hall for every Thursday evening, and the nurse was willing to give ten lectures.—The Chairman: We are very glad that we have a lady in our employ who is competent to do this.—Mr. Furness: Is this for Red Cross nurses only ?—Miss Broadbent: Anyone can come.—The members unanimously decided to grant their permission.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Preachers for To-morrow.</head>
        <p>Preachers for To-morrow.</p>
        <p>Legh-street Primitive Met io is —...lorning, Mr. G. Faulkner;'evening, MY. A. E. Hoyle. Bold-street Wesleyan—Morning, Rev. A. J. Morton; evening, Rev. H. Williamson. Bewsey-road Wesleyan—Morning, Rev. W. T. Derry; evening, Rev. A. J. Morton. Wycliffe P.S.A.—Afternoon, 3 p.m., Rev. T. T. James (chairman), Rev. W. Reid (speaker). St. John's Presbyterian—Morning and even- ing, Rev. W. Reid. Christadelphians—Mr. J. W. Dorricott, Stock- port. Cairo-street Chapel—Morning and evening, Rev. Irvine Lister. Welcome to new Pastor, Thursday, October Ist. Laechford Baptist—Morning and evening, Student. Knutsford-road Primitive Methodist—Morn- ing and evening, Rev. A. J. Chessum. Emmanuel Church, Bold-street--Morning and evening, Rev. W. T. Jaye.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Vital Statistics.</head>
        <p>Vital Statistics.</p>
        <p>--- --- The weekly report on the health of Warring- ton issued by Dr. G. W. N. Joseph, Medical Officer of Health, °sets forth that during the seven days ended September 19th there were 44 births (14 male and 30 female) as against 31 in the corresponding period of last year. During the same period the number of deaths also showed a big increase, viz., from 17 to 30. No fewer than 12 of the deaths were those of chil- dren under one year of age, whilst a further seven died at ages ranging from one year to five. Two of the deaths were those of persons between 15 and 25, four of those between 25 and 65, and five of those who were either nearing or had passed the allotted span. The death- rate was 20.8, as compared with 15.4 for the previous six weeks, and 11.9 for the correspond- ing week of last year. The birth-rate was 30.5 as against 23.9 last year. The deaths included 10 from diarrhcea, two from enteritis, two from phthisis, and one each from the following causes: Tubercular disease (other than phthisis), pneumonia, premature birth, and heart disease, and 11 from all other causes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>CALL TO ARMS—continued.</head>
        <p>CALL TO ARMS—continued.</p>
        <p>day, and the best wishes of all go with him. Whilst he has been with the Warrington Club he has enjoyed much popularity, and the pub- lic showed their appreciation of his valuable services on June 27th last, when, on the occa- sion of his benefit match', there was the largest attendance that had been recorded for several seasons, and the subscription list showed a marked increase on any of the pre- vious years. On that day Hannam was the highest scorer, making only 10 short of his half-century, a total which contributed greatly to Warrington's ultimate win. His record for , the past season- was as follows:—Bowling: Overs, 220; maidens, 53; runs, 519; wickets, 60; , average, 8.65. Batting: Innings, 18; not out, 3; total runs, 181; most runs in an innings, 40; average, 12.06. We understand that a promise has been made to Mr. Hannam to the effect that the professionalship will be kept open for him for next season, and all lovers of the game will join in the hope that he may be back again with the Warrington team for the 1915 season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Difference.</head>
        <p>The Difference.</p>
        <p>" One walks in his sleep and the other sleeps in his walk—that's the difference," said the man who seemed to be letting out an audible thought. "Difference between what?" asked the party with the butt-in habit.</p>
        <p>"Between a somnambulist and a messenger boy," explained the noisy thinker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>READY FOR ANY EMERGENCY.</head>
        <p>READY FOR ANY EMERGENCY.</p>
        <p>"How England Prepared for War" is the heading of a remarkable article in the "Fort- nightly Review," in which Mr. Archibald Mad explains how it came about that we were ready to strike the moment Germany showed her hand. We astonished ourselves and the world by the alacrity with which we got to work; but this is why, Mr. Hurd says, we were ready : " A few simple orders, and an Expeditionary Force was ready to be moved over sea directly the Navy could give a guarantee of safe trans- port," says Mr. Hurd. "Only a few months before Field-Marshal Earl Roberts had declared 'We have no Army.' The Army which we I had not got exercised, if we may believe French statements, no small influence on the course of military events on the Continent of Europe.</p>
        <p>What Was Done. "The groundwork of a war organisation had been created, owing largely to the Prime Minister's foresight, at a time when men talked only of peace. What was the result? Before the declaration of war a number of precaution- ar3r measures were taken without causing panic or commotion by all the departments of State— the Admiralty, the War Office, the Treasury, the Home Office, the Board of Trade, the Post Office, the Board of Customs, and others. In less than a week from the first shock, which was calculated to produce panic in a great demo- cracy living upon the sea, the commercial affairs in the great cities and towns of England began to resume their normal condition.</p>
        <p>"Without excitement or the slightest signs of confusion, every department of State took up its war burden, and without delay orders issued forth mobilising the nation in support of its fighting arm. For the first time for a hundred years the British people were confronted with war by land and sea, and a war which in pros- pect had defied imagination in its probable hor- rors,and they remained undismayed. . .</p>
        <p>"he secret of our preparedness is to be traced to the patient labours of the Committee of Imperial Defence, its Sub-committees, and its small permanent staff directed by Captain Maurice Hankey, C. 8., the successor to Rear- Admiral Sir Charles Ottley, who laid the foundations of the organisation which enabled us to face war in calm confidence. It costr the countrye,' so the Prime Minister has state?., very little more than ...e5.000 (a year) in salaries and expenses.' This body has been the great advisory organisation of the British Govern- , ment in matters of defence, and has been re- sponsible for the co-ordination of all our com- munal activity so as to render us fit to stand the strain of war.</p>
        <p>In Case of War. "Two years ago Mr. Asquith, in a notable speech in the House of Commons, gave the nation some conception of the character of one aspect of the work which was then being quietly performed by this small body, unrecognised, though it is, in our Constitution, and regarded, as it has been since its birth, with no little sus- picion and distrust. Mr. Asquith related that the Committee of Imperial Defence had ap- pointed what was styled a sub-committee for the co-ordination of departmental action at the outbreak of war.' Describing this particular work of the Committee of Imperial Defence, Mr. Asquith added:— 'This sub-committee, which is composed of the principal permanent officials of the various Departments of State, has, after many months of continuous labour, compiled a War Book, We call it a War Book—and it is a book which definitely assigns to each Department—not merely the War Office and the Admiralty, but the Home Office, the Board of Trade, and , every Department of the State—its responsibility for action under every head of war policy. The Departments themselves, in pursuance of the instructions given by the War Book, have drafted all the proclamations, Orders in Coun-</p>
        <p>cil, letters, telegrams, notices, and so forth, which can be foreseen. Every possible provi- sion has been made to avoid delay in setting in force the machinery in the unhappy event of war taking place. It has been thought neces- sary to make this Committee permment in order that these war arrangements may be constantly kept up-to-date.' Result of Readiness.</p>
        <p>" When the cataclysmistic war did come, we were not unready. The War Book had been re- vised from month to month; it had only to be opened and action taken on the lines specified. It was only necessary for the various officials in the frozen or so Departments of State concerned to turn to the War Book and the proclamations, Orders in Council, letters and telegrams flowed forth as from automatic machines. Owing to this businesslike procedure, confidence was soon established, and the British people, convinced by decisive acts that every conceivable eventu- ality had been foreseen and provided for, were able to reap all the blessings which sea power confers upon an island kingdom which is the nerve centre of a maritime Empire: „ .</p>
        <p>"Within a few days of the -declaration of war, Germany, by the influence of the British Fleet-in-being, was divorced from the colonial possessions, on which she had set such store; her shipping was captured or frightened off the seas; her oversea supplies of food and raw material— so essential to her economic health, and never more essential than when engaged in war—were cut off. Though not a gun was fired on board a British battleship, nor a torpedo sent hissing front its tube, the influence of British sea power was immediately effective, in the absence of immediate challenge, sealing the North Sea, commanding, in association with the French Fleet, the Mediterranean, and holding dominion over all the ocean highways of the world. Within less than a week the British people were presented with an impressive picture of the silent pressure of sea power. Sea Power.</p>
        <p>"As the British Army of nearly 400,000 men was placed on a war footing, and the Territorial Force, a quarter of a million strong, was em- bodied, not a word was heard of the peril of invasion. The nation had faith in the Fleet. As the Dominions turned to the task of pre- paring to support the Mother Country, they were embarrassed by no fear of aggression from overseas. From end to end of the Empire it was realised in the hour of supreme crisis that we were a people who lived on and by the sea, and that we could do no ether than place reli- ance on the squadrons and flotillas under the White Ensign. "No one can prophesy the course of future events, but in facing the stupendous issues which have to be decided, we have at least the consoling satisfaction that, in spite of many obstacles thrust in its path of duty by idealists with their heads in the air and pacificist with their hands in their pockets, the nation was not caught unready when the great emergency arose. Before the struggle is over everyone in these islands will assuredly realise the truth that battleships are cheaper than battles.</p>
        <p>The Man. "All men, irrespective of party, will pay homage to the man, above all others, whose keen foresight, cool judgment, and level-headedness contributed to enable us to face the blows of Fate with calm courage. " Under the direction of Mr. Asquith the task of preparation was carried forward when most people believed peace was assured. Whatever the issue," concludes Mr. Hurd, "the British people will never forget the services of the British Prime Minister, the leader of a great democratic party, who proved himself the great- est of all War Ministers the nation has ever honoured."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>MR. JOHN BENNETT DEAD.</head>
        <p>MR. JOHN BENNETT DEAD.</p>
        <p>Born at Fearnhead Eighty-Four Years Ago.</p>
        <p>With regret we record the death of Mr. John Bennett, which took place at half-past four on Tuesday afternoon at his residence, Grange Mount, Padgate. Although he had not been in good health for some months, Mr. Bennett, whose age was 84, passed away quite peacefully and unexpectedly. On Tuesday morning he was 'downstairs and about the house just as usual. Born at Fearnhead in the year 1830, Mr. Ben- nett resided for about thirty years in Padgate, removing at length to Bewsey Hall Farm, upon his marriage to Miss Grange, of Bewsey. That lady's demise occurred five years ago, after the celebration in September, 1908, of their golden wedding. For about forty years the farm was the late Mr. Bennett's home, and at the end of that period he retired from farming and again I took up his residence in Padgate—at Grange Mount, where his father had formerly lived.l Until quite recently Mr. Bennett continued as an active partner of the firm of Bennett Bros.. cattle dealers, of Bruche—a position he had held during the whole of his business life. The deceased gentleman was a convinced Wesleyan Methodist, and had held most of the offices open to laymen whilst a worshipper atl Bewsey-road Church, including that of circuit steward. During this period his home was the rendezvous of very many of the most promi- nent Wesleyan preachers, who always found a hearty welcome there. Mr. Barnett was of a quiet and unobtrusive disposition; he exercised a great influence for good upon all with whom he came in contact. In politics he never took any active part, always preserving an entirely independent view. . _ .</p>
        <p>_ Ife leaves five sons, the sixth and eldest, Mr. William Grange Bennett, having died about seven years ago. The surviving sons are: The Rev. John Grange Bennett, of London, a promi- nent Wesleyan minister; Mr. Joseph Bennett. of Bruche and Paddington; Dr. Bennett, of Padgate; Mr. F. S. Bennett, of London (well known in the motor world); and Mr. Leonard Bennett. Two daughters also are left—Miss Emily Bennett and Mrs. F. W. Flanagan. The interment took place in the Warrington</p>
        <p>Cemetery on Friday afternoon. It was preceded by a service in the Padgate Wesleyan Church, conducted by the Rev. John Gunnell, an old friend of the family.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>DEATH OF IR. T. W. PEARCE.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF IR. T. W. PEARCE.</p>
        <p>Warrington Loses One of Her Best Musicians.</p>
        <p>It is with regret that we have this week to record the death of Mr. Thomas William Pearce, a young but successful musician, who, up to last July, held the post of organist at the Bewsey- road Wesleyan Church, Warrington, and who was well known in local musical circles. Only 24 years of age, the deceased was the son of Mr. Thomas Pearce, of Old Hill, Staffs. As a child; the deceased displayed considerable aptitude for music, • and his talent and tastes in that direction were fostered when, coming to 'Warrington some few years ago, he was intro- duced to the local musical societies and music lovers by his uncle, Mr. W. Hipkiss, of Lodge- lane. His skill, especially as an organist, was soon bruited abroad, and, on account of his youth, when he first took his seat in the organ loft, he was dubbed "the boy organist," which appellation stuck to him till the day of his death. His youth—he was only 16 then—added to his ability made him in great request—for there is always a special attraction about a young prodigy—and soon he was frequently get- ting invitations to play at the various churches in the town and district; invitations which he delighted to accept, and which were the means of enhancing his reputation. A PUPIL OF PROMISE.</p>
        <p>The late Mr. Pearce received his early tuition from Mr. W. H. Payton, now of Bold- street, Warrington, who speedily found in him a pupil of great promise, to assist in whose bringing out was a pleasure and a reward in itself. It was -mainly due to the high opinion held of his abilities by his old music master that when only about 15 years of age the de- ceased was making frequent public appearances,</p>
        <p>whilst, when but a year older, he was given the appointment at Bewsey-road. During the twelve months prior to his stepping into the organ loft at the Wesleyan Church as its regular occupant, he frequently sat on the stool in other churches and chapels as deputy for the ordinary organist.</p>
        <p>- Mr. Pearce studied at the Royal College of Music, Manchester, prior to which he had passed with distinction the preliminary ex- amination of the Trinity College of Music, Lon- don. Whilst at Manchester he pursued his studies with such assiduity and success that he was awarded a Halle Scholarship, a fact which highly delighted his old music master, Dr. Max Meyer. When studying in Cottonopolis Mr, Pearce had charge of the Manchester Grammar School Orchestra, and subsequently upon his return to Warrington be became accompanist to the Warrington Apollo Male Voice Choir, whilst for some time he also acted as conduc- tor of the Stockton Heath Choral Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>BRILLIANT PIANIST.</head>
        <p>A BRILLIANT PIANIST.</p>
        <p>This last year or two Mr. Pearce chiefly de- voted his attention to pianoforte music, of which he was a brilliant executant, his playing being marked by a thorough grasp of technique and the distinct individuality which stamps the master. But though mainly concerned with the piano and the teaching of the same, Mr. Pearce lost none of his old love for the grandest and most difficult of all musical instruments—the sonorous organ. The late Mr. Pearce also took a deep interest in choral work, and was the possessor of a good baritone voice, which was often heard to advan- tage on local concert platforms.</p>
        <p>profound sympathy is felt with the young widow, who, by the way, is herself a polished musician, oneness of taste in regard to the art being the means of bringing them together. Prior to her marriage, which only took place two months ago, Mrs. Pearce was Miss Phyllis Buckley, of Greenfields, near Manchester. _</p>
        <p>- , The funeral took place ht Glasgow on Wednesday.</p>
        <p>HAMILTONISTREET. SCHOOL. Mr. Frank H. Longshaw Appointed Head Master. It was reported at the Education Committee on Monday night that Mr. Frank H. Longshaw, senior assistnt master at Hamilton-street School, had been appointed head master. Mr. Longshaw is the Superintendent of the Commer- cial Institute, and it was recommended that he be permitted to continue as superintendent until the end of the present session. Canon Stevenson said that he was voicing the feeling of the Committee when he said that they recognised the value of the fourteen years' service which Mr. Longshaw had put in as an assistant master in the town, service which had been most efficient. They also had in mind the social service which he had rendered in many ways to the community, and they fol- lowed him with their best wishes.—The ap- pointment .Lhong swaasw, who W confirmed.a mr. , chile School liannWd trained dtealt was educated at Wy yearsßC oaormtl eaarsst leienor ot liCfioße Coll teaegrded. After having served four the school of which hahes He has had a large aes In.issedtanamsonuwtec cmtheoar;it ear:Lc aruisnnt edgrte .or nt to sful experience of evening school work, extending over the last nineteen Years. He formed an evening class at Hamilton-street School, with thirty students, several years before the commencement of the work of the Education Committee. In 1910 he was appointed superintendent of Beamont Branch Technical School, and this year prin- cipal of the Commercial Institute, which has nearly 400 students. He has received excellent reports from His Majesty's Inspectors for even- ing school work. Mr. Longshaw is well known on the social side of school life. He has taken a deep interest in Bulb Show w founded eight years the scholars' sports and an active part in getting enthe is rae3l3l- . 18 cchhiillddrreenwtso Annualgrow plants. and has since been the hon. secretary. He has been the secretary of the Warrington Teachers' Association wh owtlaiesr services w the vices he has rendered his pro- no since 1 membership 905, fAbeemerssailoalnigP, Mr. Longshaw has represented the local teachers at eight conferences of the National Vain of Teachers.</p>
      </div>
