      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD OUR DAUGHTER NATIONS. JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD OUR DAUGHTER NATIONS. JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Another "Pulse" Story.</p>
        <p>When a resident of these parts who was before I the Borough Bench last week mistook a police- I sergeant for a doctor and asked him to feel his I pulse, the inference was that he had tarried not wisely with John Barleycorn. The incident somehow reminded me of an Irishman who had occasion to take counsel 'with his medical adviser, and, when the latter proceeded to test his pulse, protested—"lt's no good feelin' ov me wrist, docthor ; the pain's not there at on—it's in me hid entoirely !"</p>
        <p>Mistaken Neighbourly Kindness. The e3tperience of the Warrington gentleman above .Tcferred to, and especially the "Inven- tory" which was also the subject of a para- graph in the last issue, recalled another inci- dent for which lam indebted to a relative across the "herring pond." It is narrated that a good Samaritan, passing an apartment house in the "wee sma' hoors ayant the twal'," noticed a drunken fellow leaning against the doorway and with much difficulty helped him up to the .second floor. Rather than face an irate wife who might, perhaps, take him for a companion more at fault than her spouse, he opened the first door he came to and pushed the limp figure , in. The good Samaritan groped his way down- stairs again. As he was passing through the vestibule he was able to make out the dim out- line of another man, apparently in worse con- dition than the first one. He, too, said he lived on the second floor, and was assisted in like manner, being pushed into the same door as before. As the good Samaritan again reached the front, he dilerned the shadow of a third man, evidently worse than either of the other two. He was about to approach him when the object of his solicitude lurched out into the street and threw himself into the arms of a passing policeman. "For Heaven's sake, officer," he gasped, "protect me from this man. He's done nothing all night long but carry me upstairs an' throw me down th' elevator shaft."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Adulteration.</head>
        <p>Adulteration.</p>
        <p>Milk is still the most adulterated of all foods (and that is b. great deal to say), so that the activity of the responsible officials in taking samples of all and sundry is wholly commend- able. Milk has been found to be adulterated, not merely with water, but with boric acid and colouring matter, and vessels in which it was conveyed have been shown to have been im- properly sterilised and unclean, thus spreading tuberculosis, typhoid, and other evils. More- -oer, the lactometer test is authoritively pro- nounced absolutely valueless; and the worst of it all is that ailing infants with weakened vitality are the chief sufferers from these criminal practices. Milk invariably prepon- derates in the "nourishing diet" commonly ordered by doctors in attendance on such cases, and if it be impure, of course it renders the treatment worse than useless, the development of alarming symptoms being traceable thereto. As to adulteration in general, it is to be borne in mind that the mania for cheapness on the part of the purchaser, as well as the desire to make money on the part of the trader, has much to do with it. As a specialist observed in an article on the subject a while ago, it is all dons so cleverly that prohibition seems useless, and when existing laws are put in force some eminent expert is always able to prove it is all for our benefit. There was a comparatively recent case in which a provision dealer was summoned for selling butter adulterated with boracic preservative to the extent of 0.9 per cent, of boric acid, to the prejudice of the pur- chaser. Counsel for the prosecuting authority was constrained to admit that, although the Butter and Margarine Act gave the Local Gov- ernment Board power to make regulations, the regulation was not made; whereupon, of course, ' it was promptly submitted that the defendant could not be convicted of breaking a regulation which did not exist, and the summons was dis- missed. As one commentator well and truly remarks, it is the poor on whom the bulk of adulteration evils fall. Boric acid, howevttr, is strictly impartial. Its use in sausages, cream, &amp;c., insure its ill effects falling on rich and poor alike. Why can we not follow the Danish idea and abolish it altogether? Sugar and salt are good enough preservatives, and things which are so advanced in putrefaction that these will not avail have no business to be kept at all. After all, it is pleasant to think that we have in our midst many honest traders who would as' soon cut off a hand or pluck out an eye as knowingly be a party to such uncleanness as is above indicated. Gardening.</p>
        <p>The reference to the beautiful station garden at Moore set me thinking of wonderful trans- formations effected in many Manchester back- yards since Mrs. Grindon began to interest her- self in such things there, and to encourage tram- waymen and others to cause these former wilder- nesses to rejoice and blossom as the rose. It may seem late in the year to introduce such matter; but Bacon points out that with due ,management we may have some freshness and beauty in our gardens all the year round, and evidence of this happily accumulates in this age of advancing science. As one expert put it some time ago, there is spring in the soil always. Efeat and cold are only relative terms which im- ply a little, more or less, of the former, and there is always enough heat to quicken some kinds of vegetation in our gardens.</p>
        <p>Warrington's Loyalty. "Les Assassins "—as the poor Belgians now I habitually term those brutal oppressors of the I weak, the 'self-styled modern Attila and his Huns I —have this week added to the Appalling lest of their crimes against humanity and civilisation ; ' and I find it an immense relief and satisfaction to turn from these distressing considerations and to think (although I may not now write at I length) of the very substantial and worthy con- tribution made by this district toward the over. I throw of the insufferable Prussian military ' tyranny which threatened to ride roughshod over Europe and trample the boasted liberties of its freedom-loving democracies. Warrington may well be proud of her valiant and heroic sons of whom some have already done conspicuous service with the Expeditionary Force, while more are bravely anticipating and qualifying for like places of honour in the fighting line. The country is well assured of a steady succes- sion of such fine fellows, and we stay-at-homes ought indeed to be grateful and to do our duty to those whom they h&amp;ve left to our care. Curious Slips.</p>
        <p>It was surely an error in Mr. Birrell's appreciation which (probably it has been cor- rected in later editions) prefaced Mr. P. L. Parker's abridgement of Wesley's Journal. The passage in question read: " Eight thousand miles was his annual record for many a long year, during each of which he seldom preached less frequently than five thousand times "—or 13 to 14 sermons daily! While on the subject, I may supplement a recent paragraph by the following miscellaneous lot :—Dealing Nvith standing literary errors of the world, the. " Book Monthly" observed that there is one in the " Te Deum "—an ancient blunder which goes OR. It lies in the words, " Make them to be numbered with thy saints," which was so ren- dered when manuscript copies of the "To Deum" gave place to printed books. The medieeval Latin word used was "numerari," "to be num- bered," while the word which should actually have been used was " munerari," "to be re- warded." All prayer-books would be printing that to-eta?, and congregations would be singing , _ it, were it not tr,x. a 15th century printer's error. Poor oldprinter ! But, as I remarked the other week, he is by no means he only sinner of the kind; indeed, he often suffers for sins not his own. To hasten to a close, how's this for an epitaph</p>
        <p>"Erected to the memory f William Tucker, °- Accidentally shot. as a mark of affection by hig wife." "Cleanliness is next to Godliness," and ticularly "God tempers the wind to the 8113:4; lamb," are by many supposed to be Scripture passages. On the other hand, Miss Rea per. trates this in one of her books: "Truly, as saith St- Augustine, 'the letter killeth, but the spirit giveth life' "; and a' well-known writer opened a magazine article with "George Eliot has nobly said, 'The kingdom of God is within YOU. "</p>
        <p>Every an may have his price, but he shouldn't squander too much in adverti3ing it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>How They Rally in the Time of</head>
        <p>How They Rally in the Time of</p>
        <p>Need.</p>
        <p>GIFTS OF MONEY AND SUPPLIES.</p>
        <p>The Government 'have issued a White Paper containing the text of the cable- grams which have been exchanged between the Colonial Secretary and the Governors-General and Governors regarding the offers of naval and military assistance made by the Dominions. The following is a summary of the offers re- ceived : . .</p>
        <p>Canada.—H.M.'s C.S. Niobe and Rainbow for general service; a division of 22,000 men of all arms; an infantry battalion of ex-regulars, raised and equipped by Mr. Hamilton Gault, and units of 1,000 men each raised and equipped by Manitoba, New Brunswick, and Calgary respectively. Australia.—Commonwealth Navy placed at dis- posal of the Admiralty; an expeditionary force of 20,000 men, with the addition of an infantry brigade and a light horse brigade, comprising 6,383 men. New Zealand.—One mounted rifle brigade; one field artillery brigade, and one infantry bri- gade; 200 Maoris for service in Egypt. South Africa.—Union Defence Force to replace Imperial troops in South Africa. Newfoundland.—Expeditionary Force of 500 ' men; Naval Reserve raised to 1,000 men, some of whom will be used to complete H.M.S. Niobe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>MONEY AND OTHER GIFTS.</head>
        <p>MONEY AND OTHER GIFTS.</p>
        <p>In another White Paper are set forth details of the gifts of money, foodstuffs, and other sup- plies from tho Dominions and Colonies. These are set forth beneath:—</p>
        <p>Canada.-98,000,0901b. of flour; Alberta, 500,000 bushels of oats; Nova Scotia, 100,000 tons of coal; Quebec, 4,000,0001 b. of cheese; Prince Ed- ward Island, 100,000 bushels of oats and quan- tities of cheese and hay; Ontario, 250,000 bags of flour; Saskatchewan, 1,500 horses, valued at 250,000 dollars; New Brunswick, 100,000 bushels of potatoes; Manitoba, 4,900,0001 b. of flour; British Columbia, 25,000 cases of canned salmon. Australia.—(Messrs. Cullen and Wallace), 1,000 gallons of port wine; Queensland, 5,6001 b. but- ter, 16,2201 b. bacon, 550 cases of pressed beef. 9,6001 b. condensed milk, 50 cases of condensed milk "contributed for the benefit of children of the Empire," and 2.4 tons arrowroot. Barbados.—X2o,ooo from the Colonial Treasury. British Guiana.-1,000 tons of sugar. Falkland 151and5.—..22.250 voted by Legislative Council (equal to £1 per head of the popula- tion), and £750 collected privately for the Prince of Wales' Fund. Jamaica.--Sugar. Leeward Islands (St. Kitts and Nevis).—.2s,ooo to the Prince of Wales' Fund. Mauritius.-1,000.00011). sugar for the Army; 1,000,00011). sugar for the Navy. Southern Rhodesia (Agricultural Union).— Maize.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>INDIAN LOYALTY.</head>
        <p>INDIAN LOYALTY.</p>
        <p>Princes as Volunteers.</p>
        <p>The India Office publishes further particulars of the remarkable offers of help made by Indian notables. The Secretary of State for India communicates the following: Since the receipt of the summary of offers of service, gifts, B:c., which have been made in India in connection with the war, the Government of Madras has reported that the Madras War Fund is prepared to supply, in addition to the horses previously mentioned, a fully-equipped hospital ship named Madras, with 300 beds, for the use of the Indian Expedi- tionary Force. The field hospital, previously described as the gift of the Delhi Medical Association, has been presented on behalf of all Indian Musulmans. Other offers have been received, so that the list is now as follows :</p>
        <p>His Highness the Aga Khan: In addition to directing the community of which he is the spiritual head to place their personal services and resources unreservedly at the disposal of Government, has volunteered to serve as a private in any infantry regiment of the Indian Expeditionary Force.- His Highness the Aga Khan is the spiritual head of the Klioja Mos- lems, numbering some millions, and the recog- nised temporal leader of over 60 millions of Indian Mahomedans. He is at present staying in London, having come here from Zanzibar at the outbreak of the war.</p>
        <p>The Raja of Akaikot: Personal service in the field. His Highness the Gaekwar of Baroda: All his troops and resources. .</p>
        <p>Their Highnesses. the Maharaja and the Maharani Maji Sahiba of Bharatpur: (1) The whole resources of their State: (2) two motor- cars and a chauffeur, ii;jth all expenses; (3) Rs. 2,000 to the Indian Relief Fund. His Highness has offered also personal service in the field. His Highness the Raja of Pudukota:, "All I possess"; expresses his anxiety to serve in any capacity. Has placed his motor car at the dis- posal of Government, and is returning to India to raise, subject to approval, a regiment of his subjects to release a Regular regiment. Mir Ghillam Ali Khan of Khairpur: Personal service in the field. Sahibzada Nasir Ali Khan of Rampur: Per- sonal service. The British-Indian residents in this country of every class and creed have been forward with loyal and generous offers of personal services and help. The Secretary of State for India has received the following additional information from the Viceroy regarding the offers of service, ST., which have reached him in connection with the war:— His Highness ,the -Maharajah of Idar has been selected for active service. .</p>
        <p>Further contingents of Imperial Service Troops have been accepted from three States, making 15 States in all, namely :—Alwar, Bharatpur, Bikaner, Faridkot, Gwalior, Hyder- abad, Indere, Jaipur; Jind, Jodhpur, Kapur- thala, Kashmir, Mysore, • Patiala, Rampur. Imperial Service contingents from Bahawal- pur, Bhavnagar, Bhopal, Idar, Junagadh, Khairpur, Maier Kotla, Navanagar, Sirmur, and Tehri will be accepted later if need arises. A joint offer from Dhar, Barwatii, Ali Rajpur, and Thabua, who maintain no Imperial Service Troops, is under consideration. His Highness the Maharajah of Gwalior has also given £22,000 for motor cars and transport, and £5,000 for relief of Belgian sufferers. Their, Highnesses the Rajah and Rani of Dhar have contributed to His Royal Highness the Prince of Wales' Fund.</p>
        <p>Offers of horses have beign made by the Chiefs of Baroda, Bhopal, Dhar, Jodhpur, Mysore, Patiala,</p>
        <p>Loyal offers of service have been received from the tribes of Knrram and the Swat Valley and from the Sam Ranizai, who are ready to raise corps of Frontier _tribesmen if desired. _</p>
        <p>dffers have also been received from the Feuda- tory State of Amb, from the Bhutan Mahara- jah, and from the Arab Chiefs in .the Aden Hinterland.</p>
      </div>
