      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>TILE MCVAEINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 26, 1914.</head>
        <p>TILE MCVAEINER, SATURDAY, SEPTEMBER 26, 1914.</p>
        <p>GOOD NEWS FOR THE BLIND. CHULAR BROTHERS, INDIAN EYE SPECIALISTS,</p>
        <p>CURE ALL DISEASES OF THE EYE. 42, PARKER STREET, WARRINGTON. 7, RODNEY-STREET, WIGAN.</p>
        <p>EXTRACTS FROM LOCAL TESTIMONIALS. Miss H. Widdows, of 14, Harvey-street, Gol- borne, writes as follows :—" I have been totally blind for 20 years with a skin over the right eye, and the left one was very badly affected. I have been under other doctors without any result, but after your treatment I can now read the newspapers without glasses." Mr. W. Enwright, 283, Thelwall-lane, War- rington, writes :—" Thanks for wonderful cure you have made of my daughter's eyes. 'She suffered with inflammation and a skin growing over for about 6 months; tried other means without success. After 2 months of your treat- ment her sight is completely restored." Mr. W. Holmes, of 10, Jockey-street, Warring- ton, writes:—" I thank you for the great benefit received from your treatment. I was gradually going blind, and could not tell the difference between a penny and a two-shilling piece. I am now able to work without glasses, after just one week of your treatment." Mr. M. J. Kindon, of 69, Dudley-street, War- dington, writes :—"lt is about 3 years since my eyesight failed; although I wore glasses, no im- provement followed. I was advised to see you, and now my eyesight is almost restored. The only regret I have is that I did not consult you sooner."</p>
        <p>Mrs. Mercer, of 33, Bank-street, Widnes, writes:'—" I have been suffering with my eyes for a number of years. After a few weeks of your treatment I can see quite plainly." Mr. and Mrs. Bennett, of 90, Ellesmere-street, Warrington, writes:—" My son suffered for 7 years with failing sight, and could not see a yard in front of him, but now we owe our sin- cere gratitude to you for the ,beneficent treat- ment he has received." Mrs. Jackson, of 118, Battersby-lane, War- rington. writes :—" My boy has suffered frdm short sight for 3. years; has undergone special treatment, but derived little benefit until I brought him to you. Now he can see all right again." Mrs. Wooley, of 44, Plumpton-street, Sankey Bridges, Warrington, writes :—" My little girl's eye was bad about 6 years with very bad blains —inflammation and skin growing over it. After 6 weeks of you remedy it is entirely cured." Mr. S. Walton, of Fielding Green, Aston, near Northwich, writes :—" I suffered with cataract, which caused headache, for a long time; after a course of your treatment am considerably better." Mrs. Cairns, of 19, Garner-street, Warrington, writes:—" I suffered from failing sight and bad headache for many years. After a few months of your wonderful treatment am 'quite better." THESE ARE A FEW FROM HUNDREDS OF UNSOLICITED TESTIMONIALS. WARRINGTON CONSULTING HOURS: Monday, Wednesday and Friday, 10.30 a.m. to 12 noon, 4 p.m. to 7 p.m. WIGAN CONSULTING HOURS: Tuesday. Thursday and Saturday, 4 p.m. to 7 p.m., at 7, Rodney-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>BOROUGH Pomcg COURT.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Councillor _H. Roberts in the chair), Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes, T. Locker, and</p>
        <p>J. Hepherd. Boys Stole the Pigeons.—Jelin. Goulden (16), 22, Wellington-street, and Arthur Hart (12), 11, Porter-street, were summoned for stealing two pigeons, value is. 6d. the property of Peter Turner, 28, Brick-street.—Superintendent Wil- liamson said the boy Turner showed his three pigeons to the other two boys on Sunday after- noon. About 9.45 on Sunday night the cote was discovered opened and the three pigeons were missing. The boy Goulden had two pigeons at his home, but the other one could not be found. Both boys denied seeing three pigeons, but one of them produced a key with which he ad- mitted unlocking the door of the cote. They both admitted taking ,two pigeons. -The lads were ordered to pay 2s. 6d. each towards the costs of the case and were put under the Pro- bation Officer for six months. Too Old for the Army.,--Edward fooling, lodging at 27, Barbauld-street, who had been remanded from the previous Court on a charge of drunkenness and disorderly behaviour, on account of his expressing a desire to join the Army, was again put into the dock. On the previous occasion he stated that he was 42 years of age and had previously been in the Army.— The Magistrates' Clerk explained that he had been rejected because he was 52 years of age.— The Superintendent: They cannot accept him on account of his age and•his record.—Prisoner: I might get away if I had a chance.—The Clerk: What regiment were you in before?—Prisoner: The South Lancashires.—The Superintendent : He also states he has been in a cavalry regi- ment.—The Chairman: Seeing that you have been under arrest since Sunday we will let you go.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. A. J: Jolley (in the chair), Dr. Naden, Mr. W. Roberts and Mr. W. T. Williams.</p>
        <p>Costly Midnight Drive.—Harold W. Laxton,; 100 a, Lincoln-road, Peterborough, was sum- moned for having driven a motor car without having a licence.—Defendant did not appear, but the Magistrates' Clerk read a letter from him, in which he stated that on the day in question, at 12.45 ,a.m., he was travelling from Liverpool to Manchester with a Mr. Gray, who had arrived from New York on the Campania. Mr. Gray was rather afraid to drive, as it was very foggy in places, and be (defendant) con- sented to do so, not knowing that his driving licence had expired. He would \plead guilty and pay the fine.—Supt. Williamson said they had no reason to disbelieve the statement of defend- ant, who had acted quite straightforwardly in the matter.—The Bench ordered that the de- fendant should contribute .21 to the poor box. "Terror of the Neighbourhood."—Mary Har- ris, 19, Beswick-street, who made her 21st ap- pearance, was summoned for having used obscene language at the rear of Beswick-street. —P.C. Ballard said the defendant was behaving like a mad woman and calling everyone in the street foul names. He had previously received strong complaints about the woman, who had been described as "the terror of theneighbour- hood."—Defendant said the people would not leave her alone. They were always calling after her and saying they would send her back again to where she had been. The children also shouted after her.—Defendant was committed to prison for 14 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>FRIDAY.</head>
        <p>FRIDAY.</p>
        <p>Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Bolton, Pierpoint and Smethurst, and Messrs.</p>
        <p>A.J. jolley W. Furness, S. Wright and G.</p>
        <p>Pilling. threatened to " Knife" a Policeman.—George Grayton, of no fixed abode, was charged with having ben drunk and disorderly.---Detective Summers said he found the prisoner lying across a footpath in Church-street, and when requested he refused to go away. He caught hold of witness, threatening to "knife" him,. and saying, "You are only a 'special.'" He put his hand in his pocket to get hold of a knife (produced), hut it was wrested from him. He used shock- ing_ language and became violent. —Prisoner</p>
        <p>said he was very sorry and did not intend to do the officer any harm. He was in the Afghan and South African Wars, and was, at one time, stationed at Orford Barracks.---He ,was sent to prison for one month, with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>CRESCENT LAWN TENNIS CLUB.</head>
        <p>CRESCENT LAWN TENNIS CLUB.</p>
        <p>Racquet Wielders Help Belgian Refugees. The playing season of the Crescent Lawn Ten- nis Club, Latchford, closed on Saturday with an exceptionally good attendance of members and friends, owing to the fact that it had been decided to give the gross proceeds of the tea to one of the war funds. This decision, made pos- sible by the generosity of one of the lady mem- bers, resulted in Al 14s. being collected, which amount has been handed over to the local treas- urer of the Belgian Refugee Relief Fund. A vote of thanks to the lady helpers was pro- posed by Mr. Tickell and seconded by Mr. Walker. Cheers were given for the Army and Navy, and the proceedings closed with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>t',pIT,IcT PfW5 ij</head>
        <p>t',pIT,IcT PfW5 ij</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH</p>
        <p>Without a Red Light—At Chester on Satur- day, Robert Holland, 47, Bedford-street, Stock- ton Heath, was summoned for failing to have a lighted red rear lamp attached to his motor cycle and side-car when motoring in Hoole on the night of September 9th.—He was ordered to pay the costs.</p>
        <p>The Training Corps.—The Stockton Heath Training Corps, which is now nearly a hundred strong, held its first drill on Monday evening in the Church Institute. The men present a very smart appearance, a fact upon which they have been complimented.</p>
        <p>Children's Concert for Red Cross Funds.—A. children's concert was recently held at Mrs. Baskerville's, 14, Egerton-street, Stockton Heath, in aid of the funds of the Red Cross Society. The programme consisted of songs, dances and pianoforte solos, which were sup- plied by the following children: The Misses L. M. H. M. Rabbitt. E. Hughes, L. Melling, E. Wilkinson and M. Robinson, and Master G. Baskerville. The members of the audience were children, and the conert resulted in the sum of 10s. being handed over to Miss Broadbent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>The New Gas Manager.—Mr. W. H. West, the new gas works manager, has entered upon his duties with the Council.</p>
        <p>Watching the Water.—Lymm's water supply is watched night and day to prevent its being tampered with in any way by maliciously dis- posed persons. At night two watchmen patrol the works and all day long Boy Scouts keep a wary look-out.</p>
        <p>Football Match Abandoned.—The Lymm Shop Assistants' Football team should have jour- neyed to Marple last Wednesday to fulfil a Man- chester Wednesday League engagement, but the match had to be abandoned through the failure of Marple to raise a team. The Lymm eleven are without a fixture for Wednesday next.</p>
        <p>Evening Classes.—Under the auspices of the Cheshire County Council the Lymm Administra- tive Sub-committee for Education are organis- ing classes in cookery and dressmaking. The first lesson in cookery will be given on October 13 and in dresSmaking on the following Friday. The teacher of the first-named subject will be Mrs. Harrison and of the latter Miss Chappel. Intending students are requested to communi- cate with Mr. Wm. Mullard, at the Council offices.</p>
        <p>Homes Wanted for Belgian Refugees.—Doubt- less there are kind-hearted persons in the Lymm district who would like to take their share, along %with the people in other parts of the country, of affording hospitality to some of the unfortunate Belgian refugees, who, rendered homeless and, in many cases, penniless by the war, are now flocking to this country in con- siderable numbers. The local Relief Fund Com- mittee are undertaking the task of finding homes for a number of these unhappy people, and per- sons desiring of offering shelter and hospitality are requested o send in their names to Mr., H. T. Mercer. or Mr. William Mullard, the hon. secretaries of the local Relief Fund, or to Mr. T. F. W. Dixon, the hon. treasurer.</p>
        <p>harvest Celebrations.—Harvest festival ser- vices were held in the Primitive Methodist Church on Sunday. The Rev. R. W. Ferguson preached appropriate and excellent sermons, both morning and evening, and at the latter service the choir gave a fine rendering of the anthem, "The Lord is loving" Simper). On Monday evening a service of praise was sung by the choir, the subject being "Royal Oak." The connective readings were given by Mr. G. L. Welford. The principals were the Misses B. Cress, D. Cross, and M. Broady; and Messrs. G. W. Cross, H. Clare, and E. Appleton, all of whom acquitted themselves creditably. The concerted numbers were admirably sung as oe- gards time and expression. The conductor was Mr. Edwards, whilst Miss Shore made an ex- cellent accompanist. The decorations were the prettiest seen in the church for some years. The congregations were good throughout the festival and the collections in advance of last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>HIGH LEGH.</head>
        <p>HIGH LEGH.</p>
        <p>Bottle as a Weapon.—At the Lymm Police Station on Monday morning, before Mr. A. Watkin and Mr. Ridgway, William Venables, a farm labourer, of High Legh, was charged with assaulting Martin Garvey, an Irish farm labourer.—The evidence showed that at about 10.30 on Sunday night Garvey was going towards his home, when Venables rushed at him and struck him with a bottle. The injured man made his way to the Rev. Mr. Surridge's house, and Dr. Leonard Thorpe, of Lymm, was called to render assistance. The police were commu- nicated with and prisoner was arrested.—A fine of 20s. and costs was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>GRAPPENHAL L.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHAL L.</p>
        <p>Harvest Thanksgiving Services.—Harvest thanksgiving services were held at the Parish Church on Sunday, the preacher in the maim- ing being the Rector (the Rev. H. V. Pigot), and in the evening the Rev. E. Gunner. The sacred edifice was without the customary deco- rations, but this did not prevent the attendance of large congregations to give thanks for the ingathering of a, bountiful harvest. The choir rendered the anthems "Ye shall dwell in the land" and "All Thy works praise Thee, 0 Lord," in a very creditable manners the soloists being Messrs. T. Leigh (bass) and E. Leigh (tenor). Special psalms and appropriate hymns were also sung. Mr. W. A. Wood presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>PRESTON BROOK.</head>
        <p>PRESTON BROOK.</p>
        <p>Farm Fire Causes £l5O Damage—On Tues- day afternoon a fire broke out at the farm of Mr. J. Melbourne, Aston Heath, near Preston Brook, but the strenuous efforts of the Wil- derspool Fire Brigade prevented the danger spreading. ' The Brewery Brigade, with ,their new motor engine, were the first to arrive on the scene and they at once turned their atten- tion to a 50-ton haystack, which was well alight. The burning stack was the centre of five ricks in the yard, and the situation was extremely diffic4lt, flut,, fortunately, only a slight wind was blowing. When the Warring- ton Brigade arrived, in charge of Sergeant Henry Cook, and with the steam engine Major, they found that Supt. Croughton's men had the outbreak well in hand. However, it was not until eight o'clock the following morn- ing that the Brewery Brigade were able to leave I the scene. The damage, which is roughly esti- mated at' 150 is, we understand, covered by insurance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>DARESBURY.</head>
        <p>DARESBURY.</p>
        <p>Voters' Lists Revised.—The annual task of revising the voters' lists for Daresbury, Preston Brook, Moore and other parishes in the district was undertaken at the Sessions Room at Dares- bury on Saturday by Mr. Clement Elphinstone Lloyd. The Liberals were represented by Mr. W. Clifie, and the Conservatives by Mr. James Duckwortll.—The Assistant Overseers present were Messrs. A. Booth (Daresbury), A. De Prez (Moore), A. Clare (Preston Brook), W. Hewitt (Hatton and Higher Whitley), Wilkinson (Seven Oaks and Lower Whitley), T. H. Howard (Antrobus) and J. Wright (Newton-by-Dares- bury). The business was of a formal charac- ter.—At the conclusion of his brief task the Revising Barrister was thanked by the two Political agents for the painstaking and cour- teous manner in which he had conducted the business, and, in reply, he congratulated the agents upon the thoroughness with which they had done their work. During his long experi- ence, he said, he had never held a better or quieter Revision Court.—Thanks were also ten- dered to the Assistant 'Overseers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Scholastic Success.—The list of county schol- arship winners just issued by the Lancashire Education. Committee includes the name of E. R. Brown, of Penketh, whose proficiency in secondary school subjects entitles him to £6O per annum for three years.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Harvest Festival.—There were large congregations at the special services held in the Wesleyan Chapel on Sunday in celebration of the safe ingathering of the harvest. The building was decorated with grain, fruit and vegetables, but there was a smaller show of flowers than usual, the recent rough weather having done much damage among the blooms. A feature of the decorations was a huge loaf of bread. The Rev. H. G. Dixon was the special preacher at the evening service. He tpok as his subject the )vheat seed, which, after being ' planted and apparently dying, burst forth again into new life, first producing the blade and then the precious ear.</p>
        <p>Druids' Bowling Main.—The annual bowling main organised in connection with the local lodges of the Ancient Order of Druids took place on the green at the Limerick Hotel, Burton- wood, on Saturday, in; stormy weather, which militated against the attendance and the num- ber of entries, both falling below those of kre- vious years. Lack of numbers was made up for by some close and exciting games, the " Greenall " Challenge Cup being won, after a keen struggle, by Bro. Ernest Dumbill (Penketh United Lodge), with Bro. J. Campbell second and Bros. J. Hall and A. Goulden third and fourth respectively. After the games the bowlers 'sat down to an excellent tea at the Unicorn Hotel. The tables having been cleared, the Grand Master of the "Lord Elliot" Lodge presented the cup to the winner, and the rest of the evening was given over to harmony.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>GLAZEBROOK.</head>
        <p>GLAZEBROOK.</p>
        <p>Primitive Methodist Anniversary.—The anni- versary of the opening of the Primitive Metho- dist Church was celebrated on Sunday, when Mr. Hoyle, of Warrington, preached specially appropriate sermons in the afternoon and even- ing. Music suitable to the occasion was ren- dered by the Choir and the Sunday school chil- dren, Mr. J. Hudson Anducting. Collections taken at the services realised the substantial total of over £3l.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>POTATOES FOR THE POOR.</head>
        <p>POTATOES FOR THE POOR.</p>
        <p>Runcorn Gentleman's Welcome</p>
        <p>THE NEW RATE.</p>
        <p>Gratuity Instead of Superannuation,</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held on Tuesday at the Dutton Workhouse, Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun., presiding. AN OFFICER'S HEALTH.</p>
        <p>The Chairman drew the attention of the mem- bers to the fact that their respected friend, Mr. Thomas Cooke, was again with them that day. They were all very pleased to see him— (hear, hear)—and they trusted that it was only a question of a short time before he would be restored to the very best of health. He was to have undergone a second operation that day, but preferred to postpone it and attend that meeting. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Cooke thanked the Board for their kind- ness and patience with him during his illness. He had gone through his first operation suc- cessfully and had to wait three months for the second. He had postponed going that day in order to thank the Board for their patience, and to ask them to bear with him a little longer. While he had been away he had been doing what he could to assist his son, and, be- tween them, they had managed very nicely.</p>
        <p>A WELCOME GIFT. Mr. Cooke mentioned that in his work of ad- ministering to the poor in his district he was pleased to acknowledge the kindness of Mr. Richard Moore, of Runcorn, who had generously placed a small field of potatoes at his disposal. The poor were able to take a note to the gar- dener and return with a good store of potatoes. There was, however, a time when potatoes had to be taken from the ground, and in conse- quence the potatoes had been sold and the amount realised, -t3 65., had been handed over to him (Mr. Cooke). At these times, when there was so much distress, many eases were referred to the relieving officer which he was 'not able to relieve in the ordinary course, and such a gift was very acceptable. The Chairman said that it was very gratify- ing that they had gentlemen in Runcorn like Mr. Richard Moore to take such an interest in the poor. His kindness and goodness of heart were very much appreciated. He (the Chair- man) moved that Mr. Moore be accorded a vote of thanks.</p>
        <p>ThiS wa:s,carried unanimously. PRESENTS FOR THE CHILDREN. The Children's, Home Committee recorded their thanks to Mr. Stafford, of Dutton, and Mr. Mounfield, Barnton, for gifts of fruit, and to Mrs. Armitage for a parcel of books. In moving the confirmation of the minutes, which were adopted, Mr. Wood said that the Master now reported that the electrical Plant was working satisfactorily.</p>
        <p>A NEW NURSE. The Visiting -Committee gave permission to Captain Hanson to hold a mission at-the Work- house 'on October 14th. Miss Hilda Rose,. of Barnton, was appointed as assistant nurse at a salary of .225 per annum, with rations and allowances.</p>
        <p>The minutes were adopted. THE VACANT RELIEVING OFFIGERSHIP. The Clerk presented the special report of the Finance Committee dealing with the appoint- ment of a successor-to Mr. Be Prez, as relieving officer for the Budworth district. It was re- commended that subject to Mr. Harry War- burton relinquishing the position of registrar of births, marriages and deaths, and collector to the Union in the Budworth district, Mr. James Pearson, of Frodsham, be appointed re- lieving officer for Budworth district, at a salary of .1100 a year, and Mr. Harry Warburton be appointed reliving officer for the Frodsham district, at a salary of ~tlOO a year. The com- mittee also recommended that the three reliev- ing officers be appointed collectors to the Guar- dians for their own areas, and they be paid ten per cent. on the amounts of the returns. Mr. Linaker, sen., inquired how much per cent. the Guardians had paid the collector. The Chairman: Twenty! This is ayeduction of ten per cent. We think that the relieving officers have time to do that duty. Mr. Woodward said that the unfortunate re- tirement of Mr. de Prez had opened up an avenue whereby the Board could now secure some reduction of expnnditure in this direction, a thing which•many of them had long consid- ered necessary. As they knew, the work of their three- relieving officers of reecnt years had been very much 'cut down. In the Budworth district, for example, Mr. Be Prez had only been distributing something like £l6 in a fort- night, and he had been receiving a salary—well deserved by him; he (the speaker) had not a word to say about that—of £l2O. In the Frod- sham district they found very nearly the same thing happening. Mr. Pearson had been dis- tributing .tlf?, in a fortnight and receiving £lOO a year. On the face of it, therefore, it did appear quite time to think of cutting down expOnditure in this department. The commit- tee were absolutely unanimous in making the recommendations. It would mean a reduction of .1:16 a year in the salary that had been paid to the officer for the Budworth district, and a saving of .t 25 paid to the collector and ~tlO to the registrar, so that there would be, in the change, an all-round saving of something like -t45. They were not disposed to interfere with Mr. Cooke at Runcorn; they hoped he might live long to serve them and would enjoy the best of good health to do it; but they thought that by this change the work could well be covered by the two officers without making any</p>
        <p>new ;appointment. Mr. Warburton Nv:is an officer of the Board, and when notice was for- mally given he (Mr. Woodward) did not think there would be any opposition. Be uas a prpved man and well qualified for the verk would have to perform. Mr. Morris said that when the Frodsham district relieving officership Was last vacant Mr. Warburton applied and he gave him all his support, and he now fully endorsed all that Mr. Woodward had said. But, apart from that, this was a public appointment, and as such ought to be advertised and thrown open. The Board could still appoint Mr. Warburton if he was the best candidate. The Chairman: This is rather different! You see they are both officers under the Board al- ready. Mr. Morris: They are not both relieving officers.</p>
        <p>The Chairman: True; but one is and the other is the next thing to it; he is already registrar of births, marriages and deaths. Mr. Mclntyre: It is relieving one and assist- ing the other. M. Linaker considered it would be a piece of folly to advertise the position—getting gentle- men to send iii their applications, when the Board had their minds made up! Mr. Gibbs said that it had occurred to him that Mr. Pearson only received £BO a year when he was appointed and he had to wait two years before he got .2100. Mr. Woodward had spoken about &lt;RI2O being paid to Mr. De Prez to col- lect A:l6, and at Frodsham £lOO to Mr. Pearson for collecting .tl2, and he suggested that that was a point worthy of consideration. It w)uld be farcical to advertise the position and get good men to come there in the hope of being successful when the minds of the Guardians were made up. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward said that if Mr. Warburton had been a man just getting into harness he would have appreciated Mr. Gibbs' point of view, but seeing that the gentleman named had served the Board some time as an officer, he (the speaker) did not see any reason for cutting down the salary. Mr. Gibbs: I quite accept that explanation. Mr. Woodward formally gave notice that a fortnight hence he would move that Mr. Pear- son be appointed relieving officer, registrar and collector for the Budworth district. THE CONTRACTS.</p>
        <p>The Contracts Committee recommended the acceptance of the following tenders :—Grocer- ies, Kydd and Co.; bread, Lawless; butchery, Hancock; fuel, Taylor, Frith and Co.; coke, Runcorn Gas Co.; wine and spirits, Rowson; drapery, Lee and Clark; clothing, P. R. Wright; tobacco, Thomas. Runcorn ; oil and colours, J. White, Widnes; brushes, Peers and Albrton; earthenware, E. Sherratt, Runcorn; conveyances, Booth, Runcorn; boots, J. Buzley, Runcorn; drysaltery, J. H. Weston, Runcorn; artisan labour, T. K. Collinge, Runcorn.</p>
        <p>The recommendations were approved. HALF-YEARLY ESTIMATES.</p>
        <p>The Clerk submitted a statement as to the estimated receipts and expenditure for theetalf- year ending March 31st next. It set forth the 4 fact that the total amount to be provided was put at £18,767. Included in that figure was a sum of £6,272 on account of common fund charges, against which was set off a total of receipts estimated at £3,020, leaving £3,252 to be provided under this head. The county rate charge was put at £13,792, and contingencies, and the amount required to meet the expenses of the subsequent half-year before a further call is made, at £3,790. Deducting the probable balance on September 30th of £3,192, the com- mon fund expenses and amount of county rate to be provided would total £17,642, to which had to be added £1,125 in respect of special education, making the grand total to be pro- vided £18,767. The assessable value of the Union is £260,193, and a rate of is. 4d. in the would produce £17,346. Answering Mr. Kaye, the Clerk said the rate was the same as last half-year. The estimates were adopted. MISS CUNNAH'S SUPERANNUATION.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from the Local Gov- ernment Board with reference to the applica- tion of Miss E. E. Cunnah, late assistant matron at the Workhouse, for the return of the super- annuation contributions paid by her under the Poor Law Officers' Superannuation Act, 1896. The Board stated that no sanction was neces- sary to the repayment of contributions legally returnable under Section 8 of the Act, but they were not aware of any authority to return to an officer who resigned. However, in the special circumstances the Board would be wil- ling to consider an application from the Guar- dians for sanction to a reasonable gratuity being made to Miss Cunnah. The Clerk said that Miss Cunnah had paid in £2l 13s. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Pilling: Have we not paid back the con- tributions to the superannuation fund pre- viously? The Clerk: You cannot do it where 'there is a voluntary severance of the service. It was decided to make Miss Cunnah a gratuity of .t2l.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL. How Diphtheria Was Imported into Halton. A SIXPENNY RATE SUGGESTED</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL. How Diphtheria Was Imported into Halton. A SIXPENNY RATE SUGGESTED</p>
        <p>Mr. C. E. Linaker, sen. presided over the fortnightly meeting of ;he Runcorn Rural Council, which was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday.</p>
        <p>CANAL BRIDGE TO BE REPAIRED. The Clerk read a letter from the resident Engineer of the North Staffordshire Railway Company calling attention to the dangerous state of the road surface over the canal bridge near Dutton Lodge. The macadam had been completely worn off the centre of the brick arch, leaving a deep and dangerous narrow track. A further letter stated that the ,Engineer had given instructions for the road- way to be repaired without prejudice. The Chairman: Then the work will be done.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>WATER QUESTION AT WHITLEY.</head>
        <p>THE WATER QUESTION AT WHITLEY.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from the Walton Estate Office stating that the Council's pro- posals had been submitted to Sir Gilbert Greenall and he could not fall in with their wishes. The terms offered on behalf of Sir Gilbert were that he should arrange to lay a main from the termination of the Warrington Corporation's main, costing approximately £750. Sir Gilbert would be willing to pay half the interest and sinking fund for a period of 15 years, and would also agree to take or pay for Ivater at the cost charged by the Corporation to the Runcorn Council on the nett rateable value of the whole of his property in Lower Whitley. The Clerk said the offer was a poor one and he did not see how the Council could proceed On those lines.</p>
        <p>The mat te7 was deferred for further consid- eration.</p>
        <p>DIPHTHERIA AT HALTON.. Dr. Burton, acting as locum tenens for Dr. Adams, wrote stating that he had inquired into the sanitary condition of certain houses at Halton, where there had been outbreaks of diphtheria. In some of the cases there were defects in sewering and ashpit-privies that re- quired emptying. Yards were found badly re- quiring attention, and pools of filthy water were lying at the backs of the premises. It was decided that sanitary improvements should be undertaken at once. The Nuisance Inspector, in his report, ex- pressed the opinion that the outbreak might possibly be due to a little girl who had come to the village from London and -had contracted the disease there."</p>
        <p>THE ESTIMATES. The Clerk presented his estimate of expendi- ture for the half-year ending March 31st as follows :—The general expenses, including high- ways, £4,956; contingencies and amount to meet expenses of subsequent half-year before a fur- ther call be made, £4,956; deduct probable balance at 30th September, £4,875; nett expen- diture to be provided, £5,027. The assessable value of the district is £198,267, and a rate of 6d. in the will produce £4,956.</p>
        <p>KAISER AND GIP SP to , nore ," How Long Is This Fine P r Last?"</p>
