        <p>11: keond Saturday in succession War- ' ; ttre defeated on their own ground, this .Z„hands (and feet) of Rochdale h“er the brilliant manner in which -,ad opened their season by beating -riuddersfield—no mean opponents— wn grounds, it was confidently ex- lat 4, Y would add to their laurels at Of the Hornets. But fate—and• I:j—decreed otherwise. The game was c:tating one from the point of view of Ls °l's, affording them many exciting There were two alterations in the 4111414,3's compared with the side that did t ();'-eal against Broughton Rangers. )B,lait'eeluarter, and Thomas, at forward., Taberner and Green taking their :1015 Hornets fielded a strong eombina- tPhut 11P an excellent game, despite the v were handicapped by the loss of I,heir full-back, after the first few having to retire hurt. His place In the first half by Corsi, who left L4l7th , reequarter rank to, strengthen as `ne of defence. In the Second half telieved by Williamson, who had been win g threequarter. The crowd was of dimensions, all things considered, (~„"housand people passing the turn- which would seem to warrant the 'cutive in deciding to carry on despite</p>
