IN, m' Prom the scrummage ____,. '3, P°kccession, but another chance was thkiclt-ileek-OP, Corsi created a divers' Ilt ' Which found touch on the half-way 11 thc r l' 4 klkin e lef was only temporary, War- ',. -' A b 'BE imm the ag- ediately resuming ~..... 4cidrant run by Ulu Hopwood was iltkite,a_t of note. The outcome of it was a 'Nei:leer the Warrington end, but POB- - rt° the home citadel was averted by . ths 1. elieved in fine style. 11 'l7'4 Passing by the visitors' three-: ilijcite down when Carter passed fol- 44.01. ever, he immediately redeemed his 'rY -11rimving Taberner into touch, when 114e-lAll43,.gcing for the line, and had only b,ArN I-0 Pass. Hopwood, In trying to kicked the ball against one of Vt.,. This would have been &amp;it 4z.tch.Vit'ictle'l :the .fact that the_i a sphere just Taberner stopped tii into *1 atelli .g the ball, sent, play back htln el'ritory. Carter intercepted the littircge-naitP..4:.sill‘g Movement initiated by the ,ecrfei?elia teis, but nothing resulted from 4lttirtieed al. Amidst great excitement, li ed d ter•the ball to the visitors' ors' goal 1;44low ~'"wn under the posts, hut theepo-oinnt. ,safte:'' k because of a previous kno k . proeClleda Taberner, in attempting 0 . t 01htbe.: "or the corner flag on the reserve ti:Yieg's,l‘°u/Ild • Hereabouts Warrington It, lihilti"' spirited game, a fact which met ki,tl• ttated approval on the Par( of the - 4.-!1, 41mrcokes eluded several of the Roc",- 'Llhet-' Passed to Taberner, who was lot, h°4 the line. However, scrambling l'n.t*eeat P{.l,E'Ved the ball, and forcing opposition' he succeeded in Ikt‘vit. 'fl and securing the first points of 1164 'hid kitchley took the kick, but he 1,:l kr.ce talnor points. the ACK ON THE HORNETS. IA _. to 04 rt Brookes and Fearnley was led _ b )(kit "te Hornets, and Woods klig ti °ver his own line to save. Both ~lellini:lch indulged in a display of long .ie end • - . ' ' for ,e. f, ed in a slight advantage . -111 -'ll the half-way line Taberner tiir4to tbe opposing three-quarters, the .cIY for his side, kicked into er t ellizitval drew near, play was of a; h ',peter, and centred mainly in ' It4if' /337 this time the rain had If% 'lcd hot st been heavy enough to r_. t the t f leiv a 0 0 the ground. When ia afcr the interval, Warrington S Pressing movement, and held °f one try (three points) to</p>
        <p>Pc` 0 .itO ; I ev</p>
        <p>4</p>
        <p>By " Centre:,</p>
        <p>When the game was resumed, Goulden was st)11 missing from the Hornets' ranks, William- son figuring at full-back in his stead. The first few minutes of this half witnessed a number of long punches between the 'Naas, Jolley bring- ing variety by touching down. A fine dribbling movement by the visitors, initiated by Jones at half-way, provided some exciting moments for the onlookers, before the check was applied by Dainteth. Jones broke through, but Jolley, se- curing the ball, kicked to half-way, where Wil- liamson knocked on. This gave Warrington temporary relief. The visitors again asserted themselves, but progress was slow. Young picked up smartly from a scrummage, but was effectively tackled before he had made much ground. A passing movement by the visitors was summarily checked. Taberner got posses- sion of the ball, but was forced into touch before he had made much headway. Try as they might neither team for a time could get far away from the half-way line.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>AN EXCITING TRY.</head>
        <p>AN EXCITING TRY.</p>
        <p>Young made an exciting dash for the line, but, in receiving the pass, he had knocked on, sO was brought back by the referee's whistle. War- rington slowly gained ground, and a free-kick against Schofield, for obstruction, almost re- sulted in Jolley obtaining a goal. A bright piece of individual play, of which Hopwood was the hero, followed. Warrington were pressing near the visitors' line, when Hopwood cleverly intercepted a pass and raced at top speed to- wards the Warrington goal. He threaded his way through the home ranks, and when he had only Jolley to beat he punted over the Warring- ton full-back's head, and a neck-and-neck race between the pair ensued- Hopwood reached the ball first, and, kicking over the line, registered a splendid try, before he was " downed" by three of the defenders, who had rushed to Jolley's assistanoe. Warrington appealed to the referee, but his decision was against them. Jones took the kick at goal, but failed miserably. The teams were now on an equal footing, and, if possible, play became keener than ever, the visitors playing up pluckily, though handi- capped by still being one man short. Brookes took a penalty kick, awarded to the home team for obstruction, but the little ground gained was quickly recaptured by the Hornets. Jones had a drop at goal, hut the ball went wide. Soon afterwards Jolley had to bestir him- self to save his charge, but he was found all there. Young kicked the ball from a melee in the home half, and Prudence picked up, but he had not gone far when Brookes brought him to his knees. Warrington again assumed the aggressive, but a chance was spoiled by the ball being kicked dead. Williamson relieved the pressure, and Baker made a mark on the visitors' "25" line, but Brookes, who took the kick, sent the ball wide. Young, Brookes and Taberner then figured prominently with a hot dash, but the movement was brought to a full stop when the last-named was tackled, very little ground having been gained. The Warrington vanguard, led by Tranter, rushed for the line, and Williamson, finding himself in difficulties, thought it discriminate to kick over his own line to save the situation. The visitors were awarded a free-kick, but Brookes, who secured, mulled when passing to Young. Fitzsimmons was at fault when he got offside, providing the home team with a penalty kick, which was taken by Jolley, who put Warrington ahead when about a quarter of an hour from time, amid intense excitement. THE FINAL STAGES.</p>
        <p>The visitors, through Williamson, found touch near the Warrington line, and, after several scrummages, Jones passed to Woods, who, after eluding a couple of opponents, handed on to Corsi, who forced his way over, thus putting the visitors in f;ont by a single point, an ad- vantage which was increased when the same player added the goal points. Stung by this set-back the Warrington pack redoubled their efforts, but, alas! to no purpose. Corsi was again in the picture a moment later, when be brushed aside several of the home team. How- ever, he spoiled this good effort by kicking low into the hands of Jolley. Hopwood was slightly injured in a tackle, but was able to resume after being off the field a short time. The visitors pressed, and Carter was thrown into touch near the corner-flag. From the scrummage the ball was handed to Jones, who was only pulled up in the nick of time. Warrington took a turn at attacking, and Brookes, obtaining possession from Dainteth, kicked the ball into the vicinity of the Hornets' goal, where Corsi, picking up, punted over the touch-line and the ball was brought back. However, from the scrummage, Jones secured, and the ball was worked to the halfway line. Just on time a penalty was awarded the Rochdale team, but Hopwood kicked wide. Dainteth got control, but mulled in trying to send the ball down the field; The final whistle blew with the visitors pressing and with the score 8 points to 5 in the Hornets' favour-1 goal 2 tries to 1 goal 1 try. Warrington's supporters could not help feel- ing disappointment on Saturday, but, after all, it has to be admitted that their defeat was in- flicted by a superior side, despite the fact that for practically all the time it consisted of only twelve men. • The homesters' weakness lay chiefly in the three-quarter line, which seemed to lack that mutual understanding which marked the corresponding line in the visitors' ranks. The Warrington forwards were, how- ever, rather better than the Hornets' vanguard, especially in the loose, although the visitors' front rankers pushed the ball from the serum- : mage far oftener than did the home men. Jolley played another really good game, but he has yet to exhibit his last year's form. Taberner was probably the pick of the homesters, playing a reliable and tactful game. He was mostly always there when wanted. The two tries se- cured by the visitors were well deserved, and that which came per Hopwood undoubtedly con- stituted the finest piece of individual play of the iatch. Young acquitted himself admirably on a number of occasions, and though Brookes was at fault once or twice he made amends by putting in more than one bit of sterling work. Among the forwards, Clare and Tranter specially distinguished themselves, and there was little to choose between the remainder, all of whom worked hard.</p>
        <p>The threequarters were the pick of the Roch- dale team, the forwards showing a tendency to leave the lzulk of the work to them. Jones and Schofield played well in their respective positions, and Carter and Woods were the most effective of the visitors' scrummagers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S"; ..SECOND STRINC</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S"; ..SECOND STRINC</p>
        <p>BEATEN.</p>
        <p>Hornets' Reserves Win By 20 Points.</p>
        <p>Warrington's reserve team journeyed to Roch- dale on Saturday and met the Hornets' second string on the Athletic Ground. The game was a somewhat one-sided affair, the Hornets being the stronger combination and running out win- ners by the substantial margin of 20 points. Few people witnessed the match, that is to say, few by comparison with the number usually to be seen on the Hornets' ground on match days. Wa. rrington fielded the team selected the pre-</p>
        <p>"C 4141 M t or- -0 Nam. . cycles and Cars. Repairs by competent men. Petrol and tyres, "'Was STOCKTON HEATH GARAGE, LONDON ROAD. 4110414aum_ TelesPhone 63 Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>;It f,,</p>
        <p>vious Wednesday, no alterations having been rendered necessary. The game was an inter- esting one, and although the Hornets totted up most points they by no means had things all their own way, the Warrington forwards caus- ing them plenty of trouble by their persistency. When the game had been in progress about a quarter of an hour Bevan, of Warrington,-had the misfortune to split his head open, and he had to leave the field and have a number of stitches put in the wound. Taylor opened the scoring for the Hornets, getting over the line in good style. Mole converted. Nothing else was scored in the first half, the interval thus arriving with the score at 5--nil in favour of the homesters. _VISITORS PUT_UP A GAME FIGHT.</p>
        <p>Warrington tried hard to get on level terms in the opening minutes of the second half, but they were unable to do so, though they put up a game fight, and one which occasioned their opponents plenty of work to withstand it, to say nothing of the anxiety.' When the second half was a few minutes old Warrington were unlucky to lose another man in the person of 'Worrell, who had been putting in some most strenuous work on their behalf, and who had the misfor- tune to stave a couple of ribs in. This left Warrington with only eleven effective men, and, needless to say, just about destroyed their last chance of, winning. Thus weakened they could do little save act on the defensive. Once, how- ever, opportunity presented itself .and their for- wards got away in swinging fashion, and Davis crossed the line. Bramhall, however, failed to convert.' As time .wore on the advantage in numbers held by Rochdale began to tell, and Warrington were driven to acting practically entirely on the defensive. Stott, Taylor (two), and Roman scored tries for the homesters, and Mole added three goals. When the final whistle nunded the score read: Rochdale, four goals five tries (23 points); Warrington, one try (three points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>WARRINGTON HANDICAPPED.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON HANDICAPPED.</p>
        <p>The loss of two of their men, of course, handi- capped Warrington to a great extent, but even had they been able to retain- their services throughout the match it is doubtful whether defeat could have been turned into victory, the Hornets' front line proving much the smarter of the two. The Warrington defence was their strong point, though it may sound curious to say so, in face of the disparity in the scores of the two teams. But the fact remains that had the Warrington backs not worked like Trojans, and been well up to what was required of them, the adverse balance would have been a much bigger one.</p>
        <p>AIANCIIESTER AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. First Division. ANCHOR CABLE CO. v. RYLANDS' RECS. This match was played on the Old Sham- rocks' ground, Leigh, before a big attendance. The Anchor Cable team, which was mainly composed of last year's Shamrocks' players, are new members of the Manchester and District League. Anchor Cable, winning the toss, elected to play with the wind. Stretford kicked off for the Recs. Both teams played keenly, tackling well. Good play by the Brothers Wood,, of Leigh, nearly ended in a score, Horrocks just saving in the nick of time. Woods dropped a goal for Leigh. Another attempt was inches wide. Good work—by the Recs.' backs took the play to the home citadel, where Higson saved. The Recs. still continued to press, and Povey, giving a short kick, followed up, and outpacing the opposition scored at the corner. Half-time arrived with the score as follows: Rylands', 1 try (3 points); Anchor Cable Co., 1 goal (2 points). The home team commenced the second half against the wind. From a passing movement among the Recs.' backs Rowlinson got over at the corner, the goal kick failing. Griffiths, after a good run, was just grassed on the line. The last quarter of an hour's play was strenu- ous, both sides putting in all they knew. The " Cablers" tried specially hard to get the win- ning point, but the Recs.' defence held good. The final score was: Rylands' Recs., 2 tries (6 points); Anchor Cable, 1 dropped goal (2 points). Horrocks, Povey and Griffiths were the pick of the visitors' backs. The forwards, ably led by Stretford, held their own with the burly pack of the homesters. Higson and the Brothers Wood were the pick of the Cable Co.'s thirteen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>INTERMEDIATE RUGBY LEAGUE. Opening Games of the Season.</head>
        <p>INTERMEDIATE RUGBY LEAGUE. Opening Games of the Season.</p>
        <p>BY "OLD CESTRIAN."</p>
        <p>Expressions of pleasure at sight of so many "fields" being occupied in Victoria Park by the teams engaged in the above League reached me as I went about from match to match on Saturday, the first day of operations. In all, five League matches took place. The game arranged to be played before the War- rington-Rochdale match was taken to Victoria Park, the Warrington Committee having decided on Friday night that the Wilderspool ground was too soft for two matches to be played in the one afternoon. I hear that many people were disappointed at not Seeing this match. It undoubtedly provided a great struggle, St. Mary's just managing to beat St. Ann's by 5 points to 3. St. Mary's were hest represented by Rimmer, Sauce and. McKie, and St. Ann's by Owen, Marshall and Hewitt. The game was very even throughout and each side scored when playing with the wind in their favour. St. Mary's were only very slightly better than their opponents. Bolton Council, 64 points; St. Alban's, 0! Such was the result of another match. It needs no description. St. Alban's were outweighted fore and aft, and the " match " resolved itself into a series IA processions over their line. The Bolton team are a hefty lot and will make their presence in the League felt. They posses„ strong pack. The Brothers Jones make two good half-backs, though they are prone to kick too much. " Chuck " Edwards and Bate are two capable backs. , The Heathside Freghall v. Parochial match produced a rare struggle, the latter winning by a goal-kick. The Parochial full-back, Bran. wood, played a sterling game for his side and was ably supported by Broadhurst and Brown. The Heathside "stars" were Griffiths, Way- well and Harris.</p>
        <p>In their match with Wycliffe, St. James' showed themselves more experienced in the handling department. Stan. Rigby stood out as the brainiest player on the field. Brannon, Higham, Green and "Dai " Davies also played well for the "Jimmies." Holloway and Wor- rall did best for Wycliffe. The score of 16 points to 5 in favour of the Latchford team was a true reflex of the game. ' Arpley-street lost to Beamont United by 11 points to nil. The old failing in Arpley- street was still shown—they lacked dash and sting! Beamont were a little heavier, but they fully deserved their victory. Considering that this was the opening Satur- day of 'the season, the quality of the football displayed was very good, and those interested can look forward to seeing some hard games later on. TO-DAY'S FIXTURES. Before the Warrington Match.. Kick-off 2.15. Parochial 0.8. v. Arpley-street 0.B.; referee, W. Bibby.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>In Victoria Park. Kickoff 3.30.</head>
        <p>In Victoria Park. Kickoff 3.30.</p>
        <p>Helithside Bewsey 0.8. v. Sacred Heart 0.B.; T. Flynn.' Silver-street 0.8. v. St. Alban's 0.13.; R. Wil- liams.</p>
        <p>Bolton Council 0.8. v. St. James', 0.8. Juniors; G. Crozier. ' Beamont 0.8. Athletic V. St. Barn,abas' 6.8.; J. Bearsdell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>To-day the Warrington first team journey to Oldham and meet the locals at Watersheddings. The train leaves the Central Station, Warring- ton, at 12.50, and the kick-off is timed for 3.30.</p>
        <p>Warrington's first string were selected on Wednesday night as follows : _</p>
        <p>Jolley. Baker, Brookes (captain), Morgan, Taberner. Dainteth (scrum), Young. Cox, Clare, Fearnley, Tranter, Critehley, Green. Reserve, Harmer. Whilst the senior teams are fighting out their</p>
        <p>(antinued in next column.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <p>All the Junior Clubs Busy on Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>SOME KEEN CONTESTS WITNESSED.</head>
        <p>SOME KEEN CONTESTS WITNESSED.</p>
        <p>On Saturday there was a full complement of matches in the local League, all the clubs being engaged. Some very keen and exciting games were !witnessed, and in these instances the scores were alike, each winning team scoring five goals to their opponents' three By their victory over Latchford Baptists the Fairfield United team are proving themselves to be a good all-round side, having, so far, scored 14 goals against three. On Saturday they had the assistance of the veteran, W. Hunter, who made his first appearance this season at inside-right, and his presence instilled more confidence in the younger members of the team. Although the Baptists were defeated they were not disgraced, and at the interval they deserved to be on equal terms. It was in the early stages of the second half that they fell to pieces. Their half-back line gave a very poor exhibition, especially in the centre, a posi- tion which ought to be among the strongest in every team. The Elmwood Cons. and Paddington match proved to be a keenly contested game. The visitors had gained the only point at the inter- val. In the second half neither team were able to pierce their opponents' defence, and ulti- mately victory rested with the visitors. The result must be encouraging to the Cons.' Com- mittee, but yet not so satisfactory as could be desired. Still it shows an improvement in the display of the team when compared with pre- vious results. _</p>
        <p>_ The Earlestown T.M.C.A. were visited by the Bank Quay Recs.; who had to return defeated by five goals against two. Judging from the form displayed the Recs. hardly deserved to be beaten by so substantial a margin, and it was owing to weak play by their backs, which pro- vided the home forwards with many chances which they accepted and turned to good account. The Earlestown St. John's Mission received as visitors the Orford White Star. In this in- stance the visitors proved victors by 5 goals to 2. The visitors scored three times in the first half, when they had the wind in their favour, and it was soon evident in the second portion that the homesters had exerted themselves to their limit when playing against the elements, as they were not able to accept many chances that offered themselves during this period of the game. At the same time credit must be given to the White Star players for achieving a sub- stantial victory on their opponents' ground. A very hard and vigorously contested game was witnessed at Newton, where the Cons. were opposed by Hollins Green. This being the Cons.' first appearance in League games, they are to be congratulated on the result. The meeting of Longford United and the Central on the Horse and Jockey Ground was a keen and exciting game, each team scoring once before the interval. About half-way through the second moiety the United scored twice in quick succession. The visitors exerted themselves to their utmost to reduce the arrears and rough play was indulged in, with the re3:llt that some of the players received a caution from the referee, which, happily, served to keep them within bounds.</p>
        <p>At Lovely-lane St. Barnabas' were opposed to Fairfield. A hard and keen game was witnessed. Each side scored a couple of goals and conse- quently shared the points.</p>
        <p>Notes by "Linesman."</p>
        <p>day on the ground of the first-named. The home team had made a couple of changes in their personnel from the previous week. The following players turned out : Elmwood Cons.: Norke; J. Phoenix and Jas. Mellor; Pritchard, E. Mellor and Jas. Phoenix; W. Phoenix, Wells, Ashton, Ellam and Lamb. Paddington: George; Burden and Smith; Gar- nett, Marsh and Holmes; Holcroft, Pickering, Derbyshire, Bleasedale and Craig. , The referee was Mr. A. Holt.</p>
        <p>The home captain won the toss and elected to play with the sun behind his men. Derby- shire set the ball rolling and the visitors played 1, in determined style, but met a stout defender in J. Phoenix, who tackled well and cfrove the ball up the field with a fine kick. End-to-end play followed and the home goal was in. jeopardy, owing to partial clearances and the strong wind. From a long, swinging centre from the visitors' left wing, Mellor miskicked,, and Holcroft raced in and scored a good goal for the visitors. The homesters were kept on the defensive, and the ball was constantly in touch, but the visitors were unable to add to their advantage, and at half-time still held the lead by one goal to nil. The second moiety opened in favour of the home team, who showed improved form, but they were up against a pair of good has, and although they gained several corner kicks they failed to break down the visitors' defence. The visitors transferred play to the home end, and Norke brought off a good save from Derbyshire at close range. The Cons.' forwards were corn - Wining very well together, but failed to press home their attack, and when the final whie ?A() sounded no further scoring had been registered, the result therefore being: Paddington, cne goal; Elmwood Cons., nil. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT</p>
        <p>LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>FAIRFIELD UNITED v. LATCHFORD BAPTISTS.</p>
        <p>These old rivals met on Saturday for the first time in League competition, the Padgate-lane ground being the scene of the contest. There were a fair number of spectators. Both teams were at full strength, and lined out as follows: Fairfield: •Potter; Brown and Millward; Gleave, Bebbington and A. Woodhouse; Cox, Hunter, Scott, Poulter and S. Woodhouse. Baptists: Rushton; E. Hardy and Shelmar- dine; Noden, Hardy and Done; Pierce, Harding, Clark, Stubbs and Blease. The referee was Mr. R. Brandreth.</p>
        <p>Tile home captain won the toss and defended the railway goal. In the teeth of a strong wind Clark set the ball rolling, and the visitors led off with an attack on the home 'goal, but were ' sent back by Brown. The visitors were soon attacking again, Pierce gnally shooting wide. The homesters took up the running, and forced Shelmardine to concede a corner, which proved fruitless. Aided by a strong wind, the home team were having the best of the game, and the visitors' custodian saved at the expense of a corner. The resultant kick being well placed, S. Woodhouse opened the score with a grand shot. Following a spell of even play in mid- field, the visitors' forwards broke away and gained a couple of corners in quick succession, but they proved of no avail, Harding in each case heading just over the bar. The home for- wards paid a visit to the visitors' end, where Cox shot over the bar. The visitors' goal was hotly attackad, Cox and S. Woodhouse showing up well and Rushton making several good saves. Poulter sent in a rare shot just on the wrong side of the post. From the goal kick the visit- ors were seen to better advantage and Stubbs, getting past the home back, equalised with a fast shot. The game at this juncture was being contested at a fast pace, and both ends were visited in turn. Scott gained the lead for the home team, heading just out of the reach of the visitors' goalkeeper. The visitors lost a grand chance of equalising by handling the ball in front of goal from a corner kick, and at the interval Fairfield were leading by two goals against one. On the resumption the wind had dropped con- siderably and the home forwards were soon in front of the visitors' goal. S. Woodhouse se- cured and Hunter placed just wide of the posts. The home team, encouraged by their success, were playing a good game, and, after Hunter had hit the bar. Poulter secured the ball and sent it between the posts for the third goal, Scott subsequently adding a fourth. The visit- ors livened up and showed some good combina- tion. Ifillward made a grand clearance at back, and the visitors were awarded a penalty kick from which„ Stubbs ,scored their second goal. From this onward the homesters had the best of play, the visitors' half-back line being weak. Poulter added a fifth goal and directly afterwards Bebbington skimmed the bar with a hard drive, which was followed by two unsuc- cessful corners. At the call of time the result was: Fairfield, 5 goals; Latchford Baptists, 2 goals. _ELMWOOD CONS. V. PADDINGTON.</p>
        <p>These two neighbouring clubs met on Satur-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY—continued.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY—continued.</p>
        <p>battle in Cottonopelis, Oldham "A" will journey to Warrington, where they. will meet the local "A" team at Wilderspool, the kick-off being timed for 3.30.</p>
        <p>Warrington's team will consist of : Bramball, full-back; Davis, Hammond, Nicholls and Cherrett, threequarters; Laws and Douglas, half-backs; Harmer, Thornton. Mather, Logan, Pouncey and Fletcher, forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>Rylands' Recs.._ v. Swinton St. Mary's.</head>
        <p>Rylands' Recs.._ v. Swinton St. Mary's.</p>
        <p>These teams will meet in a First Division (Manchester and District League) fixture at Gorsey-lane, to-day, the kick-off being timed for 3.30. Rylands' Rees.: F. Horrocks. full- back; J. Griffiths, 11. Povey, A. Horrocks, and J. Rowlinson, tbreequarters; G. Warren and J. (fallop, half-backs; A. Stretford (captain); J. Whittaker. J. Brandwood, J. Broadhurst, F. Potter and J. Smith, forwards: Reserves: T. Riley (back) and J. Dumbell (forward).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>EARLESTOWN Y.M.C.A. v. BANK QUAY</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN Y.M.C.A. v. BANK QUAY</p>
        <p>RECS.</p>
        <p>These teams opposeil-e-aa other for the first time in a League match on the ground of the first-named, the following players taking the field:—</p>
        <p>Y.M.C.A. : Martin; Chambers and Atkinson; Fairhurst, Goulden and Buckley; Scott, Hughes, Riley, Gilman and Lee.</p>
        <p>Recs. : Bushill ; Berry and Smith; Long. Webb and Worrall; Eecleston, Deakin, Millington, Davies and Weston.</p>
        <p>Reteree Howell had charge of the game. The home team won the toss, and Millington kicked off. The visitors soon got into their stride and worked towards the home goal, where Eccleston, with a fine centre, caused Martin to fist away. From the goal kick the home for- wards combined well toother, and Riley beat the visitors' backs and sending the ball past , Bushill, opened the score. Directly afterwards, the home forwards came again, led by Gilman and Lee, and Riley nipped in and converted a centre into a goal, the visitors' backs being at fault. Good play by Webb, Millington and Deakin led to an attack on the home citadel, which resulted in Webb scoring a fine goal for the visitors. The interval arrived with the home team leading by two goals to one. During the first half the game was very keenly and evenly contested, but in the early stages of the second moiety it was noticeable that the visitors seemed to have slackened off. Certain it is that they did not show to the same advan- tage. The home forwards swung the ball from wing to wing, a style of play which appeared to put the visitors' defence off their usual game. Fairhurst, with a first-time shot, notched a third goal. The visitors' citadel continued to be the scene of action, and Smith and Berry were being hard pressed, when Webb cleared with fine judg- ment, and Davies, racing up, secured possession and tricking the home backs, scored the second goal for the Recs. The homesters, hereabouts, began to play a more steady game, and with better results, for Lee increased their lead with a good effort, and just before the close Riley added another point from a penalty kick, with the result that at the call of time the score was: Earlestown Y.M.C.A., 5 goals; Bank Quay Recs., 2 goals. EARLESTOWN ST. JOHN'S MISSION v. ORFORD WHITE STAR.</p>
        <p>The above teams met on the ground of the former, before a fair crowd of spectators. The visitors won the toss and decided to play with the wind and slope in their favour. The game started in an exciting manner. Straight from the kick-off the visitors' centre secured the ball, and beating several opponents scored a splendid goal. The White Star continued to have the best of the game, and after fifteen minutes' play increased their lead. Stung by these reverses the home team redoubled their efforts and for a time had a greater share of the game, but they failed at the critical moment. Allen scored a third goal for the visitors. Griffiths then man- aged to put one up for the home team. At half- time the score read: Star, three goals; Mission, one goal. On resuming, the shome team had the wind behind them, and were soon attacking the visit- ors' goal. Smith reduced the arrears from a penalty kick. Even play followed for a spell, when the homesters began to show signs of dis- tress owing to the hard work in the first half, and the visitors, pulling themselves together, scored two more goals before the call of time, !which arrived with the figures as follows: Orford White Star, 5 goals; Earlestown St. John's Mission, 2 goals. NEWTON CONGREGATIONAL v. HOLLINS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>GREEN.</head>
        <p>GREEN.</p>
        <p>Newton Congregational were at home to Hol- lins Green on Saturday, this being the first- named club's opening match in their' League campaign. The teams lined out as follows:— Newton Cons: Wightman; Fearnley and Tither; W. Clarke, Porter and Lennox; Beres- fiord, A. Clarke, Rowbotham, Smart and Gleave.</p>
        <p>, . Hollins Green : A. Perrin; Martin and RH- shaw ; Buckley, Atherton and Cleworth; H. Per- rin, Mellor, Etchells, Taylor and Atkinson. The home captain won the toss and set the visitors to face the sun and wind. In the open- ing stages Perrin was called upon, after which the visitors got acclimatised to the elements, and had more of the play, which, however, was chiefly in mid-field, until after seventeen min- utes Smart scored with a long shot, which Perrin misjudged. The same player put on a , second point five minutes later, after beating! Martin for possession. These reverses nettled the visitors, and from the place kick Mellor. with! a splendid individual effort beat several opponents and sent .the ball to Etchell, who, being in a good position, had not much difficulty in beating Wightman. Shortly afterwards At- kinson equalised the scores, and at • half-time the teams stood on a level footing, each having two goals to their credit. On resuming both teams played keen and vigorous football. Both goals were strongly at- tacked in turn. Although now facing the wind, the homesters appeared to play a better game than in the first half, their left wing being espe- cially conspicuous for their industry and enter- prise. It was from one of their centres that litowbotham gained the lead, the same player subsequently adding a fourth goal to the credit of his side. The reverses they suffired stung the visitors to redoubled energy, and they played a fine game, but the home defenders were in a resolute mood, and were equal to all the attacks made upon them. The game was splendidly contested, both teams playing good football; whilst they are also to be congratulated upon the sportsmanlike spirit w‘liich they displayed. At .the call of time the • score read : Newton Cons., 4 goals; Hollins Green, 2. goal4s. LONGFORD UNITED v. WARRINaTON</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>CENTRAL.</head>
        <p>CENTRAL.</p>
        <p>These olubs met on Saturday on the ground of the former, which is situated behind the Horse and Jockey Inn, Winwick-road. There was a</p>
        <p>good crowd of spectators. The teams were as follows :</p>
        <p>Longford: Brown; Moseley... and Holden; Mason, Stevens and Sherratt; Parr, Cox, Finch, Houghton and Hands. Central: Roberts; Tully and Glover; Goulden, Hill and Williams; Jates, S. Hands, McCabe, Bates and Barnes. '-</p>
        <p>, The referee was Mr. S. C. Essenhigh. The visitors won the toss and with a strong wind behind them, Finch set the ball rolling. Some good passing movements were seen early in the game. The Central began to be more aggre- sive, but when near goal their forwards were weak and erratic. A free kick placed the home goal in danger, and Bates scored for the visit- ors. This reverse caused the game to be keenly fought, and Hands missed a grand chance of equalising when in front of an open goal, as, later on, did Finch at the opposite end, shooting over the bar. Shortly afterwards, however, Stevens made amends by scoring a splendid goal, thus putting the scores equal. The visitors now fought strenuously to gain the lead, and were inclined to adopt rough tactics, which course brought some of their players under the ban of the referee, who had to administer cautions. At the interval the score was one goal each. On the restart the game was keenly contested, and .the homesters spoilt several chances by lying off-side. A free kick brought relief to the visitors' defence, when Sherratt was penalised for foul play. The home goal was placed in danger, but the backs managed to clear and play was soon again in the visitors' goal area. However, no score resulted. The Central then made the running, and gained a couple of fruit- less corners, following upon which the home forwards were again in a dangerous position. and Hands scored a second point, thus giving the home team the lead. The Central strove hard to equalise, but the home backs staved them off, and Parr and Cox transferred to the visitors' end, where the latter registered the third goal. No further scoring took place, and the game ended: Longford United, 3 goals; Warrington Central, 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>ST. BARNABAS' v. FAIRFIELD.</head>
        <p>ST. BARNABAS' v. FAIRFIELD.</p>
        <p>...... The above teams met on Saturday on the Lovely-lane ground, which was in a heavy con- dition owing to the recent rains. Both clubs were well represented and the game started in brisk fashion. The visitors fairly held their own and were the first to score, the centre-for- ward converting a splendid centre from the left. This reverse roused the homesters, and their efforts were soon rewarded, Vernon equalising. When the interval was called the score was one goal each. The second half was fought out keenly, both teams being anxious to gain -the points. Each side scored again, Houghton and Appleton doing the needful, and at the call of time the score was 2 goals each, a result which was a fair reflex of the game.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>_ Latchford Baptists v. Elmwood Cons.; W. Dennett.</p>
        <p>Bank Quay Rees. v. Earlestown Mission; 0. Knight. Fairfield A:F.C. v. Earlestown Y.M.C.A.; J. T. Hadkiss.</p>
        <p>Orford White Star v. Newton Cons.; S. C. Essenhigh. Hollins Green v. St. Barnabas' ; J. Smith. Warrington Central v. Paddington; R. G. Brandreth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>_ Elmwood-avenue Congregational v. Latchford Baptists.—At Latchford. Players meet at Bridge Foot at 2.30. Elmwood-avenUe: R. Nork, goal; James Phenix and Jas. Mellor, backs; C. Mel- lor, Jas. Phoenix and F. Dutton, half-backs; W. Phenix, D. Gleave, A. E. Ashton, T. Wells and A. Lamb, forwards. Reserves: N. Ellam and W. Hall.</p>
        <p>Paddington A.F.C. v. Warrington Central.— In this match, which will be played in Victoria Park this (Saturday) afternoon, the first-named club will be represented by the following team: J. George, goal; E. Holmes and C. Smitii, full- backs; J. Wrench (capt.), A. Marsh and S. Tay- lor, half-backs; Holcroft, Bleasdale, Darbyshire, Garnett and Craik, forwards. Leigh Villas v. Grappenhall.—The first-named club will be represented in their match against Grappenhall (at Grappenhall) this afternoon by the following: E. Howells, goal; A:Barton and A. Bailey, full-backs; A. Garside, T. Bar- ton and Farnell, half-backs; W. Burrows, Kir- win, H. Underhill, J. Solon and J. Holden, for- wards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>ITEMS.</head>
        <p>ITEMS.</p>
        <p>Fairfield United fancy their chances in the League. •</p>
        <p>They are now fielding the strongest team in the history of the club. In the three opening matches of the season they have scored 14 goals, as compared with a bare three put up against them. _</p>
        <p>They inclUde in their ranks three players who formerly did service for other local clubs, vie.: W. Hunter (late of Warrington Albion), Poul- ter (formerly of Great Sankey), and Millward (who used to play for Orford St. John's). In A. and S. Woodhouse they possess a couple of extremely useful players. .</p>
        <p>In Stubbs the Baptists possess a player who rarely makes a mistake when it comes to penalty kicking. On Saturday he scored both goals for his side.</p>
        <p>Elmwood Cons. showed greatly improved form on Saturday. Garnett, who formerly played for the Adult School and Padgate, now turns out with the Paddingtonians. The Y.U.C.A. surprised Bank Quay Recs. on Saturday. .</p>
        <p>Riley again put up three goals for the young men.</p>
        <p>As a centre forward he takes a lot of beating amongst the junior clubs. In the Longford v. Central match, Glover and Stevens, who used to play together in the Great Sankey team, were in opposition to each other. Newton Cons. are to be congratulated on their first win, Hollins Green being the victims.</p>
        <p>The White Star somewhat surprised the Mis- sion lads on 'Saturday. The Stars have got together a strong combina- tion this season and their motto is "Keep your eyes on us for the championship."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>Warrington Working Men's</p>
        <p>Association.</p>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE.</p>
        <p>The annual match, which took place in the Aintree Canal, was not up to expectations by a long way. The number of members 1 Aking part in it was the lowest the club has 11..1 for this last five years. It was no fault of the offi- cials that the contest was not a success, as the prizes competed for were worth a better enh•y, and it is for them to say why they did net turn up in greater numbers at the annual meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>GOWY„</head>
        <p>THE GOWY„</p>
        <p>The Gowy has once more provided us with a fine dace of 13oz. Gdr., caught by J. Green, and we hear that it is to be set up. A dace of that weight will take all the beating members can give it, and it is the correct thing to do. Dace of this weight are recorded only occasionally. We hear such poor results from onr canals that Mickle Trafford is the only place left from which members can get specimens. -</p>
        <p>THE WAR FUND MATCH. We notice that the preliminary bills have been published for the above contest, and the Northern Anglers and Provincials have joined hands in the contest. It is better that they have done so. The prike list will be one that should draw a good entry. It is due from all the anglers of Warrington to make an effort to make it a success, whether they dare members or not, as both societies are determined that they will leave nothing to chance, to hand over to the fund a substantial amount. "Mr. Mounfield on Specimen Fishing" will appear next week. A.S.</p>
        <p>THE MAIN POINT. A &amp;Offish laird invited an English friend to go salmon fishing with him. The Englishman hooked a salmon, but in his excitement fell into the stream. The keeper, seeing he was no swim- mer, went to his aid, when the laird called out: "What are ye about, Donald? Get hand o' the rod' and look to the feesh. He will wait. lint the feesh winna."</p>
      </div>
