      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>BATTLE OF THE AISNE.</head>
        <p>THE BATTLE OF THE AISNE.</p>
        <p>A GERMAN STAND THAT NIYSTI- FIED THE ALLIES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>Sir John French's Account.</head>
        <p>Sir John French's Account.</p>
        <p>When the great retreat from before Paris began and became a rout the Allies thought the Germans were on the -run and would quickly -be beyond the French frontier again. Having, rapidly traversed the distance between the Marne and the Aisne, however, the Germans on Sunday, September 13, settled down in entrench- ments that had been prepared for them, brought forward reinforcements and big guns, and set themselves to defying us all along the line. In his despatch dated September 14 General French said the Aisne engagements that were proceed- ing "may be merely of a rearguard nature on a large scale, or the commencement of a battle of a more serious nature." It has raged almost continuously ever since, and has become so " serious " as to be reckoned the greatest Wattle in history. Sir John French's official descriptive writer has furnished the Press Bureau with an account of the Battle of the Aisne from the date of his last report, September 14, to September 18 (last Friday) inclusive. It was not known, it is still stated, whether the battle was originally intended for a rear- guard action, but it had developed into a pitched battle, and the Allies were still fighting "just across the river" on their "whole front." PRISONERS AND GUNS CAPTURED.</p>
        <p>On the 14th -strong reinforcements of our troops were passed to the north bank, the troops crossing by ferry, by pontoon bridges, and by the remains of the permanent bridges. Close co-operation with the French forces was maintained, and the general progress made was good. Although the opposition was vigorous and the state of the roads after the heavy rain made movements slow, one division alone failed to secure the ground it expected to: The Ist Army Corps, after repulsing repeated attacks, captured 600 prisoners and 12 guns; the cavalry also took a number of prisoners. Many of the Germans taken belong to Reserve and Landwehr formations, which fact appears to indicate that the enemy is compelled to draw on the older classes of soldiers to fill the gaps in his ranks. GERMAN ATTACKS REPULSED. There was heavy rain throughout the night of the 14th-15th. The Germans bombarded our lines nearly all day, using heavy, guns, brought no doubt from before Maubeuge, as well as those with the corps. All their counter-attacks, however, failed, although in some places they were re- peated six times; one made on the 4th Guards Brigade was repulsed with heavy slaughter. Further counter-attacks made during the night were beaten off. Rain came on towards even- ing and continued intermittently until 9 a.m. on the 16th. Besides adding to the discomfort of the soldiers holding open trenches in the firing line, the wet weather to some extent ham- pered the motor transport service, which was also hindered by the broken bridges. On Wednesday, the 16th, there was little change in the situation opposite the British. The efforts made by the enemy were less active than on the previous day, though their bom- bardment continued throughout the morning and evening. Our artillery fire drove the de- fenders off one of the salients of their position, but they returned in the evening. On Thursday, the 17th, the situation still re- mained unchanged in its essentials. The Ger- man heavy artillery fire was more active than on the previous day. The only infantry attacks made by the enemy were on the extreme right of our position and, as had happened before, were repulsed with heavy loss, chiefly on this occasion by our field artillery. GENERAL IDEA OF THE FIGHTING.</p>
        <p>In order to ponvey some idea of the nature of the fighting it, may be said that along the greater part of our front the Germans have been driven back from the forward slopes on the north of the river. Their infantry are holding strong lines of trenches amongst and along the edges of the numerous woods which crown these slopes. These trenches are elaborately con- structed and cleverly concealed. In many places there are wire entanglements and lengths of rabbit fencing both in the woods and in the open, carefully alined so that they can be swept by rifle fire and machine guns, which are in- visible from our side of the valley. The ground in front of the infantry trenches is also as a rule under cross fire from field artillery placed on neighbouring features and under high-angle fire from pieces placed well back behind woods on top of the plateau. A feature of this action, as of the previous fights, is the use made by the enemy of their numerous heavy howitzers, with which they are able to direct a long-range fire all over the valley and right across it. Upon these they evidently place great reliance. Where our men are holding ' the forward edges of the high ground on the north side they are now strongly entrenched. They are well fed, and in spite of the wet weather of the past week are cheerful and confident.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>"A SIEGE ACTION."</head>
        <p>"A SIEGE ACTION."</p>
        <p>British Disregard lor German Artillery</p>
        <p>A later descriptive account from the front says the battle has become more like a siege than a general action. Both sides are strongly en- trenched. Artillery fire goes on day after day almost without a stop,. Aeroplanes reconnoitre the positions. The days described were days of almost cease- less rain. _ _</p>
        <p>Some interesting passages from letters found on prisoners are quoted. The Germans praise the bravery of the British and the skilful use of cover made by our infantry. Plainly, the enemy are not comfortable. Despite their officers' reassuring statements, the men are be- ginning to feel doubts. The retreat from the Marne is difficult to explain away, and has left an impression which the terrible losses inflicted .during the recent fighting b 3", the French field artillery have deepened.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>MAGNIFICENT BRITISH SPIRIT.</head>
        <p>MAGNIFICENT BRITISH SPIRIT.</p>
        <p>The brunt of this long conflict has "naturally fallen upon the infantry.". With their trenches half full of water, constantly soaked to the skin, subjected to interminable bombardment by the heaviest German artillery—probably in- tended for the siege of Paris—they have held out with magnificent spirit and have repulsed every attempt by the enemy to break their line. The German theory that no troops could stand such a bombardment has broken against their cheerfulness and courage. They ridicule the German shells, with their heavy columns of black greasy smoke, by such nicknames as "Black Maria." It is believed that the German commanders expect their big guns to shake the moral of the opposing infantry, and thus win victories by terror rather than destruction. Against the British , such a result has not been achieved. The troops maintain their positions, says the headquarters report, with so enduring a courage that the chief result of the relentless bombard- ment has been a prodigious waste of ammuni- tion.</p>
        <p>THE ENCIRCLING MOVEMENT. On their left wing the French have advanced a short way between Lassigny and bye. Much further north a French detachment has occu- pied and holds Peronne. GERMANS WITH THEIR BACKS TO THE WALL.</p>
        <p>French official reports come to haml that the Germans, were fighting desperately with their backs to the wall to maintain a footing in France, though the distance between them and</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>,^r.r.:,..',4.2;•• 7-4;A</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>WEEK.</head>
        <p>WEEK.</p>
        <p>Paris, now approaching one hundred 'miles at the hearest point, was daily growing greater. Last Monday it was reported that the fighting had been less violent and that the Allies had made appreciable progress, particularly be- tween Reims and the Argonne; and Sir John French reported that several further counter- attacks had been successfully repulsed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>GERMAN COMMUNICATIONS THREATENED.</head>
        <p>GERMAN COMMUNICATIONS THREATENED.</p>
        <p>Rumours of a flanking movement from the direction of North-West France and Belgium gained in force. It was seen that to make frontal attacks upon enttenched positions would be only to play the game of the enemy, but signs to justify the rumours were slow to make their appearance. Rowever, by Wednes- day this week, after eleven days of pitched battle, it could be said with Confidence that a movement by the Allies in considerable force against the German line of communications through Belgium was in progress, and there was every reason to believe that the prolonged battle was turning with slow but sure decisive- ness in favour of the Allies. The bulletin issued in Paris on Tuesday afternoon was cautious in tone. "On the entire front," we were told, the Germans "manifested a certain activity without gaining any appreciable results." A telegram from Northern France reported considerable military activity in the Amiens region. German cavalry were pushing their reconnaissance far west of their supports, and behind Cambrai the enemy's infantry were gathering. Whether preparation was being made by the German leaders for an advance against the Allied left, or whether they feared themselves to be made the object of a flanking movement and were preparing to guard against it, could not be told from the scanty informa- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>CROWNING GERMAN INFAMY.</head>
        <p>CROWNING GERMAN INFAMY.</p>
        <p>Reims Cathedral in Ruins.</p>
        <p>An outstanding feature of the Battle of the Aisne has been the destruction of Reims Cathe- dral by the German gunners, presumably as the result of having been forced to retreat be- yond that town. The destruction of this ancient and historic building, which was being used as a hospital at the time, has aroused horror throughout the avilised world. All that remained of the cathedral was four charred walls and the tower. Hundreds of non-combatants were killed and hundreds of houses destroyed in the town, and the popula- tion is suffering intense misery. There was no military reason for the bom- bardment of Reims. The French Foreign Office has sent a protest to the neutral Powers. It says that the bombardment was undertaken "solely for the pleasure of destruction," and that a revolting act of vandalism has handed over to the flames a sanctuary of French his- tory and robbed humanity of an incomparable possession. The famous tapestry in the cathe- dral was removed before the bombardment. The Pope is reported to have expressed in- credulity at the news, and to have said that it was hardly possible that we had been thus plunged back to the time of the Attila, the Scourge of God. ARCHBISHOP'S LAMENTATION.</p>
        <p>The "Echo de Paris" publishes an interview with Cardinal Lucon, Archbishop of Reims. He said:— _ _ • . .</p>
        <p>"I already knew that the church of Saint- Remi had suffered badly, but I had hoped that my old age might have been spared the destruc- tion of the cathedral, the cradle of Christian Prance, linked with our history by so many memories. What terrible punishments God must be reserving for this German nation, which commits so many acts of sacrilege! It is more than ever necessary that I should return without further delay to Reims, that I may soothe the wounds of my poor diocesans, and, on the ruins of our city, call upon the justice of an avenging God."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>RAMSHACKLE EMPIRE.</head>
        <p>RAMSHACKLE EMPIRE.</p>
        <p>STILL CRUMBLING BEFORE RUSSIA</p>
        <p>AND SERVIA.</p>
        <p>Austria is still struggling on against the enemies oppoSed to her, apparently without a gleam of hope. Last week-end the Russians occu- pied Sandomir, effected another crossing of the San, and put the Austrians to flight. Many guns and 5,000 prisoners were taken in the region of Jaworow. It was reported from Petrograd that the Russians had seized the fortified positions of Siniava and Sambor. The Austrian rearguards had been hurled back from the River fishnia beyond the River San. In the region of Radimno and Medika the enemy have destroyed the bridges over the San. On September 15th, in the region of Sandomir and Radomysl, over 3,000 prisoners and 10 guns were taken, and many waggons with artillery supplies were captured at Nemirov and in the neighbourhood. The Austrian rearguard, in trying to check our advance, says an official Russian com- munique, in the region of Baranoff and Rant- choff, north-west of Jaroslav, was repulsed with great loss. Our heavy artillery is bombarding the fortifications of Jaroslay. The garrison of Przemysl is engaged in the combat, and the guns of the fortress have opened fire. During the passage of our troops through the forests we found some Austrian patteries which had been abandoned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>FALL OF JAROSLAV.</head>
        <p>FALL OF JAROSLAV.</p>
        <p>From the Austrian frontier comes the import- ant news that the fortress of Jaroslav has fallen to the Russian assault. The Russian flag floats over the fortress. This fortress is of great strategic importance. It gives command of the direct railway com- munication between Lwow (Lemberg) and Cracow, besides helping Russian forces to sur- round the derelict Austrian armies that have taken refuge in. Przemysl.</p>
        <p>THE LITTLE NATIONS' CONTRIBUTIONS.</p>
        <p>An official message from Cettigne announces that the Montenegrin Army has arrived before Serajevo, repulsing the Austrians in great dis- order. The Serbians, marching in the same direc- tion, have effected a junction with the Montene- grins, and a battle has begun. The Serb Press Bureau at Nish has issued the following communication regarding the position , on September 21 On the fronts Ljubovje-Zvornik-Leshnitsa and the Mitrovitza-Shabatz fierce fighting took place on the 21st, with a satisfactory result for our troops.</p>
        <p>- On the front Loznitsa-Rateha our troops on the night of September 20-21 repulsed some at- tacks by the enemy, on. whom they inflicted serious losses.</p>
        <p>On the Save front the enemy attempted to seize the island of Podgaratz and the positions to the east of Debretz on the Save and Main Ada near Belgrade, but were repulsed at all these points. On the Danube front the enemy attempted to cross the river near Skela ,below Smederevo with the aid of boats. Our troops, however, captured all the boats and completely routed the enemy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>GERMANS CHECKED.</head>
        <p>GERMANS CHECKED.</p>
        <p>--•••••—•-- --------- The German success in East Prussia has bec-, short-lived. General Rennenkampf, who wa: forced to retreat before the German advance, has been reinforced by other Russian troops and is again moving upon the frontier of Germany. Meanwhile Russia presses her advance against Austria, determined to wring all possible ad- vantage from her victories.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>BRITISH CRUISERS LOST.</head>
        <p>BRITISH CRUISERS LOST.</p>
        <p>THREE TORPEDOED WITH GREAT LOSS OF LIFE.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday the Secretary of the Admir- alty communicated the following statement for publication:— KM. Ships Aboukir (Captain John E. Drum- mond), Hogue (Captain Wilmot S. Nicholson), and Cressy (Captain Robert W. Johnson) have been sunk by submarines in the North Sea. , The Aboukir was torpedoed, and whilst the ' Hogue and the Cressy had closed and were standing by to save the crew, they were also torpedoed. A considerable number were saved by H.M.S. Lowestoft (Captain Theobald W. B. Kennedy), and by a division of destroyers, trawlers and boats.</p>
        <p>Lists of casualties will be published as soon as they. are known. _</p>
        <p>The Aboukir, Hogue, and Cressy, sister ships of about 12,000 tons displacement, each carried a complement of about 700. It is not clear how many German submarines attacked, but a Berlin report, coming through Amsterdam, says only the Ti 9 was engaged, and she escaped undamaged. There is every reason to`fear that two-thirds of the crews lost their lives. At Harwich, Lowestoft, and in Holland between 700 and 800 men have been landed. Unhappily, almost all the others, thirteen hundred out of 2,100, are dead. The survivors landed in Holland have been sent to a camp in Friesland, where active belligerents who cross the Dutch border are being detained for the term of the war.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>H.M.S. PEGASUS DISABLED. Useful Career Ended.</head>
        <p>H.M.S. PEGASUS DISABLED. Useful Career Ended.</p>
        <p>Since the outbreak of war H.M.S. Pegasus, Commander John A. Inglis, Royal Navy, has been wOrking from Zanzibar and has rendered very useful services, including the destruction of Dar-es-Salaam and the sinking of the Ger- man gunboat Mowe and floating dock. Early on Sunday. morning, however, she was at- tacked by the Konigsberg while at anchor in Zanzibar harbour cleaning her boilers and re- pairing machinery. The Pegasus was thus taken at a disadvantage and, being somewhat outranged by the newer 4in. guns of the Konigs- berg, was completely disabled after suffering a loss unofficially reported as 25 killed and 80 wounded. This is a high proportion out of a crew of 231. The damage done to the Konigs- berg is not known; she was last seen steaming away to the southward.</p>
        <p>The Konigsberg approached at, full speed at five a.m. on Sunday, and openea fire on the Pegasus. Her shooting, which was very accu- rate, began at a range of 9,000 yards, closing to 7,000. All the broadside of the Pegasus was engaged, but her guns were disabled in 15 minutes, when her fire ceased. After a lull of five minutes the Konigsberg again opened fire for another quarter of an hour, the Pegasus being unable to reply. The British flag was shot away twice, but was held up by hand by Marines.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>GERMAN CRUISER'S SUCCESS.</head>
        <p>GERMAN CRUISER'S SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>Destruction of Ships in Bay of Bengal.</p>
        <p>On September 10 the German cruiser Emden, from the China Station, after being completely lost for six weeks, appeared suddenly in the Bay of Bengal, and during the period September 10 to 14 captured six British ships as follows: Indus, Lovat, Killin, Diplomat, Trabbock and Kabanga, of which five were sunk and the sixth sent into Calcutta with the crews. The Emden is now reported at Rangoon, and it is possible that she has made some other captures.</p>
        <p>Apparently Th-e---E-m-den's Marksmanship against her prizes when she was destroying them was poor, confirming thereby the belief, founded on earlier events, that the German navy does not shoot well. On the Emden both officers and men treated their prisoners with every consid- eration and courtesy. The Emden's action has resulted in a direct loss to British owners and underwriters of three-quarters of a million sterling. The Kil- lin's cargo was valued at one lac of rupees, that of the Diplomat at .C 350,000 and the five hulls collectively were valued at &lt;C400,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>EXCITING DUEL BETWEEN ARMED CRUISERS.</head>
        <p>EXCITING DUEL BETWEEN ARMED CRUISERS.</p>
        <p>Carmania's Brilliant Achievement. The British auxiliary cruiser Carmania, Cap- tain Noel Grant, Royal Navy, went into action on September 14 off the east coast of South America with n German armed merchant cruiser supposed to be the Cap Trafalgar or Berlin, mounting eight 4in. guns and pom- poms. The action lasted one hour and 45 mtn- utes, when the German ship capsized and sank, her survivors being rescued by an empty col- lier. Of the crew of the Carmania nine men were killed, five men seriously injured, and 21 men slightly wounded. None of the officers was injured. The First Lord has sent the following telegram to Captain Noel Grant, Royal Navy, of the Carmania:—</p>
        <p>"Well done:— You have fought a fine action to a successful finish."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>• STEAMBOATS v. WARSHIP. Remarkable Scenes on the Cameroon</head>
        <p>• STEAMBOATS v. WARSHIP. Remarkable Scenes on the Cameroon</p>
        <p>River.</p>
        <p>H.M.S. Cumberland, Captain Cyril T. M. Fuller, Royal Navy, reports from the Cameroon River that a German steamboat, on the night of September 14th, attempted to sink H.M. gunboat Dwarf, Commander Frederick E. K. Strong, Royal Navy, with an infernal machine in the bows. The attempt failed, and the steamboat, with one prisoner, was ciiptured. On the night of September 16th the Dwarf was Purposely rammed by the Nachtigall, a German merchant ship. The Dwarf was slightly damaged, but sustained no ,casualties. The Nachtigall was wrecked. The enemy lost four white men and 10 coloured men, and eight white men and 14 coloured men missing. A further report received from H.M.S. Cum- berland states that two German launches, one carrying explosive machines, were destroyed, the enemy's losses being one white man killed and three white men and two natives taken prisoners.</p>
        <p>AUSTRALIAN SUBMARINE LOST.</p>
        <p>The Admiralty announce that Submarine A E 1, of the Australian Navy, has been lost in an accident off her home coast. Between 30 and 40 officers and men are missing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>ANOTHER ARMED CRUISER CAPTURED.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER ARMED CRUISER CAPTURED.</p>
        <p>German Colliers Taken.</p>
        <p>The Secretary of the Admiralty reports that H.M.S. Berwick (Captain Lewis C. Baker), oper- ating in North Atlantic waters,captured the Ger- man Hamburg-American liner Spreewald. This vessel was known to be fitted as an armed merchant cruiser. At the same time two col- liprs were captured with coal for the German ci.nisers operating in Atlantic waters. The vessels had between them 6,000 tons of coal and 180 tons of provisions. OUR PROFIT AND LOSS ACCOUNT.</p>
        <p>Balance Against Germany. The total number of German vessels which, according to the latest reports, have been cap- tured by British vessels at sea, or by British port authorities, now amounts to 92. In addi- tion to this, 95 German vessels were detained in British ports on the outbreak of war, making a total of 187 German vessels in our possession at present. A6+, against this, 70 British vessels were de- tained in German ports on the outbreak of war. Since then 12 British seagoing vessels have beeri captured and sunk at sea, out of upwards of 4,000 British vessels carrying on • oversea trade.</p>
        <p>The change in the German command may be due to that affairs, or possibly to the loss of the Magdeburg, which grounded during an engage- ment with, the Russians. Admiral von Coerper is a sailor of high reputation. As naval attache in London some Years ago he was a keen observer of the British service. Admiral von Ingenohl is the Commander-in-Chief of the Kaiser's fleet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>MADRAS BOMBARDED.</head>
        <p>MADRAS BOMBARDED.</p>
        <p>The German cruiser Emden appeared off Madras on Tuesday evening and began firing on the oil tanks. Two tanks were set on fire and one or two buildings were hit, while two Indians and a boy were killed. When the guns of the forts replied the Emden extin- guished her lights and steamed away. The whole affair lasted only about 15 minutes. British, vessels in eastern waters are search- ing for the Emden and also the Konigsberg. ! The wide spaces of the oceans are excellent hiding-places for vessels which avoid the most frequented tracks. '</p>
        <p>IN ENEMY'S CAMP.</p>
        <p>TWO CAN PLAY AT BOMB- DROPPING.</p>
        <p>We have this week read the Germans a lesson which may make them pause before they com- mit any further bomb outrages. On Wednes- day the Press Bureau communicated a state- ment from the Secretary of the Admiralty to the effect that British aeroplanes of the Naval Wing had delivered an attack on the Zeppelin sheds at Dusseldorf, on the Rhine, on Tuesday. Their pilots, though hampered by the misty weather, which made the conditions very diffi- cult, succeeded in reaching the vulnerable spot. Flight-Lieutenant Collet dropped three bombs on a Zeppelin shed, approaching within 400 ft. On the return his machine was struck by one projectile. All, however, returned safely to their. point. of departure. . .</p>
        <p>This daring raid shows, as the Admiralty communication points out, that if more bombs are dropped into Antwerp or other . Belgian towns, "measures of reprisal can certainly be adopted, if desired, to almost any extent." Essen, with its immense armament works, and Cologne are not far from Dusseldorf, and are equally open to attack.</p>
        <p>SOUTH AFRICA.</p>
        <p>GENERAL BOTHA TO LEAD THE ARMY.</p>
        <p>General Botha's decision to take supreme command in the field of the operations against German South-west Africa has created an ex- cellent impression. He will retain office as Prime Minister. The Union Government has called for 7,000 then for service in South Africa. These are in addition to the troops already chosen.</p>
        <p>ROLL OF HONOUR.</p>
        <p>SOUTH LANCASHIRE REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>OFFICER DIED IN HOSPITAL. Hadfield, Lieutenant W. J. M. OFFICERS UNOFFICIALLY REPORTED</p>
        <p>KILLED.</p>
        <p>Berry, Second Lieut. F. (previously reported missing). Widdowson, Second Lieutenant A. I. (pre- viously reported missing). OFFICER UNOFFICIALLY REPORTED WOUNDED. Adams, G. (previously reported missing). OFFICER UNOFFICIALLY REPORTED WOUNDED AND PRISONER.</p>
        <p>Irwin, Second Lieutenant H. G. W. (pre- viously reported missing). REPORTED TO BE PRISONER. Stretton, Lieutenant A. L. de C.</p>
        <p>ADMITTED TO HOSPITAL. Denmark Hill, London. Jones, 7583 Private W. Roden, 7713 Private C. KING'S LIVERPOOL REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>OFFICERS WOUNDED. Feneran, Captain F. E. Tanner, Captain H. E. Caty, 8811 Private C. CHESHIRE REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>KILLED IN ACTION.</p>
        <p>Jones, Captain E. R. (previously reported missing). Roberts, 9993 Corporal J. W. •</p>
        <p>OFFICERS WOUNDED AND MISSING. Holton, Second Lieutenant R. H. (previously reported missing). Stewart, Second Lieutenant W. L. (previously reported missing). Tahourdin, Captain V. R. (previously re- ported missing).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>OFFICER REJOINED.</head>
        <p>OFFICER REJOINED.</p>
        <p>Frost, LieUien;;;;.P. L. (previously reported missing). OFFICER PRISONER. Mrs.. Cuthbert Mattersom of Chester, has re- ceived a letter from her husband, Lieutenant C. A. K. Matterson, of the Cheshire Regiment, dated August 27th, saying that he had been captured and is well. He had previously been reported missing. ADMITTED TO HOSPITAL. King Edward's. Neu-on, Lieutenant N. A.</p>
        <p>Brighton. Davies, 9872 Private G. WOUNDED. Atkinson, 8081 Private G., shrapnel wound, elbow. Field, 8479 Private S., wounded in mouth. Griffiths, 8020 Private R., severely wounded. Hall, 7871 Private J., wounded right hand. Hogan, 9242 Sergeant W. E., wound left fore- arm. Parry, 9211, Sergeant D. J., wound left fore- arm. Stagg, 9153 Private 0. E., wound right fore- arm. Tomkinson, 9178 Sergeant E., scalp wound. Wood, 10044 Private A., wounded both hands. Note.—The widows of warrant officers, non- commissioned officers, and men should imme- diately request the local agencies, through which during the war they have been receiving assistance, to notify the Secretary, Royal Patriotic Fund, 17, Waterloo-place, London, from whom temporary relief may be obtained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-09-26_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>AN UNSCRUPULOUS ENEMY.</head>
        <p>AN UNSCRUPULOUS ENEMY.</p>
        <p>Hesitating, Soldierly Condemnation.</p>
        <p>In the despatch sent under the direction of Sir John Prem..ll this week from the seat of war the following passage occurs : The Germans are a formidable enemy. Well trained, long prepared, and brave, their soldiers are carrying on the contest with skill and valour. Nevertheless they are fighting to win anyhow, regardless of all the rules of fair play, and there is evidence that they do not hesitate at anythiig in order to gain victory. A large number of the tales' of their misbe- haviour are exaggerations, and some of the stringent precautions they have taken to guard themselves against the inhabitants of the areas traversed are possibly justifiable measures of war.</p>
        <p>But at the same time it has been definitely established that they have committed atrocities on many occasions and they have been guilty of brutal conduct. TREACHERY PUNISHED. Amongst the minor happenings of interest is the following • . During a counter-attack by the German 53rd Regiment on portions of the Northampton and Queen's Regiments on Thursday, the 17th. a force of some 400 of the enemy were allowed to approach right up to the trench, occupied by a platoon of the former regiment, owing to the ' fact that they had held up their hands and made gestures that were interpreted as signs that they I wished to surrender. When they were actually' Oil the parapet of the trench held by the North- amptons they opened fire on our men at point blank range. Unluckily for the enemy, however, flanking them and only some 400 yards away there hap- pened to be a machine-gun manned by a detach- ment of the "Queen's." This at once opened fire, cutting a lane through their mass, and they fell back to their own trench with great loss. Shortly afterwards they Were driven fur-; ther hack with additional loss by a battalion of the Guards ;which came Up in lufvort.</p>
        <p>PRISONERS AS SHIELDS. It is officially reported that Germans have de- scended to such tactics as the misuse of the white flag and the driving of prisoners before them to corer their attack.</p>
