        <p>Recruiting for the Army Service Corps. large number of men are now required to be enlisted for the Horse Transport branch, and for the Supply branch of the Army Service Corps. For the former branch drivers are particularly required, but also wheelers, saddlers and shoeing-smiths ; in Vie Supply branch only bakers and butchers. Owing to the small personnel available for training these men, it is particularly necessary that only thoroughly qualified tradesmen of the trades open to recruiting, or men who can take charge of a pair of horses and also drive them should be accepted ; in order to ensure this, recruiting officers will obtain, either from a responsible firm or from a man of standing in the county, a certificate to the effect that the candidate is thoroughly efficient in the duties he will have to perform. In addition, it should be explained to the recruit that, owing to the lack of accommodation, the following procedure will be necessary : Every man will be put through a short test on joining and is liable to be discharged at once if not considered likely to become an efficient soldier. If he is found to be proficient, he will immediately be sent on furlough and will be called up for service when required, if possible 10 days' notice being given Men who are not proficient, if not dis- charged at once, will either be sent on furlough awaiting training," and will be called up later to undergo the necessary training, or will be then and there put through a short course of training until they are proficient and will then be sent on furlough. During the time that these men are on furlough they will be paid at the rate of 3s. a day. APPLY CAIRO. ST. SCHOOLS, WARRINGTON.</p>
